00:07:37.580 --> 00:07:39.300
Okay, let's talk about sugar,

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:41.630
let's talk about how
sugar impacts your brain,

00:07:41.630 --> 00:07:44.800
and how your brain impacts
your pursuit or your avoidance

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:46.310
of sugar.

00:07:46.310 --> 00:07:48.440
Let's get a few things
out of the way first.

00:07:48.440 --> 00:07:50.150
The first thing is that there's nothing

00:07:50.150 --> 00:07:52.410
inherently bad about sugar.

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:55.400
I know the word sugar
gets a bad rap nowadays,

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:57.680
and indeed, you're going to
hear over and over again,

00:07:57.680 --> 00:07:58.870
during this podcast,

00:07:58.870 --> 00:08:01.340
that consuming a lot of refined sugars,

00:08:01.340 --> 00:08:03.680
in particular high fructose corn syrup,

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:07.410
is known to have a very
large number of bad effects

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:08.330
on the brain and body.

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:09.490
I don't know that there's anyone

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:11.710
that really debates that anymore.

00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:15.470
Even if we just agree, and
I think we should all agree,

00:08:15.470 --> 00:08:18.810
on the so-called calories in,
calories out principle, right?

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:20.380
It's a principle thermodynamics

00:08:20.380 --> 00:08:23.440
that if we ingest more
energy than we burn,

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:24.560
we are going to gain weight.

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:27.430
If we ingest less energy than we burn,

00:08:27.430 --> 00:08:29.170
we are generally going to lose weight.

00:08:29.170 --> 00:08:31.400
And if the two things are in balance,

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:34.380
ingestion and burning of energy,

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:36.610
well, then we're going to maintain weight.

00:08:36.610 --> 00:08:39.160
So everyone agrees on
that, I agree on that.

00:08:39.160 --> 00:08:41.670
But beyond, there are a number of ways

00:08:41.670 --> 00:08:43.480
in which particular nutrients,

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:45.820
in the case of today's episode, sugar,

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:48.460
impact the way that the brain works,

00:08:48.460 --> 00:08:52.410
such that we tend to seek out
more of particular nutrients.

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:54.740
For instance, if we eat sugar,

00:08:54.740 --> 00:08:57.420
there are two, or at least two mechanisms,

00:08:57.420 --> 00:08:59.530
by which we will crave more sugar.

00:08:59.530 --> 00:09:01.180
I think most people are
aware of that experience,

00:09:01.180 --> 00:09:03.690
but today I'm going to explain
exactly how that works.

00:09:03.690 --> 00:09:06.600
But also that when we ingest sugar

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:08.250
it has a bunch of different effects

00:09:08.250 --> 00:09:10.970
on the way that our neural circuits work

00:09:10.970 --> 00:09:13.580
that can allow us to be
more or less focused,

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:16.320
more or less agitated,
a more or less happy,

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:19.770
more or less depressed in some cases.

00:09:19.770 --> 00:09:21.937
So today, as we explore this
thing we're calling sugar,

00:09:21.937 --> 00:09:24.680
we are going to explore
that mainly in the context

00:09:24.680 --> 00:09:26.060
of the nervous system,

00:09:26.060 --> 00:09:27.720
but also in the context of how

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:29.110
the nervous system regulates

00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:31.290
many, many functions and behaviors

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:33.330
that are important to all of you,

00:09:33.330 --> 00:09:35.530
your ability to think,
your ability to extra size,

00:09:35.530 --> 00:09:37.340
your ability to gain weight, lose weight,

00:09:37.340 --> 00:09:39.660
whatever your goals might happen to be,

00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:42.850
sugar plays a critical role
in achieving those goals.

00:09:42.850 --> 00:09:44.200
And in some cases,

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:46.160
if you're ingesting too
much at the wrong times

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:49.620
or the wrong forms, sugar can
actually impede those goals.

00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:52.620
In fact, sugar can prevent
all the right behaviors

00:09:52.620 --> 00:09:55.470
from allowing you to achieve
the goals that you want.

00:09:55.470 --> 00:09:57.000
So today we are going to place sugar

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:59.070
into its proper context.

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:01.130
The way I want to start off by doing that

00:10:01.130 --> 00:10:04.260
is to tell you a little bit
of what happens when we eat

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:06.840
and a little bit of what the brain does

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:09.490
to respond to those events.

00:10:09.490 --> 00:10:10.900
So what happens when we eat?

00:10:10.900 --> 00:10:14.520
Well, I've done an entire
episode on metabolism.

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:17.720
So if you're interested
in the full cascade

00:10:17.720 --> 00:10:20.860
of hormonal and neural events
that occurs when we eat,

00:10:20.860 --> 00:10:22.410
please check out that episode.

00:10:22.410 --> 00:10:24.050
But for the sake of today's discussion,

00:10:24.050 --> 00:10:26.940
let's just take a, what
I call top contour view

00:10:26.940 --> 00:10:30.200
of the hormonal response
to ingesting food.

00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:31.760
Now, anytime we eat,

00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:33.510
that is the consequence
of a number of things

00:10:33.510 --> 00:10:36.010
that happened before we ate.

00:10:36.010 --> 00:10:38.530
There's a own in our
brain body called Ghrelin,

00:10:38.530 --> 00:10:40.990
spelled G-H-R-E-L-I-N.

00:10:40.990 --> 00:10:44.090
Ghrelin is a hormone that increases

00:10:44.090 --> 00:10:46.890
depending on how long it's
been since we ate last.

00:10:46.890 --> 00:10:49.750
So the longer it's been
since we had a meal,

00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:52.680
Ghrelin levels are going to be
higher and higher and higher,

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:54.360
and it essentially makes us hungry

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:56.810
by interacting with particular neurons

00:10:56.810 --> 00:10:57.927
in an area of the brain called

00:10:57.927 --> 00:11:00.420
the arcuate nucleus of the hypothalamus

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:03.800
and some other areas as well,
like the lateral hypothalamus.

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:05.430
You don't need to know the
names of those brain areas,

00:11:05.430 --> 00:11:08.390
but if you'd like to
know them, there they are

00:11:08.390 --> 00:11:11.580
Ghrelin increases, it
tends to make us hungry.

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:13.500
And then when we eat,

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:16.510
typically what happens is
Ghrelin levels go down.

00:11:16.510 --> 00:11:18.260
So it's a very logical system.

00:11:18.260 --> 00:11:22.210
Now, when we eat, assuming
that we eat carbohydrates,

00:11:22.210 --> 00:11:25.000
but even if we just eat
some protein and some fats,

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:28.500
we will experience a
slight, or in some cases,

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:31.060
a large rise in blood glucose,

00:11:31.060 --> 00:11:33.700
blood glucose is simply blood sugar.

00:11:33.700 --> 00:11:37.710
And the body and brain, we
should say in particular,

00:11:37.710 --> 00:11:40.060
the nervous system doesn't function well

00:11:40.060 --> 00:11:42.310
if blood sugar is too high or too low.

00:11:42.310 --> 00:11:44.370
So as a consequence, we
have another hormone,

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:45.690
which is released from the pancreas,

00:11:45.690 --> 00:11:46.840
which is called insulin,

00:11:46.840 --> 00:11:49.670
which helps regulate the amount of glucose

00:11:49.670 --> 00:11:50.630
in the bloodstream.

00:11:50.630 --> 00:11:53.890
So even if you were to
ingest an entire cup,

00:11:53.890 --> 00:11:56.090
an eight ounce cup of pure table sugar,

00:11:56.090 --> 00:11:58.690
which would send your blood
glucose very, very high,

00:11:58.690 --> 00:12:00.540
assuming that you have a
normal insulin response,

00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:02.350
that you're not diabetic,

00:12:02.350 --> 00:12:04.520
that insulin response would help clamp

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:07.480
that blood glucose level
so that it did not cause

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:09.760
damage to your brain and body.

00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:11.330
Because if blood sugar goes too high,

00:12:11.330 --> 00:12:14.150
it's actually toxic to neurons
and other cells of your body,

00:12:14.150 --> 00:12:15.160
it can kill them off.

00:12:15.160 --> 00:12:17.320
And neurons of the central nervous system,

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:18.450
meaning the brain and spinal cord,

00:12:18.450 --> 00:12:20.350
once they are dead, they do not come back.

00:12:20.350 --> 00:12:23.580
So your biological systems understand this

00:12:23.580 --> 00:12:25.290
at a biological level that is,

00:12:25.290 --> 00:12:28.630
and prevent that death of
cells due to high blood sugar,

00:12:28.630 --> 00:12:31.380
by keeping insulin around,

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:33.540
in order to clamp blood glucose.

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:35.710
Diabetics, we call them
type one diabetics,

00:12:35.710 --> 00:12:36.860
who don't make insulin,

00:12:36.860 --> 00:12:38.980
have to take insulin when
they eat in particular,

00:12:38.980 --> 00:12:40.980
when they eat foods that
raise their blood sugar,

00:12:40.980 --> 00:12:42.777
specifically to avoid that neurotoxicity

00:12:42.777 --> 00:12:45.660
and the other deleterious
effects of high blood sugar.

00:12:45.660 --> 00:12:47.380
So Ghrelin is a hormone that goes up

00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:49.110
the longer it's been since we've eaten,

00:12:49.110 --> 00:12:51.250
it tends to stimulate hunger.

00:12:51.250 --> 00:12:53.680
When we eat, Ghrelin is suppressed.

00:12:53.680 --> 00:12:55.540
Blood glucose typically goes up,

00:12:55.540 --> 00:12:59.290
especially when we eat a
carbohydrate containing meal.

00:12:59.290 --> 00:13:02.470
When blood glucose goes up,
it's regulated in the body,

00:13:02.470 --> 00:13:04.770
meaning its peaks and its valleys

00:13:04.770 --> 00:13:06.650
are more or less smoothed out,

00:13:06.650 --> 00:13:08.430
and that glucose is sequestered,

00:13:08.430 --> 00:13:11.020
it's taken away where it
needs to be taken away.

00:13:11.020 --> 00:13:13.390
And in certain locations,
it's delivered to cells

00:13:13.390 --> 00:13:15.990
so that those cells can use the glucose.

00:13:15.990 --> 00:13:19.240
Now, one of the chief organs
for glucose utilization

00:13:19.240 --> 00:13:20.620
is the brain,

00:13:20.620 --> 00:13:23.790
neurons are tremendously
metabolically active.

00:13:23.790 --> 00:13:27.440
And their preferred mode of metabolism

00:13:27.440 --> 00:13:28.850
is glucose metabolism.

00:13:28.850 --> 00:13:32.050
In other words, neurons
basically run on sugar,

00:13:32.050 --> 00:13:34.670
which is not to say that you
should eat a lot of sugar.

00:13:34.670 --> 00:13:37.840
As you'll see today, there
are states of mind and body,

00:13:37.840 --> 00:13:39.540
for instance, fasted states,

00:13:39.540 --> 00:13:42.580
in which people report
having immense amounts

00:13:42.580 --> 00:13:44.950
of mental clarity and their blood glucose

00:13:44.950 --> 00:13:46.370
is actually quite low.

00:13:46.370 --> 00:13:49.000
So it is simply not the
case that the more sugar

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:51.730
that you ingest, the better
that your brain will function.

00:13:51.730 --> 00:13:53.960
But it is the case that for most people,

00:13:53.960 --> 00:13:55.750
meaning people who are not on a ketogenic

00:13:55.750 --> 00:13:57.350
or very low carbohydrate diet,

00:13:57.350 --> 00:13:59.500
they're not adapted to
low carbohydrate diets,

00:13:59.500 --> 00:14:02.100
that neurons in their brain and body

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:04.100
are using glucose in order to function.

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:05.340
That's what allows those neurons

00:14:05.340 --> 00:14:07.770
to fire electrical potentials,
that's how we refer to it,

00:14:07.770 --> 00:14:11.100
firing, meaning sending electrical
signals down their length

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:13.260
to communicate with other neurons.

00:14:13.260 --> 00:14:15.490
To illustrate just how
important glucose is

00:14:15.490 --> 00:14:16.940
for brain function,

00:14:16.940 --> 00:14:20.360
I'd like to describe a study
that just recently came out,

00:14:20.360 --> 00:14:23.140
that sits on a long
history of similar studies.

00:14:23.140 --> 00:14:25.600
But the one that just came out
is particularly interesting.

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:30.020
Now I want to point out
that, unless I say otherwise,

00:14:30.020 --> 00:14:32.820
I'm going to refer to typical diets,

00:14:32.820 --> 00:14:35.680
meaning I have to believe
that most people out there

00:14:35.680 --> 00:14:37.990
are in ingesting some starch
some starch or carbohydrate.

00:14:37.990 --> 00:14:39.590
I do realize there are people following

00:14:39.590 --> 00:14:41.160
very low carbohydrate diets

00:14:41.160 --> 00:14:42.690
or moderately carbohydrate diets.

00:14:42.690 --> 00:14:43.970
I even know that there's
some folks out there

00:14:43.970 --> 00:14:45.620
who are on the so-called carnivore diet,

00:14:45.620 --> 00:14:48.600
they only eat meat and
organs, maybe a little fruit.

00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:50.700
But I'm going to assume
that the vast majority

00:14:50.700 --> 00:14:55.130
of people listening ingest
proteins and carbohydrates.

00:14:55.130 --> 00:14:58.920
So unless I say ketogenic, or
I emphasize ketosis itself,

00:14:58.920 --> 00:14:59.890
which I will,

00:14:59.890 --> 00:15:01.450
I'm referring to a kind of typical diet

00:15:01.450 --> 00:15:04.560
where people are consuming fats,
proteins and carbohydrates.

00:15:04.560 --> 00:15:07.720
I count myself as one such individual.

00:15:07.720 --> 00:15:10.040
At some point I might try the
carnivore diet, who knows,

00:15:10.040 --> 00:15:12.720
I might I a pure vegan diet, who knows.

00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:15.760
But for my entire life up
until now, I'm 46 years old,

00:15:15.760 --> 00:15:17.670
I've been a proud omnivore,

00:15:17.670 --> 00:15:21.020
meaning I've tried to eat
high quality as much as I can,

00:15:21.020 --> 00:15:22.080
unprocessed foods,

00:15:22.080 --> 00:15:23.960
I try and really avoid
highly processed foods.

00:15:23.960 --> 00:15:26.240
But I do eat from those
three macronutrient groups,

00:15:26.240 --> 00:15:28.190
proteins, carbohydrates, and fats,

00:15:28.190 --> 00:15:30.860
and I'm going to assume
that most of you do as well.

00:15:30.860 --> 00:15:32.460
The study I'd like to emphasize,

00:15:33.890 --> 00:15:36.120
recorded from neurons, nerve cells,

00:15:36.120 --> 00:15:39.297
in the brain, in particular
in the part of the brain

00:15:39.297 --> 00:15:41.700
`that responds to visual images,

00:15:41.700 --> 00:15:43.220
the so called visual cortex.

00:15:43.220 --> 00:15:47.340
And neurons in the visual
cortex are beautifully tuned,

00:15:47.340 --> 00:15:50.993
as we say, to particular
features of what we see.

00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:52.883
The primary example of this,

00:15:52.883 --> 00:15:55.060
the kind of classic example,

00:15:55.060 --> 00:15:57.620
is if you put a little
electrode next to a neuron

00:15:57.620 --> 00:15:59.260
in your visual cortex,

00:15:59.260 --> 00:16:03.080
or if we put you into
an FMRI scanner machine,

00:16:03.080 --> 00:16:04.590
which can detect neural activity,

00:16:04.590 --> 00:16:08.930
and I were to show you a
bunch of just little lines,

00:16:08.930 --> 00:16:10.810
bars of light, they could
be dark bars of light,

00:16:10.810 --> 00:16:13.470
they could be light bars of light,

00:16:13.470 --> 00:16:14.690
on a screen in front of you.

00:16:14.690 --> 00:16:16.640
So some would be vertical,
some would be horizontal,

00:16:16.640 --> 00:16:18.410
some would be at 45 degrees,

00:16:18.410 --> 00:16:23.170
what we would see is that
some neurons respond best,

00:16:23.170 --> 00:16:25.800
meaning they fire a lot
of electrical activity

00:16:25.800 --> 00:16:26.740
to vertical lines,

00:16:26.740 --> 00:16:28.790
other ones respond to horizontal lines,

00:16:28.790 --> 00:16:31.670
and others respond to 45 degree lines.

00:16:31.670 --> 00:16:34.270
And this so called orientation tuning,

00:16:34.270 --> 00:16:36.100
meaning because of the
orientation of the line,

00:16:36.100 --> 00:16:39.320
is a cardinal classic feature of the way

00:16:39.320 --> 00:16:41.180
that your visual system is built.

00:16:41.180 --> 00:16:43.590
And everything that you see,

00:16:43.590 --> 00:16:47.010
whether it's a face or a
dog or a cat or a landscape,

00:16:47.010 --> 00:16:51.210
is built up from these very
simple neuron responses.

00:16:51.210 --> 00:16:53.620
In other words, when you look at a face,

00:16:53.620 --> 00:16:56.170
there are neurons deep in the
brain that respond to faces.

00:16:56.170 --> 00:16:57.950
But the only reason that those neurons

00:16:57.950 --> 00:16:59.240
can respond to those faces

00:16:59.240 --> 00:17:01.210
is because they receive
signals from neurons

00:17:01.210 --> 00:17:02.850
in your visual cortex.

00:17:02.850 --> 00:17:04.500
Some of which respond to vertical lines,

00:17:04.500 --> 00:17:05.837
some of which respond to horizontal lines,

00:17:05.837 --> 00:17:08.260
and some which respond to 45 degree lines.

00:17:08.260 --> 00:17:10.060
And all of those are built up

00:17:10.060 --> 00:17:12.030
in what we call a
hierarchical representation,

00:17:12.030 --> 00:17:14.230
which just fancy language for
those are the building blocks

00:17:14.230 --> 00:17:16.710
by which you see a face
and you recognize a face.

00:17:16.710 --> 00:17:18.200
And it's really an amazing phenomenon,

00:17:18.200 --> 00:17:19.760
it happens very, very fast.

00:17:19.760 --> 00:17:21.460
You never notice that you're doing this,

00:17:21.460 --> 00:17:22.750
but everything is built up from these

00:17:22.750 --> 00:17:26.080
fundamental orientation-tuned neurons.

00:17:26.080 --> 00:17:30.120
Now orientation-tuned
neurons are so fundamental

00:17:30.120 --> 00:17:31.870
that they are the building blocks

00:17:31.870 --> 00:17:34.950
by which you make up all
other things that you see,

00:17:34.950 --> 00:17:35.783
it's the way you read,

00:17:35.783 --> 00:17:38.080
it's the way that you recognize
faces, as I mentioned,

00:17:38.080 --> 00:17:39.080
and everything else.

00:17:41.030 --> 00:17:43.200
Experimentally, it's quite straightforward

00:17:43.200 --> 00:17:47.230
to measure how sharply tuned
one of these neurons is.

00:17:47.230 --> 00:17:50.330
In other words, if I were
to show you a vertical line

00:17:50.330 --> 00:17:51.730
and find a neuron in your brain

00:17:51.730 --> 00:17:53.650
that responds to vertical lines,

00:17:53.650 --> 00:17:57.490
I could also ask whether
or not that neuron fires

00:17:57.490 --> 00:18:00.650
any electrical activity
in response to a line

00:18:00.650 --> 00:18:02.170
that's not quite vertical,

00:18:02.170 --> 00:18:05.210
maybe just 10 degrees off
vertical or 20 degrees

00:18:05.210 --> 00:18:06.110
or 30 degrees.

00:18:06.110 --> 00:18:08.670
And what I eventually would
find is that that neuron

00:18:08.670 --> 00:18:13.100
was orientation-tuned over a
particular range of angles.

00:18:13.100 --> 00:18:15.270
It's not only going to
respond to vertical lines,

00:18:15.270 --> 00:18:16.610
it's also going to respond to lines

00:18:16.610 --> 00:18:19.330
that are about 10 degrees
off vertical on either side,

00:18:19.330 --> 00:18:22.280
but probably not much more, maybe 20,

00:18:22.280 --> 00:18:25.060
but usually it's going to
be anywhere from vertical

00:18:25.060 --> 00:18:26.643
to just tilted slightly.

00:18:28.903 --> 00:18:30.940
In the recent experiment
that was published

00:18:30.940 --> 00:18:32.570
in the journal, "Neuron,"
Cell Press journal,

00:18:32.570 --> 00:18:33.920
excellent journal,

00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:36.200
the authors asked a really
interesting question,

00:18:36.200 --> 00:18:38.960
they asked whether or not
the sharpness of tuning,

00:18:38.960 --> 00:18:41.980
the precision of orientation
tuning of these neurons

00:18:41.980 --> 00:18:44.820
is dependent on blood glucose level.

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:47.130
So just to cut to the chase,
to give you the answer,

00:18:47.130 --> 00:18:51.250
what they found is that
when subjects are well fed,

00:18:51.250 --> 00:18:53.220
neurons that responded to vertical,

00:18:53.220 --> 00:18:55.070
responded very strongly to vertical,

00:18:55.070 --> 00:18:57.870
but not very much at all to other angles,

00:18:57.870 --> 00:18:59.700
of what we call stimuli,

00:18:59.700 --> 00:19:02.133
so lines that are 10
degrees or 20 degrees off.

00:19:03.140 --> 00:19:06.390
If they looked at neurons that
were primarily tuned, right,

00:19:06.390 --> 00:19:09.800
that preferred horizontal lines,
they found the same thing.

00:19:09.800 --> 00:19:11.420
So it wasn't something
unique to vertical lines.

00:19:11.420 --> 00:19:13.690
What they basically
found was the sharpness,

00:19:13.690 --> 00:19:16.550
the precision of tuning
of neurons in the brain,

00:19:16.550 --> 00:19:19.890
was best when subjects were fed.

00:19:19.890 --> 00:19:22.920
And conversely, when subjects were fasted,

00:19:22.920 --> 00:19:27.640
the orientation tuning of these
neurons became much broader.

00:19:27.640 --> 00:19:29.040
What it meant was that a neuron

00:19:29.040 --> 00:19:31.010
that normally would
only respond to vertical

00:19:31.010 --> 00:19:33.760
now responded to other
angles of lines as well.

00:19:33.760 --> 00:19:35.300
You might say, well, that's great, right,

00:19:35.300 --> 00:19:37.100
these neurons that at one
point could only do one thing

00:19:37.100 --> 00:19:38.160
are now tuned to other things.

00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:41.100
But it's not so great
because what that means

00:19:41.100 --> 00:19:43.620
is that in the fasted state,

00:19:43.620 --> 00:19:47.530
your perception of the outside
world is actually distorted,

00:19:47.530 --> 00:19:51.280
it's blurred, it's not as
precise as it is when you're fed.

00:19:51.280 --> 00:19:52.450
And when I say fed,

00:19:52.450 --> 00:19:55.550
what I really mean is when
glucose is available to neurons.

00:19:55.550 --> 00:19:58.430
Now, for some of you, maybe many of you,

00:19:58.430 --> 00:20:01.380
and including myself,
intermittent fasting,

00:20:01.380 --> 00:20:04.210
or some variant thereof, is
actually a state that I like.

00:20:04.210 --> 00:20:05.250
It allows me to focus.

00:20:05.250 --> 00:20:06.950
For instance, as I mentioned before,

00:20:06.950 --> 00:20:08.190
and even earlier in this podcast,

00:20:08.190 --> 00:20:11.700
I tend to eat my first meal
sometime around 11:00 AM.

00:20:11.700 --> 00:20:13.820
And then I generally eat my last meal

00:20:13.820 --> 00:20:16.000
sometime around 8:00 PM,
plus or minus an hour

00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:17.610
on either side, I'm not
super strict about it.

00:20:17.610 --> 00:20:19.010
And occasionally I'll
wake up really hungry

00:20:19.010 --> 00:20:20.550
and I'll eat something before 11:00 AM.

00:20:20.550 --> 00:20:23.950
I'm not super strict about this
intermittent fasting thing.

00:20:23.950 --> 00:20:26.290
It just seems to be how
my appetite works best,

00:20:26.290 --> 00:20:27.740
given my schedule, et cetera.

00:20:29.430 --> 00:20:31.930
In the morning, I tend to be most focused.

00:20:31.930 --> 00:20:34.220
And I always associated that with the fact

00:20:34.220 --> 00:20:35.270
that I was fasted.

00:20:35.270 --> 00:20:37.370
I ingest water and some caffeine about

00:20:37.370 --> 00:20:39.470
90 minutes after waking
up, I drink my caffeine,

00:20:39.470 --> 00:20:41.800
but I hydrate from the time I
get up, et cetera, et cetera.

00:20:41.800 --> 00:20:43.800
And I know a lot of other people

00:20:43.800 --> 00:20:45.860
have had the experience of being fasted

00:20:45.860 --> 00:20:48.490
and feeling like they have
a lot of mental clarity.

00:20:48.490 --> 00:20:50.340
When you are in a fasted state,

00:20:50.340 --> 00:20:53.010
typically you are going to use fuels

00:20:53.010 --> 00:20:54.870
that are available to the neurons,

00:20:54.870 --> 00:20:57.940
based on your intake
of food the day before,

00:20:57.940 --> 00:20:59.730
maybe you're using some glycogen,

00:20:59.730 --> 00:21:01.320
maybe you're using some fat,

00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:03.250
maybe you're using some blood sugar,

00:21:03.250 --> 00:21:06.150
that's derived from other
storage sites in the body.

00:21:06.150 --> 00:21:09.490
You don't actually use
fat as a fuel source

00:21:09.490 --> 00:21:11.520
for neurons under typical conditions.

00:21:11.520 --> 00:21:13.830
But there are ways in
which proteins and fats

00:21:13.830 --> 00:21:16.600
and glycogen, et cetera,
are converted into fuel

00:21:16.600 --> 00:21:18.170
that neurons can use.

00:21:18.170 --> 00:21:19.690
What's interesting about this study

00:21:19.690 --> 00:21:23.290
is that the study says that when well fed,

00:21:23.290 --> 00:21:25.860
meaning when blood glucose, sugar,

00:21:25.860 --> 00:21:30.500
is at a properly elevated
level in the bloodstream,

00:21:30.500 --> 00:21:32.200
it can be delivered to the brain

00:21:32.200 --> 00:21:34.150
in a way that allows neurons to work best,

00:21:34.150 --> 00:21:36.230
which is really all just
to underscore the point

00:21:36.230 --> 00:21:37.063
that I made earlier,

00:21:37.063 --> 00:21:38.330
which is that your nervous system

00:21:38.330 --> 00:21:41.930
is extremely metabolically
demanding, and it loves glucose,

00:21:41.930 --> 00:21:44.150
neurons love glucose.

00:21:44.150 --> 00:21:46.030
So the takeaway from this study

00:21:46.030 --> 00:21:48.020
is not that you should avoid fasting,

00:21:48.020 --> 00:21:48.960
the takeaway from this study

00:21:48.960 --> 00:21:51.340
is that there are elements
of the fasted state

00:21:51.340 --> 00:21:52.820
in particular the elevations in things

00:21:52.820 --> 00:21:54.470
like epinephrine and norepinephrine,

00:21:54.470 --> 00:21:56.680
also called adrenaline and noradrenaline,

00:21:56.680 --> 00:21:58.873
that can give us this
kind of clarity of mind

00:21:58.873 --> 00:22:01.560
that many people are
pursuing when they fast.

00:22:01.560 --> 00:22:03.410
That's kind of one of the
reasons a lot of people fast,

00:22:03.410 --> 00:22:06.540
they like the way that they
feel, mentally and physically.

00:22:06.540 --> 00:22:09.520
But I think it's only fair to point out

00:22:09.520 --> 00:22:12.400
that glucose is the preferred
source of fuel for the brain.

00:22:12.400 --> 00:22:15.410
And this study that I mentioned
is one of many studies

00:22:15.410 --> 00:22:17.730
that have explored how nutritional status

00:22:17.730 --> 00:22:20.130
or blood glucose status
in the brain and body

00:22:20.130 --> 00:22:23.280
influence neuronal tuning
and neuronal function.

00:22:23.280 --> 00:22:26.960
And it really points to
the fact that ultimately,

00:22:26.960 --> 00:22:31.070
your brain as an organ, is
a glucose consuming machine.

00:22:31.070 --> 00:22:34.160
Now, when you eat a food,

00:22:34.160 --> 00:22:37.680
that food is broken down and
if it contains carbohydrates,

00:22:37.680 --> 00:22:39.460
it's going to be converted into glucose.

00:22:39.460 --> 00:22:41.670
And that glucose can't get
directly into the brain

00:22:41.670 --> 00:22:42.503
as a fuel source.

00:22:42.503 --> 00:22:43.830
It actually has to be carried across

00:22:43.830 --> 00:22:46.700
the so-called blood
brain barrier, the BBB,

00:22:46.700 --> 00:22:50.130
and the actual metabolism of glucose

00:22:50.130 --> 00:22:52.410
and the delivery of the
glucose to the neurons

00:22:52.410 --> 00:22:54.380
is carried out by a different cell type.

00:22:54.380 --> 00:22:56.140
And it's a cell type that
you should all know about

00:22:56.140 --> 00:22:59.380
because it's the most abundant
cell type in your brain,

00:22:59.380 --> 00:23:01.160
and maybe even in your
entire nervous system,

00:23:01.160 --> 00:23:03.220
and that's the so-called astrocyte.

00:23:03.220 --> 00:23:06.410
Astrocytes are one of
several types of glia,

00:23:06.410 --> 00:23:08.120
the word glia means glue.

00:23:08.120 --> 00:23:11.630
But many people have taken
that name, glia, glue,

00:23:11.630 --> 00:23:12.740
to think that, oh,

00:23:12.740 --> 00:23:13.643
the only thing that the
astrocytes are doing

00:23:13.643 --> 00:23:15.500
is just kind of holding things together.

00:23:15.500 --> 00:23:18.170
Actually, the astrocytes
are involved in delivering

00:23:18.170 --> 00:23:19.700
glucose to the neurons,

00:23:19.700 --> 00:23:21.760
they are critically involved in shaping

00:23:21.760 --> 00:23:24.010
your neuronal function
and brain plasticity,

00:23:24.010 --> 00:23:26.200
the brain's ability to change
in response to experience.

00:23:26.200 --> 00:23:27.750
So these astrocytes are like the little

00:23:27.750 --> 00:23:30.777
waiters and waitresses
bringing glucose to the neurons

00:23:30.777 --> 00:23:32.570
and the neurons are going
to do the heavy lifting

00:23:32.570 --> 00:23:35.453
that's involved in perception
and behavior and action.

00:23:36.290 --> 00:23:37.620
So if prior to this episode,

00:23:37.620 --> 00:23:40.810
you didn't already realize
that glucose, blood sugar,

00:23:40.810 --> 00:23:43.530
is vital to the function of your brain

00:23:43.530 --> 00:23:46.930
and other neurons of your
nervous system, now you know.

00:23:46.930 --> 00:23:49.660
And for those of you that have experienced

00:23:49.660 --> 00:23:52.180
the increase in mental clarity

00:23:52.180 --> 00:23:57.180
that comes after a properly
timed, properly composed,

00:23:57.350 --> 00:23:59.650
meaning it has the right
macronutrients in the right ratios,

00:23:59.650 --> 00:24:01.730
and a properly sized meal,

00:24:01.730 --> 00:24:03.560
well then now you have justification

00:24:03.560 --> 00:24:06.540
for eating something as
a way to improve the way

00:24:06.540 --> 00:24:07.373
that your brain works.

00:24:07.373 --> 00:24:09.060
It turns out that your
brain is going to work best

00:24:09.060 --> 00:24:10.850
when it's got glucose available,

00:24:10.850 --> 00:24:12.350
whether you like to fast or not,

00:24:12.350 --> 00:24:13.880
that's just the reality of things.

00:24:13.880 --> 00:24:17.410
The same thing is also true
for the neurons in your body.

00:24:17.410 --> 00:24:19.770
The way that you are able to
move the limbs of your body,

00:24:19.770 --> 00:24:22.650
the way you are able to perform exercise

00:24:22.650 --> 00:24:24.800
or movement of any kind for that matter,

00:24:24.800 --> 00:24:27.430
is because as neurons,
called motor neurons,

00:24:27.430 --> 00:24:30.210
send electrical potentials
to the muscle fibers,

00:24:30.210 --> 00:24:32.570
they release a neurotransmitter
called acetylcholine,

00:24:32.570 --> 00:24:34.480
which causes contraction
in the muscle fibers,

00:24:34.480 --> 00:24:36.730
and allows you to move your limbs.

00:24:36.730 --> 00:24:39.590
Those neurons are also very
metabolically demanding,

00:24:39.590 --> 00:24:41.210
especially when you're doing

00:24:41.210 --> 00:24:42.627
demanding types of physical work,

00:24:42.627 --> 00:24:44.570
and that could be cycling
or running or weightlifting

00:24:44.570 --> 00:24:46.940
or yoga or whatever it may be.

00:24:46.940 --> 00:24:50.440
Those neurons require a ton of glucose.

00:24:50.440 --> 00:24:52.460
If you've ever had the
experience of having to

00:24:52.460 --> 00:24:55.380
think very hard about how
you're generating a movement

00:24:55.380 --> 00:24:59.450
or force yourself to continue
to endure in a given exercise,

00:24:59.450 --> 00:25:00.283
you might have thought,

00:25:00.283 --> 00:25:01.960
oh, you know, I'm running out of fuel,

00:25:01.960 --> 00:25:04.320
that's why I'm getting
tired, it's hard to do.

00:25:04.320 --> 00:25:06.440
That's actually the case sometimes,

00:25:06.440 --> 00:25:09.120
but that's not always the case.

00:25:09.120 --> 00:25:11.310
One of the reasons that it feels like work

00:25:11.310 --> 00:25:13.370
is because your so called
upper motor neurons,

00:25:13.370 --> 00:25:14.960
the ones that control
the lower motor neurons

00:25:14.960 --> 00:25:17.210
in your spinal cord, which
control your muscles,

00:25:17.210 --> 00:25:19.110
they have to be very metabolically active.

00:25:19.110 --> 00:25:21.170
It's one thing to engage
in a reflexive movement

00:25:21.170 --> 00:25:22.220
where you're just walking around

00:25:22.220 --> 00:25:23.820
or if you're running continuously.

00:25:23.820 --> 00:25:25.990
But when you suddenly have to
focus on what you're doing,

00:25:25.990 --> 00:25:27.820
and you have to generate specific patterns

00:25:27.820 --> 00:25:28.880
of motor movement,

00:25:28.880 --> 00:25:32.440
well, that feels demanding because one,

00:25:32.440 --> 00:25:34.150
it increases the release of adrenaline

00:25:34.150 --> 00:25:34.983
in your brain and body,

00:25:34.983 --> 00:25:37.200
which makes you feel a little
bit agitated and more alert,

00:25:37.200 --> 00:25:40.180
but also deliberate thought,

00:25:40.180 --> 00:25:41.750
deliberately controlling the way

00:25:41.750 --> 00:25:43.930
that your brain and body is moving,

00:25:43.930 --> 00:25:45.820
requires more glucose uptake,

00:25:45.820 --> 00:25:48.000
more energy in those very neurons.

00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:50.700
And this is also why after
doing a long about of exercise,

00:25:50.700 --> 00:25:51.533
you might be tired,

00:25:51.533 --> 00:25:55.750
but also if you do a about of
skill learning of any kind,

00:25:55.750 --> 00:25:57.550
or if you've been reading
and thinking about

00:25:57.550 --> 00:25:58.620
what you're reading,

00:25:58.620 --> 00:26:01.450
or if you had a intense
conversation with somebody

00:26:01.450 --> 00:26:04.000
where you're really
forcing yourself to listen,

00:26:04.000 --> 00:26:05.460
and hopefully they're
listening to you too,

00:26:05.460 --> 00:26:07.120
and you're really trying to
parse what they're saying,

00:26:07.120 --> 00:26:08.390
and maybe you're doing that right now,

00:26:08.390 --> 00:26:11.007
and you're trying to really
track something, that's work,

00:26:11.007 --> 00:26:13.650
and that work requires
glucose uptake by neurons,

00:26:13.650 --> 00:26:15.620
both in the brain and in your body.

00:26:15.620 --> 00:26:17.110
Now that we've established that glucose

00:26:17.110 --> 00:26:20.540
is the preferred source of
fuel for the nervous system,

00:26:20.540 --> 00:26:23.610
I'd like to concentrate on
a few of the other types of

00:26:23.610 --> 00:26:27.280
sugars that we ingest on a common basis,

00:26:27.280 --> 00:26:29.280
and the impact that those have

00:26:29.280 --> 00:26:32.043
on brain function and body function.

00:26:33.550 --> 00:26:35.760
I'd particularly like
to focus on fructose.

00:26:35.760 --> 00:26:37.390
Fructose, of course, is found in fruit,

00:26:37.390 --> 00:26:40.450
it's also found in the infamous
high fructose corn syrup,

00:26:40.450 --> 00:26:42.620
which we will talk about today.

00:26:42.620 --> 00:26:44.410
It's worth pointing out
that the concentrations

00:26:44.410 --> 00:26:46.090
of fructose in fruit

00:26:46.090 --> 00:26:48.870
is quite low compared to the
concentrations of fructose

00:26:48.870 --> 00:26:50.390
in high fructose corn syrup.

00:26:50.390 --> 00:26:55.120
High fructose corn syrup is
approximately 50% fructose,

00:26:55.120 --> 00:26:56.030
which turns out to be

00:26:56.030 --> 00:27:01.030
an enormously high percentage
of anything, really,

00:27:01.770 --> 00:27:03.080
especially when we cut contrast that

00:27:03.080 --> 00:27:05.610
to the concentrations
of fructose in fruit.

00:27:05.610 --> 00:27:08.110
Fruits have other types
of sugars in them as well.

00:27:09.250 --> 00:27:11.170
You know, the sucrose
content of most fruit

00:27:11.170 --> 00:27:13.190
and fruit juices is low.

00:27:13.190 --> 00:27:15.960
Although there are some
fruits like melons, peaches,

00:27:15.960 --> 00:27:17.630
pineapples, and so forth,

00:27:17.630 --> 00:27:22.090
that contain a little less
than 10% or so of sucrose.

00:27:22.090 --> 00:27:24.550
Things like mangoES can
have a lot of sucrose.

00:27:24.550 --> 00:27:26.420
But typically the amount of fructose,

00:27:26.420 --> 00:27:29.240
fructose, I think is the
proper pronunciation,

00:27:29.240 --> 00:27:30.370
people are always correcting me.

00:27:30.370 --> 00:27:35.370
Fructose is anywhere from
1% to about 10%, right?

00:27:38.660 --> 00:27:40.450
It's really going to vary quite a bit.

00:27:40.450 --> 00:27:41.780
And many of you have probably heard

00:27:41.780 --> 00:27:43.340
of the so-called glycemic index,

00:27:43.340 --> 00:27:46.130
which is basically a measure
of how fast blood sugar rises

00:27:46.130 --> 00:27:48.440
after eating particular foods, et cetera.

00:27:48.440 --> 00:27:50.610
We're going to set aside
the glycemic index for now,

00:27:50.610 --> 00:27:51.970
we will come back to it.

00:27:51.970 --> 00:27:53.450
It has some relationship

00:27:53.450 --> 00:27:55.830
to the concentrations
of fructose in fruit.

00:27:55.830 --> 00:27:57.140
But the point that I'd like to make

00:27:57.140 --> 00:27:58.730
is that fructose as a sugar,

00:27:58.730 --> 00:28:03.040
is handled very differently
in the body than is glucose.

00:28:03.040 --> 00:28:06.350
But I also want to emphasize that

00:28:06.350 --> 00:28:09.990
because the percentage of
fructose in fruit is rather low,

00:28:09.990 --> 00:28:12.390
especially compared to
high fructose corn syrup,

00:28:13.230 --> 00:28:15.800
many people have demonized fructose

00:28:15.800 --> 00:28:18.500
saying that fructose makes you fat,

00:28:18.500 --> 00:28:20.770
or that fruit makes you fat.

00:28:20.770 --> 00:28:23.600
If you look at the data,
that's not really the case.

00:28:23.600 --> 00:28:24.620
The fact of the matter is that

00:28:24.620 --> 00:28:26.280
the concentrations of fructose in fruit

00:28:26.280 --> 00:28:29.460
are so low that unless someone
is consuming a lot of fruit,

00:28:29.460 --> 00:28:31.320
or they're consuming a lot of fruit

00:28:31.320 --> 00:28:34.960
on the backdrop of a highly processed diet

00:28:34.960 --> 00:28:36.600
or a diet that has a lot of other stuff

00:28:36.600 --> 00:28:38.260
that they might not want to be ingesting,

00:28:38.260 --> 00:28:40.300
you can't really say that
fructose is fattening.

00:28:40.300 --> 00:28:42.860
I don't really think that
there's any basis for saying that

00:28:42.860 --> 00:28:44.410
fructose itself is bad.

00:28:44.410 --> 00:28:47.510
Now high fructose corn
syrup is a different issue,

00:28:47.510 --> 00:28:50.850
and too much consumption of
anything, fructose included,

00:28:50.850 --> 00:28:52.580
whether or not it comes
fruit or otherwise,

00:28:52.580 --> 00:28:55.390
can be a problem for
the ways that it impacts

00:28:55.390 --> 00:28:58.560
the neural circuits that process sugar,

00:28:58.560 --> 00:29:00.320
not just glucose, but fructose.

00:29:00.320 --> 00:29:02.340
And so we'll illustrate those
neural circuits in a bit,

00:29:02.340 --> 00:29:04.800
and it'll become very clear to all of you,

00:29:04.800 --> 00:29:05.710
regardless of whether or not

00:29:05.710 --> 00:29:08.010
you have a background in
biology or metabolism,

00:29:08.010 --> 00:29:09.320
nutrition, or otherwise,

00:29:09.320 --> 00:29:12.350
why ingesting very high
concentrations of fructose

00:29:12.350 --> 00:29:13.900
is not going to be a good thing

00:29:13.900 --> 00:29:15.800
for the way that your brain functions.

00:29:16.690 --> 00:29:19.610
One of the key distinctions
between glucose and fructose

00:29:19.610 --> 00:29:21.980
is that fructose most likely

00:29:21.980 --> 00:29:23.860
cannot directly access the brain.

00:29:23.860 --> 00:29:27.580
It actually needs to be converted
into glucose in the liver.

00:29:27.580 --> 00:29:30.010
And the way that conversion occurs

00:29:30.010 --> 00:29:33.410
feeds back to a set of
hormones and neural pathways

00:29:33.410 --> 00:29:34.860
that we talked about earlier,

00:29:34.860 --> 00:29:36.540
which have a lot to do with appetite.

00:29:36.540 --> 00:29:40.663
And to just summarize what is
now a lot of very solid data,

00:29:42.130 --> 00:29:44.900
fructose and specifically fructose,

00:29:44.900 --> 00:29:48.520
has the ability to reduce
certain hormones and peptides

00:29:48.520 --> 00:29:52.910
in our body whose main job
is to suppress Ghrelin.

00:29:52.910 --> 00:29:56.220
As you recall, Ghrelin is
a hormone that increases

00:29:56.220 --> 00:29:58.230
the longer it's been since we've eaten,

00:29:58.230 --> 00:29:59.780
and Ghrelin makes us hungry

00:29:59.780 --> 00:30:03.120
by stimulating particular
neurons in our hypothalamus,

00:30:03.120 --> 00:30:05.240
it actually makes us really want to eat.

00:30:05.240 --> 00:30:08.170
And in particular, really
makes us want to eat sugary

00:30:08.170 --> 00:30:09.620
and fatty foods.

00:30:09.620 --> 00:30:12.780
Fructose reduces the
activity of the hormones

00:30:12.780 --> 00:30:14.600
that reduce Ghrelin .

00:30:14.600 --> 00:30:16.340
And so the net consequence of that

00:30:16.340 --> 00:30:19.750
is that fructose increases Ghrelin.

00:30:19.750 --> 00:30:24.340
So, although I and I think
pretty much everyone out there,

00:30:24.340 --> 00:30:26.300
say for a few individuals,

00:30:26.300 --> 00:30:28.370
agrees that calories in and calories out

00:30:28.370 --> 00:30:30.320
is the fundamental
principle of weight loss,

00:30:30.320 --> 00:30:32.150
weight maintenance or weight gain.

00:30:32.150 --> 00:30:36.190
Ingesting fructose shifts
our hormone system,

00:30:36.190 --> 00:30:39.900
and as a consequence, our neural
pathways within our brain,

00:30:39.900 --> 00:30:41.270
the hypothalamus,

00:30:41.270 --> 00:30:46.270
to be hungrier regardless of
how many calories we've eaten.

00:30:47.540 --> 00:30:50.250
Now I also want to be absolutely clear,

00:30:50.250 --> 00:30:53.370
this does not mean that eating
an apple or eating a melon

00:30:53.370 --> 00:30:55.690
or eating a couple of
apricots or something

00:30:55.690 --> 00:30:58.970
is going to make you hyperphagic,

00:30:58.970 --> 00:31:00.200
meaning it's going to
make you just want to

00:31:00.200 --> 00:31:02.280
eat, eat, and eat, that's
simply not the case.

00:31:02.280 --> 00:31:05.880
But if you compare fructose
and you compare glucose,

00:31:05.880 --> 00:31:07.910
not only are they metabolized differently

00:31:07.910 --> 00:31:10.930
in the brain and body,
but in addition to that,

00:31:10.930 --> 00:31:14.640
fructose has this impact
of reducing the hormones

00:31:14.640 --> 00:31:17.990
that reduce hunger hormones
in neural circuits.

00:31:17.990 --> 00:31:20.740
And so fructose does
have this kind of twist

00:31:20.740 --> 00:31:23.620
in its phenotype, right?

00:31:23.620 --> 00:31:27.050
Or I guess if fructose
had a dating profile,

00:31:27.050 --> 00:31:31.270
this would be a kind of a
red flag in that profile,

00:31:31.270 --> 00:31:33.730
because fructose itself,

00:31:33.730 --> 00:31:38.090
while it's actually a pretty
good fuel source in many ways,

00:31:38.090 --> 00:31:40.200
and it's often packaged
in things like fruits,

00:31:40.200 --> 00:31:42.500
which bring along fiber
and vitamins and minerals,

00:31:42.500 --> 00:31:45.040
that I think for many of us are things

00:31:45.040 --> 00:31:47.320
that we should be eating more
of and ingesting more of,

00:31:47.320 --> 00:31:51.050
it can suppress the pathways
that suppress hunger.

00:31:51.050 --> 00:31:53.770
And as a consequence,
it can increase hunger.

00:31:53.770 --> 00:31:57.000
So current recommendations for most people

00:31:57.000 --> 00:31:58.780
are to eat more fruits and vegetables.

00:31:58.780 --> 00:32:02.230
But for those of you that are
trying to control your hunger,

00:32:02.230 --> 00:32:05.613
ingesting a lot of fruit to
is probably not going to be

00:32:05.613 --> 00:32:06.446
a good idea.

00:32:06.446 --> 00:32:08.850
Certainly, ingesting it from
high fructose corn syrup

00:32:08.850 --> 00:32:09.810
is not going to be a good idea

00:32:09.810 --> 00:32:12.620
because of the enormous
percentages of fructose

00:32:12.620 --> 00:32:15.730
in high fructose corn syrup,
50%, or sometimes even more.

00:32:15.730 --> 00:32:17.220
But even from fruit,

00:32:17.220 --> 00:32:18.570
some people will find that fruit

00:32:18.570 --> 00:32:19.890
really quenches their appetite.

00:32:19.890 --> 00:32:22.487
Other people will find that
fruit stimulates their appetite.

00:32:22.487 --> 00:32:24.700
And I suppose if you're trying
to stimulate your appetite,

00:32:24.700 --> 00:32:27.820
then ingesting more fruit
might actually be advantageous

00:32:27.820 --> 00:32:28.653
to you.

00:32:28.653 --> 00:32:31.380
So fructose provides a bridge for us

00:32:31.380 --> 00:32:35.633
between a particular kind
of sugar, hormone function,

00:32:36.710 --> 00:32:39.610
in this case Ghrelin and the hypothalamus.

00:32:39.610 --> 00:32:41.050
which leads us to the next question,

00:32:41.050 --> 00:32:43.830
which is what is it about sugar

00:32:43.830 --> 00:32:46.070
that makes it such an
attractive thing for us?

00:32:46.070 --> 00:32:47.840
Why do we like it so much?

00:32:47.840 --> 00:32:51.010
And the obvious answer that
most people arrive at is,

00:32:51.010 --> 00:32:53.580
well, it just tastes really, really good.

00:32:53.580 --> 00:32:56.850
But that's actually not the way it works.

00:32:56.850 --> 00:33:00.740
The rewarding property
is as we say of sugar,

00:33:00.740 --> 00:33:03.890
whether or not they come in
the form of sucrose or fructose

00:33:03.890 --> 00:33:08.710
or foods that increase
glucose to a very high level,

00:33:08.710 --> 00:33:13.050
actually is not just related
to the taste of the foods

00:33:13.050 --> 00:33:14.110
that produce that elevation

00:33:14.110 --> 00:33:16.120
in glucose, sucrose, or fructose.

00:33:16.120 --> 00:33:19.350
It is in part, but that's
only part of the story,

00:33:19.350 --> 00:33:22.380
and the rest of the story,
once you understand it,

00:33:22.380 --> 00:33:25.840
can actually place you in a
position to much better control

00:33:25.840 --> 00:33:28.420
your sugar intake of all kinds,

00:33:28.420 --> 00:33:30.650
but also your food intake
in ways that can allow you

00:33:30.650 --> 00:33:33.400
to make much better choices
about the foods you ingest.

00:33:33.400 --> 00:33:34.450
And actually at this point,

00:33:34.450 --> 00:33:36.770
I should probably give a confession,

00:33:36.770 --> 00:33:38.840
I've said today, and I'll say it again,

00:33:38.840 --> 00:33:40.270
and I've said it I'm pretty podcast.

00:33:40.270 --> 00:33:42.510
I don't have much of a sweet
tooth and indeed that's true.

00:33:42.510 --> 00:33:45.460
And I can kind of pass
on chocolate or ice cream

00:33:45.460 --> 00:33:46.293
or things like that,

00:33:46.293 --> 00:33:48.100
it seems like with each successive year,

00:33:48.100 --> 00:33:50.840
sweet things are less
and less appealing to me.

00:33:50.840 --> 00:33:55.243
Of course, savory foods,
anything that is really fatty,

00:33:56.130 --> 00:33:59.740
salty, savory, those don't
last long in my presence,

00:33:59.740 --> 00:34:02.750
but I always say, I don't really
like sweet things so much,

00:34:02.750 --> 00:34:04.210
and I like sweet people,

00:34:04.210 --> 00:34:07.300
but I don't tend to like
sweet foods, which is true.

00:34:07.300 --> 00:34:10.480
But there's probably one
exception and that's mangoes.

00:34:10.480 --> 00:34:12.230
And it turns out that mangoes

00:34:12.230 --> 00:34:14.680
have the highest percentage
of sugar in them,

00:34:14.680 --> 00:34:18.610
in particular fructose, as
well as other forms of sugars.

00:34:18.610 --> 00:34:22.640
So what I do, because
I love mango so much,

00:34:22.640 --> 00:34:25.430
is I will have mangoes
probably twice a week,

00:34:25.430 --> 00:34:28.660
but I'll have them after some
sort of resistance training

00:34:28.660 --> 00:34:30.440
or hard run or something like that.

00:34:30.440 --> 00:34:33.290
Because it is the case that
after you exercise hard,

00:34:33.290 --> 00:34:34.960
in particular exercise that is

00:34:34.960 --> 00:34:37.020
of the high intensity variety,

00:34:37.020 --> 00:34:41.780
that your body is more efficient
at using circulating sugar.

00:34:41.780 --> 00:34:44.820
It's able to store that
or use that for fuel.

00:34:44.820 --> 00:34:47.670
And so what I'll typically
do is just take the mango,

00:34:47.670 --> 00:34:49.020
I actually eat the peels too.

00:34:49.020 --> 00:34:50.500
I know there probably some
people are going to cringe

00:34:50.500 --> 00:34:52.010
when they hear that,
I find them delicious.

00:34:52.010 --> 00:34:53.560
So I'll just bite into
those things like apples,

00:34:53.560 --> 00:34:55.370
I don't eat the pits, however.

00:34:55.370 --> 00:34:57.370
So now I want to take us on a journey

00:34:57.370 --> 00:34:59.480
into the nervous system to explain,

00:34:59.480 --> 00:35:01.520
the pathways in the brain and body

00:35:01.520 --> 00:35:04.490
that regulate our appetite for sugar.

00:35:04.490 --> 00:35:07.040
Now, keep in mind what I
already told you before,

00:35:07.040 --> 00:35:08.630
which is that when we ingest foods,

00:35:08.630 --> 00:35:11.460
they're broken down
into various components

00:35:11.460 --> 00:35:14.060
and glucose is going to
be shuttled to the brain,

00:35:14.060 --> 00:35:16.080
and of course, to other
neurons in our spinal cord

00:35:16.080 --> 00:35:18.750
and elsewhere, and to our muscles, cetera,

00:35:18.750 --> 00:35:22.230
in order for all of those
cells and organs and tissues

00:35:22.230 --> 00:35:23.530
is to be able to function.

00:35:25.140 --> 00:35:28.020
The fact that so many cells
and organs and tissues

00:35:28.020 --> 00:35:30.450
require glucose in order to function

00:35:30.450 --> 00:35:33.420
has led to a situation where you have

00:35:33.420 --> 00:35:36.500
dedicated neural machinery,
pieces of your brain,

00:35:36.500 --> 00:35:39.890
that are almost entirely,
if not entirely devoted,

00:35:39.890 --> 00:35:44.740
to seeking out of sugar or
foods that contain sugars.

00:35:44.740 --> 00:35:48.320
And to make sure that you
not only seek those out,

00:35:48.320 --> 00:35:50.120
but you know where those foods are

00:35:50.120 --> 00:35:52.670
and that you ingest more
and more and more of them.

00:35:53.530 --> 00:35:55.940
And there are two main ways
that these neural circuits work.

00:35:55.940 --> 00:35:57.970
In fact, we can see that
there are two neural circuits

00:35:57.970 --> 00:36:00.130
entirely that work in parallel.

00:36:00.130 --> 00:36:03.453
And this is a common theme
throughout the nervous system,

00:36:04.370 --> 00:36:06.150
and that's parallel pathways.

00:36:06.150 --> 00:36:08.640
Parallel pathways are the
ways that you can distinguish

00:36:08.640 --> 00:36:09.780
light from dark,

00:36:09.780 --> 00:36:11.320
parallel pathways are
the ways that you can

00:36:11.320 --> 00:36:13.650
distinguish high pitched
sounds from low pitched sounds,

00:36:13.650 --> 00:36:15.070
parallel pathways are the ways

00:36:15.070 --> 00:36:18.250
that you can flex your muscles
versus extend your muscles.

00:36:18.250 --> 00:36:19.083
For instance,

00:36:19.083 --> 00:36:20.460
if you move your wrist
closer to your shoulder,

00:36:20.460 --> 00:36:23.680
you're flexing your bicep and
you're actually inhibiting,

00:36:23.680 --> 00:36:25.370
you're actually preventing
the action of your tricep.

00:36:25.370 --> 00:36:28.600
If you move your wrist
away from your shoulder,

00:36:28.600 --> 00:36:31.530
you are essentially using
your extensor, your tricep,

00:36:31.530 --> 00:36:33.780
and you're inhibiting the
activity of your bicep.

00:36:33.780 --> 00:36:35.970
So for every function in your body

00:36:35.970 --> 00:36:38.450
that you might think is
controlled by one brain area

00:36:38.450 --> 00:36:39.950
or one neural circuit,

00:36:39.950 --> 00:36:42.690
almost always there are
two or more so called

00:36:42.690 --> 00:36:44.670
parallel pathways that ensure

00:36:44.670 --> 00:36:47.960
that that particular behavior happens.

00:36:47.960 --> 00:36:51.060
Now, in the case of sugar consumption,

00:36:51.060 --> 00:36:54.150
the two parallel pathways
involve one pathway

00:36:54.150 --> 00:36:59.050
related to the actual taste
and the perception of taste

00:36:59.050 --> 00:37:01.310
that lead, not just you,

00:37:01.310 --> 00:37:03.610
but every animal that we're aware of,

00:37:03.610 --> 00:37:07.193
to seek more sweet containing foods.

00:37:08.090 --> 00:37:10.000
The other parallel pathway

00:37:10.000 --> 00:37:14.840
is related to the nutritive
component of sweet foods.

00:37:14.840 --> 00:37:17.640
Meaning the degree to which a given food

00:37:17.640 --> 00:37:19.550
will raise blood glucose.

00:37:19.550 --> 00:37:20.560
I want to repeat that.

00:37:20.560 --> 00:37:23.900
One pathway in your brain
and body is devoted to

00:37:23.900 --> 00:37:26.510
getting you to seek out
sweet tasting things

00:37:26.510 --> 00:37:28.360
that you perceive as sweet,

00:37:28.360 --> 00:37:31.650
and another parallel pathway is devoted

00:37:31.650 --> 00:37:33.890
to getting you to seek out foods

00:37:33.890 --> 00:37:37.200
that lead to increases in blood glucose.

00:37:37.200 --> 00:37:38.990
It just so happens that the foods

00:37:38.990 --> 00:37:41.680
that lead to big
increases in blood glucose

00:37:41.680 --> 00:37:44.900
typically are associated
with that sweet taste.

00:37:44.900 --> 00:37:46.620
Now, this is distinctly different

00:37:46.620 --> 00:37:48.980
than the neural pathways
that control seeking

00:37:48.980 --> 00:37:53.250
of savory foods or salty
foods or spicy foods,

00:37:53.250 --> 00:37:55.230
for that matter, or bitter foods.

00:37:55.230 --> 00:37:58.840
The sweet pathway is what
we would call hardwired.

00:37:58.840 --> 00:38:01.000
It exists, as far as we
know, in every mammal,

00:38:01.000 --> 00:38:04.070
it even exists in fruit
flies, hence fruit fly.

00:38:04.070 --> 00:38:07.260
Basically getting sweet
stuff into the body

00:38:07.260 --> 00:38:09.440
might seem like it has a
lot to do with the taste,

00:38:09.440 --> 00:38:13.910
but it has just as much to do
with the nutritive components

00:38:13.910 --> 00:38:15.990
that sweet tasting foods carry,

00:38:15.990 --> 00:38:17.890
and the fact that your nervous system

00:38:17.890 --> 00:38:22.220
and so many cells in your
brain and body run on glucose.

00:38:22.220 --> 00:38:25.570
If you recall earlier, I said,
even if you ingest fructose,

00:38:25.570 --> 00:38:28.863
fructose can be converted
into glucose in the liver.

00:38:29.830 --> 00:38:30.800
And I mentioned, of course,

00:38:30.800 --> 00:38:32.730
that fructose may actually
work directly on the brain,

00:38:32.730 --> 00:38:34.510
that's still unclear for humans,

00:38:34.510 --> 00:38:36.920
the jury's still out on that, we will see.

00:38:36.920 --> 00:38:39.960
But the fundamental
thing to understand here

00:38:39.960 --> 00:38:42.480
is that when you think you
want a piece of chocolate

00:38:42.480 --> 00:38:44.160
or you think you want a piece of cake

00:38:44.160 --> 00:38:46.830
or you're craving something sweet,

00:38:46.830 --> 00:38:48.650
you are both craving the taste

00:38:48.650 --> 00:38:51.730
and your neurons are literally craving

00:38:51.730 --> 00:38:55.470
the nutritive components
that arrive with that taste.

00:38:55.470 --> 00:38:57.190
And simply by understanding that

00:38:57.190 --> 00:39:00.970
can allow you to circumvent
some of the sugar cravings

00:39:00.970 --> 00:39:05.530
that you might otherwise be a
complete hopeless victim to.

00:39:05.530 --> 00:39:07.610
Also in this episode,
I will talk about ways

00:39:07.610 --> 00:39:09.400
that you can sort of undermine

00:39:09.400 --> 00:39:11.600
or short circuit these
circuits, if you will,

00:39:11.600 --> 00:39:14.680
in order to reduce sugar
cravings on a regular basis,

00:39:14.680 --> 00:39:16.380
if that's your goal.

00:39:16.380 --> 00:39:18.080
Two parallel pathways,

00:39:18.080 --> 00:39:19.320
one of the parallel pathways

00:39:19.320 --> 00:39:21.110
has to do with conscious perception.

00:39:21.110 --> 00:39:24.560
So animals of all kinds
of mice, rats and humans,

00:39:24.560 --> 00:39:28.703
will prefer sugary taste
to non-sugary taste.

00:39:30.330 --> 00:39:33.250
When we eat something that tastes sweet,

00:39:33.250 --> 00:39:37.890
we register that sweet taste
by way of sweet receptors,

00:39:37.890 --> 00:39:41.910
literally little ports
or portals of neurons

00:39:41.910 --> 00:39:44.180
on our tongue and on our pallet,

00:39:44.180 --> 00:39:45.180
a lot of people don't realize this,

00:39:45.180 --> 00:39:48.310
but there are a lot of taste
receptors on the soft pallet

00:39:48.310 --> 00:39:50.710
and around the mouth, on
the sides of the mouth.

00:39:50.710 --> 00:39:51.960
So you're actually tasting things,

00:39:51.960 --> 00:39:53.920
not just with your tongue,
but with your entire mouth

00:39:53.920 --> 00:39:54.860
and your pallet.

00:39:54.860 --> 00:39:57.360
So when you ingest something sweet,

00:39:57.360 --> 00:39:59.840
very quickly there are signals
sent from those neurons

00:39:59.840 --> 00:40:04.310
in your mouth to brain areas
that cause you to seek out

00:40:04.310 --> 00:40:05.980
or at least pay attention to,

00:40:05.980 --> 00:40:08.810
the source and the abundance
of those sweet things.

00:40:08.810 --> 00:40:11.450
They literally change your perception.

00:40:11.450 --> 00:40:13.980
In fact, there are beautiful
neuroimaging studies

00:40:13.980 --> 00:40:18.010
that show that when people
ingest a sugary drink,

00:40:18.010 --> 00:40:22.260
their perception of images
of foods change very much

00:40:22.260 --> 00:40:24.810
to make those foods
appear more appetizing,

00:40:24.810 --> 00:40:28.350
and not just foods that contain sugar.

00:40:28.350 --> 00:40:30.060
Results of those studies do show

00:40:30.060 --> 00:40:31.440
that there's an increase for instance,

00:40:31.440 --> 00:40:35.440
in the perception of detail
and images of ice cream,

00:40:35.440 --> 00:40:36.980
after you ingest a sweet drink

00:40:36.980 --> 00:40:40.210
or even if you put like a
hard candy into your mouth,

00:40:40.210 --> 00:40:42.260
it will make you seek
out sugary things more,

00:40:42.260 --> 00:40:44.190
it will make sugary things
look more appetizing,

00:40:44.190 --> 00:40:46.940
but also other foods, more appetizing.

00:40:46.940 --> 00:40:49.060
So I think it's important that
people recognize that fact,

00:40:49.060 --> 00:40:51.730
that when you have a
sweet taste in your mouth

00:40:51.730 --> 00:40:54.910
or when you've tasted something
sweet within your mouth,

00:40:54.910 --> 00:40:59.750
I should say, your perception
of has immediately shifted.

00:40:59.750 --> 00:41:01.580
These are fast neural pathways.

00:41:01.580 --> 00:41:03.790
We'll get into some of the
brain structures in a moment,

00:41:03.790 --> 00:41:05.510
but these are fast neural pathways

00:41:05.510 --> 00:41:10.510
that shift your entire self
toward seeking more sugary stuff

00:41:10.980 --> 00:41:12.770
and more food generally.

00:41:12.770 --> 00:41:14.830
Now, does that mean that
you should never ingest

00:41:14.830 --> 00:41:15.880
anything sweet?

00:41:15.880 --> 00:41:18.690
No, certainly I'm not saying that,

00:41:18.690 --> 00:41:20.080
everyone has to decide for themselves

00:41:20.080 --> 00:41:22.150
what the appropriate
amount of sugar intake is,

00:41:22.150 --> 00:41:24.930
but I find it remarkable when people say,

00:41:24.930 --> 00:41:26.930
oh, you know, I need to get my sugar fix,

00:41:26.930 --> 00:41:28.700
or I need to have my chocolate,

00:41:28.700 --> 00:41:29.960
or I need to have a
little bit of something

00:41:29.960 --> 00:41:32.660
to just kind of take care
of that sugar appetite.

00:41:32.660 --> 00:41:35.290
Because in taking care
of that sugar appetite,

00:41:35.290 --> 00:41:36.740
maybe for the very disciplined of you,

00:41:36.740 --> 00:41:38.047
you can just have that
one piece of chocolate

00:41:38.047 --> 00:41:40.170
and it's great, and you can relish in it,

00:41:40.170 --> 00:41:43.380
but it does shift the
way that you perceive

00:41:43.380 --> 00:41:44.550
other foods as well.

00:41:44.550 --> 00:41:47.460
And the way it does that is through our,

00:41:47.460 --> 00:41:50.520
probably if you're listener
this podcast, now old friend,

00:41:50.520 --> 00:41:53.910
but incredible neuro modulator, dopamine.

00:41:53.910 --> 00:41:56.080
Dopamine is a molecule
that is released from

00:41:56.080 --> 00:41:57.790
several places in the brain.

00:41:57.790 --> 00:42:00.040
There's a so-called
mesolimbic reward pathway,

00:42:00.040 --> 00:42:02.530
which is a whole set of places
in the brain or circuits,

00:42:02.530 --> 00:42:05.230
designed to get us motivated and craving

00:42:05.230 --> 00:42:06.840
and in pursuit of things.

00:42:06.840 --> 00:42:08.640
And then of course there
are areas of the brain

00:42:08.640 --> 00:42:09.730
that are involved in movement

00:42:09.730 --> 00:42:13.650
that are linked up with those
areas involved in motivation.

00:42:13.650 --> 00:42:14.483
That makes perfect sense.

00:42:14.483 --> 00:42:17.260
Why would you have a brain
area involved in motivation

00:42:17.260 --> 00:42:19.100
if you couldn't actually do
something with that motivation?

00:42:19.100 --> 00:42:21.000
So the way that your brain is designed

00:42:21.000 --> 00:42:22.940
is when there's an increase in dopamine,

00:42:22.940 --> 00:42:24.440
in the mesolimbic reward pathway,

00:42:24.440 --> 00:42:26.340
there are signals sent
to an area of the brain

00:42:26.340 --> 00:42:27.190
called the striatum,

00:42:27.190 --> 00:42:28.570
we're going to spend a
little bit of time today

00:42:28.570 --> 00:42:29.460
in the striatum.

00:42:29.460 --> 00:42:31.600
It's got a dorsal part,
meaning in an upper part

00:42:31.600 --> 00:42:33.860
and a ventral part,
which means a lower part.

00:42:33.860 --> 00:42:36.250
And the dopamine sent to those area,

00:42:36.250 --> 00:42:39.500
places us, excuse me,
into modes of action,

00:42:39.500 --> 00:42:41.963
to pursue particular things.

00:42:43.360 --> 00:42:47.020
Sugar or sweet tastes, I should
say, to be more specific,

00:42:47.020 --> 00:42:49.620
have an incredibly potent ability

00:42:49.620 --> 00:42:51.440
to activate dopamine release

00:42:51.440 --> 00:42:53.630
within the mesolimbic reward pathway.

00:42:53.630 --> 00:42:56.170
This has been shown over
and over and over again

00:42:57.310 --> 00:43:00.210
in animal models and in humans.

00:43:00.210 --> 00:43:03.510
This is especially true, I should mention,

00:43:03.510 --> 00:43:06.750
through the ingestion of sweet liquids.

00:43:06.750 --> 00:43:08.990
Now this becomes a very
important point to us

00:43:08.990 --> 00:43:12.790
a little later on when we
talk about the proliferation

00:43:12.790 --> 00:43:17.200
of sodas and sweet drinks,
and dare I even say,

00:43:17.200 --> 00:43:19.490
non-sugar or diet sodas,

00:43:19.490 --> 00:43:20.780
we're going to get into
that a little bit later.

00:43:20.780 --> 00:43:23.520
They are perhaps one of
the most third rail topics

00:43:23.520 --> 00:43:25.090
in nutrition.

00:43:25.090 --> 00:43:28.540
But when we ingest something sweet,

00:43:28.540 --> 00:43:30.350
the perception of that sweet taste

00:43:30.350 --> 00:43:33.270
increases dopamine in the
mesolimbic reward pathways,

00:43:33.270 --> 00:43:36.630
which then are conveyed to
pathways for motor behavior,

00:43:36.630 --> 00:43:39.700
and in general, place us
into modes of focused action

00:43:39.700 --> 00:43:42.920
toward getting more of whatever was sweet.

00:43:42.920 --> 00:43:45.060
Again, for those of you
that are very disciplined,

00:43:45.060 --> 00:43:47.770
you can probably eat that
one piece of chocolate

00:43:47.770 --> 00:43:49.450
and be just fine.

00:43:49.450 --> 00:43:52.750
But if you understand the
way that dopamine works,

00:43:52.750 --> 00:43:54.960
what you'll realize is that
when this dopamine pathway

00:43:54.960 --> 00:43:56.020
is triggered,

00:43:56.020 --> 00:44:00.300
it tends to create not the
sensation or the perception

00:44:00.300 --> 00:44:03.180
of satiety, of feeling
like something is enough,

00:44:03.180 --> 00:44:07.370
but rather to produce the
sensation of wanting more.

00:44:07.370 --> 00:44:09.370
As described in the episode that I hosted

00:44:09.370 --> 00:44:11.450
with my phenomenal colleague

00:44:11.450 --> 00:44:14.090
from Stanford School of
Medicine, Dr. Anna Lembke,

00:44:14.090 --> 00:44:16.653
she's an expert on addiction
and dopamine pathways,

00:44:17.960 --> 00:44:20.260
the dopamine circuits of the brain

00:44:20.260 --> 00:44:23.500
have what we call a pleasure pain balance.

00:44:23.500 --> 00:44:27.680
And there I'm paraphrasing
what Dr. Anna Lembke has said,

00:44:27.680 --> 00:44:29.497
and has written about
in her beautiful book,

00:44:29.497 --> 00:44:30.330
"Dopamine Nation."

00:44:30.330 --> 00:44:32.390
If you haven't read that
book, I highly recommend it.

00:44:32.390 --> 00:44:34.740
Whether or not you have
issues with addiction

00:44:34.740 --> 00:44:37.170
or you know people that do, or you don't,

00:44:37.170 --> 00:44:39.170
it's an incredibly important read,

00:44:39.170 --> 00:44:40.820
especially if you're
interested in understanding

00:44:40.820 --> 00:44:44.590
motivated behaviors and ways
to channel your behaviors

00:44:44.590 --> 00:44:46.910
in life toward healthy,
motivated behaviors,

00:44:46.910 --> 00:44:49.770
and make sure that you avoid
some of the common pitfalls

00:44:49.770 --> 00:44:51.930
that people fall into, not just addiction,

00:44:51.930 --> 00:44:55.240
but things like overuse of social media

00:44:55.240 --> 00:45:00.160
or wasting time in general,
it's a phenomenal book.

00:45:00.160 --> 00:45:03.293
In that book, and of course,
within research articles,

00:45:04.210 --> 00:45:06.690
you will find evidence of this so-called

00:45:06.690 --> 00:45:10.190
pleasure pain balance that exists

00:45:10.190 --> 00:45:11.430
within our dopamine circuits.

00:45:11.430 --> 00:45:13.860
Nobody has dopamine
circuits that allow them

00:45:13.860 --> 00:45:15.407
to escape this pleasure pain balance.

00:45:15.407 --> 00:45:17.430
And the way this works is that anytime

00:45:17.430 --> 00:45:21.060
that we engage in a behavior
or we ingest something

00:45:21.060 --> 00:45:22.993
that increases our levels of dopamine,

00:45:24.400 --> 00:45:27.810
there is a subsequent increase
in the neural circuits

00:45:27.810 --> 00:45:32.790
that control our sense of
frustration, pain, and lack.

00:45:32.790 --> 00:45:34.990
You can actually notice this phenomenon

00:45:35.830 --> 00:45:36.770
if, for instance,

00:45:36.770 --> 00:45:39.480
you're somebody who
really likes chocolate,

00:45:39.480 --> 00:45:41.500
or you really like something else,

00:45:41.500 --> 00:45:44.510
pay attention to the
way that you experience

00:45:44.510 --> 00:45:45.993
indulging in that thing.

00:45:47.240 --> 00:45:49.280
If you eat that piece of chocolate

00:45:49.280 --> 00:45:52.440
and you really focus on
savoring its amazing taste,

00:45:52.440 --> 00:45:57.440
you'll notice that it provides
some quenching of your desire

00:45:57.710 --> 00:46:01.580
for let's say sweet stuff
or chocolate or both,

00:46:01.580 --> 00:46:05.240
but right as you stop experiencing that,

00:46:05.240 --> 00:46:08.450
right as that chocolate intake tapers off,

00:46:08.450 --> 00:46:09.940
as you swallow it down your throat,

00:46:09.940 --> 00:46:12.750
or you just pause for a second afterwards,

00:46:12.750 --> 00:46:15.580
what you'll notice is
that your brain and body

00:46:15.580 --> 00:46:19.020
actually orient toward wanting more.

00:46:19.020 --> 00:46:22.340
And that wanting of more
is really the action

00:46:22.340 --> 00:46:24.360
of the neural circuits that underlie pain

00:46:24.360 --> 00:46:27.920
and are pushing your
dopamine levels back down.

00:46:27.920 --> 00:46:30.960
And when these circuits
go awry or I should say,

00:46:30.960 --> 00:46:34.970
when people fail to who control themselves

00:46:34.970 --> 00:46:37.510
within the context of that
pleasure pain balance,

00:46:37.510 --> 00:46:40.380
the typical behavior is to
reach for yet another chocolate

00:46:40.380 --> 00:46:44.700
or to then look for something
that will quench that desire

00:46:44.700 --> 00:46:46.440
and get dopamine levels back up.

00:46:46.440 --> 00:46:49.170
Now the way these pleasure
pain circuits work

00:46:49.170 --> 00:46:50.470
is very diabolical.

00:46:50.470 --> 00:46:51.890
Because it turns out that were you to take

00:46:51.890 --> 00:46:52.780
another piece of chocolate,

00:46:52.780 --> 00:46:54.770
yes, your dopamine
levels would go back up,

00:46:54.770 --> 00:46:57.870
but not to the same extent that they did

00:46:57.870 --> 00:47:00.700
the first bite of chocolate that you had.

00:47:00.700 --> 00:47:02.850
In fact, we can say that
the longer it's been

00:47:02.850 --> 00:47:06.200
since you've indulged in
something that you really enjoy

00:47:06.200 --> 00:47:08.090
or would like,

00:47:08.090 --> 00:47:10.890
the greater the dopamine
you will experience

00:47:10.890 --> 00:47:13.540
when you finally engage in that behavior

00:47:13.540 --> 00:47:16.210
or indulge to that
thing, ingest that thing,

00:47:16.210 --> 00:47:19.030
and the greater the dopamine increase,

00:47:19.030 --> 00:47:22.450
the greater the subsequent
action of those pain circuits.

00:47:22.450 --> 00:47:25.680
So this puts you on a
very complicated seesaw.

00:47:25.680 --> 00:47:28.590
It's a very wobbly
precarious state to be in.

00:47:28.590 --> 00:47:30.430
Which is not to say you shouldn't
have a piece of chocolate,

00:47:30.430 --> 00:47:35.330
it's just to say that the
sweet taste of sweet things

00:47:35.330 --> 00:47:37.270
in particular, things
that we crave very much

00:47:37.270 --> 00:47:38.300
and we wait and wait and wait,

00:47:38.300 --> 00:47:40.250
and then we allow ourselves to indulge.

00:47:41.370 --> 00:47:43.590
Those trigger changes
in our neurochemistry,

00:47:43.590 --> 00:47:44.510
in our neuro circuits,

00:47:44.510 --> 00:47:47.720
that place us in a very vulnerable place,

00:47:47.720 --> 00:47:50.220
to either want more and more of that thing

00:47:50.220 --> 00:47:55.220
or to seek out other ways to
fill that kind of emptiness

00:47:55.330 --> 00:47:56.840
that we feel or that gap,

00:47:56.840 --> 00:47:57.860
like, oh, I would love more,

00:47:57.860 --> 00:47:59.630
but I'm not going to allow myself more.

00:47:59.630 --> 00:48:02.300
Now again, I'm not saying
that you shouldn't pursue

00:48:02.300 --> 00:48:03.133
pleasurable things.

00:48:03.133 --> 00:48:06.160
I mean this molecule
dopamine exists for a reason.

00:48:06.160 --> 00:48:09.290
Frankly, because of its involvement
in sex and reproduction,

00:48:09.290 --> 00:48:11.050
it's the reason we're all
here in the first place.

00:48:11.050 --> 00:48:12.150
Because last time I checked,

00:48:12.150 --> 00:48:15.680
the only way any of us got
here was one way or another,

00:48:15.680 --> 00:48:18.140
sperm met egg and there was conception.

00:48:18.140 --> 00:48:20.524
I still believe there are
no exceptions to that,

00:48:20.524 --> 00:48:22.143
that I'm aware of anyways,

00:48:23.280 --> 00:48:25.020
that is a process, or I should say,

00:48:25.020 --> 00:48:26.570
the events leading up to that process

00:48:26.570 --> 00:48:28.570
typically involve dopamine
in one way or another.

00:48:28.570 --> 00:48:31.180
There are exceptions to that
too, but you get the idea.

00:48:31.180 --> 00:48:33.860
These dopamine pathways are
not evil, they're not bad,

00:48:33.860 --> 00:48:36.450
but once you understand the way they work,

00:48:36.450 --> 00:48:39.230
you can leverage them to your advantage

00:48:39.230 --> 00:48:42.410
as opposed to them leveraging
you to their advantage.

00:48:42.410 --> 00:48:45.020
So when you ingest something sweet,

00:48:45.020 --> 00:48:48.370
you perceive that sweet
taste and a cascade ensues

00:48:48.370 --> 00:48:50.510
within your brain that makes you want more

00:48:50.510 --> 00:48:51.820
of the sweet thing.

00:48:51.820 --> 00:48:56.820
That's the conscious pathway
for sugar perception,

00:48:57.310 --> 00:48:59.590
for sweet perception.

00:48:59.590 --> 00:49:01.190
Now there's the second pathway,

00:49:01.190 --> 00:49:02.680
the second pathway is what's called

00:49:02.680 --> 00:49:06.300
the post-ingestive reinforcing
properties of sugar,

00:49:06.300 --> 00:49:09.370
which is really just a fancy
nerd speak way of saying

00:49:09.370 --> 00:49:11.890
there are events that
happen within your stomach

00:49:11.890 --> 00:49:14.170
and below your conscious detection,

00:49:14.170 --> 00:49:18.020
that are also driving you to
seek out sweet tasting things,

00:49:18.020 --> 00:49:20.120
independent of their taste,

00:49:20.120 --> 00:49:23.240
and foods that increase blood glucose,

00:49:23.240 --> 00:49:24.930
independent of their taste.

00:49:24.930 --> 00:49:26.610
In order to illustrate the immense power

00:49:26.610 --> 00:49:29.570
of these subconscious
circuits for sugar seeking,

00:49:29.570 --> 00:49:31.160
I'd like to describe an experiment.

00:49:31.160 --> 00:49:33.460
And this is just one experiment of many,

00:49:33.460 --> 00:49:36.090
of dozens or more experiments
done in animal models

00:49:36.090 --> 00:49:38.170
and humans, which essentially
illustrate the same thing.

00:49:38.170 --> 00:49:39.810
And as I describe this experiment,

00:49:39.810 --> 00:49:42.030
I think you will come to understand

00:49:42.030 --> 00:49:43.660
the power of these circuits.

00:49:43.660 --> 00:49:48.040
I'll provide a link to
this study in the caption.

00:49:48.040 --> 00:49:49.870
The first author is Freeman.

00:49:49.870 --> 00:49:51.870
The paper was published in
Frontiers in Bioscience,

00:49:51.870 --> 00:49:54.000
but there have been others,

00:49:54.000 --> 00:49:56.230
papers in Nature Neuroscience,
papers in Neuron,

00:49:56.230 --> 00:49:57.320
Cell Press journals, et cetera,

00:49:57.320 --> 00:49:59.270
many, many journals, many, many papers.

00:50:00.720 --> 00:50:04.480
If subjects are given the
choice of drinking plain water

00:50:04.480 --> 00:50:06.950
or a sweet tasting fluid,

00:50:06.950 --> 00:50:09.030
their preference for
the sweet tasting fluid

00:50:09.030 --> 00:50:11.580
is much, much higher, right?

00:50:11.580 --> 00:50:14.080
Sweet tastes better than plain water,

00:50:14.080 --> 00:50:18.570
at least for most people
and certainly for animals.

00:50:18.570 --> 00:50:21.410
Now, if, for instance,

00:50:21.410 --> 00:50:25.380
you take an animal which
completely lacks sweet receptors,

00:50:25.380 --> 00:50:28.750
and you can do this through
some molecular genetic tools

00:50:28.750 --> 00:50:30.950
and gymnastics in the laboratory,

00:50:30.950 --> 00:50:32.120
we call these knockout mice,

00:50:32.120 --> 00:50:33.680
where you can knock out
a particular receptor

00:50:33.680 --> 00:50:34.513
for sweet taste,

00:50:34.513 --> 00:50:37.370
you can confirm that there's
no perception of sweet things

00:50:37.370 --> 00:50:39.810
or at least no preference for sweet things

00:50:39.810 --> 00:50:40.643
in those animals.

00:50:40.643 --> 00:50:41.840
In humans, you can numb the mouth,

00:50:41.840 --> 00:50:44.130
there are other pharmacologic ways

00:50:44.130 --> 00:50:46.730
that you can eliminate sweet
receptors in the mouth.

00:50:47.580 --> 00:50:50.020
And by doing that, you
people will tell you,

00:50:50.020 --> 00:50:52.310
no, I can't taste anything sweet.

00:50:52.310 --> 00:50:54.050
You could give them a ice cream,

00:50:54.050 --> 00:50:55.370
you could give them pure sucrose,

00:50:55.370 --> 00:50:56.640
you could give them table sugar,

00:50:56.640 --> 00:50:59.020
and they wouldn't be able
to perceive it as sweet.

00:50:59.020 --> 00:51:01.320
If you eliminate the perception
of sweet taste in the mouth

00:51:01.320 --> 00:51:05.330
and you offer people
or laboratory animals,

00:51:05.330 --> 00:51:07.970
water versus some sugar
containing solution,

00:51:07.970 --> 00:51:12.440
you eliminate the preference
for the sugary solution.

00:51:12.440 --> 00:51:14.520
Which tells us that
the perception of sweet

00:51:14.520 --> 00:51:19.430
is important for the preference
for sweet tasting drink.

00:51:19.430 --> 00:51:23.180
This is also true for sweet
tasting foods, I should mention.

00:51:23.180 --> 00:51:27.050
However, in both animal
models and in humans,

00:51:27.050 --> 00:51:28.913
after about 15 minutes,

00:51:30.710 --> 00:51:34.190
subjects start preferring
the sugary water,

00:51:34.190 --> 00:51:37.660
even though they can't
taste that it is sweeter.

00:51:37.660 --> 00:51:39.130
So to repeat that,

00:51:39.130 --> 00:51:41.750
if you eliminate the
ability to sense sweet,

00:51:41.750 --> 00:51:44.400
to perceive sweetness in foods,

00:51:44.400 --> 00:51:45.980
then you eliminate the preference

00:51:45.980 --> 00:51:48.540
for sweet beverages or sweet foods.

00:51:48.540 --> 00:51:49.510
So that's not surprising.

00:51:49.510 --> 00:51:51.480
But if you wait about 15 minutes,

00:51:51.480 --> 00:51:54.680
the preference for the sweet beverage

00:51:54.680 --> 00:51:56.310
or the sweet food comes back.

00:51:56.310 --> 00:51:59.150
Now that doesn't mean that they
can perceive the sweetness.

00:51:59.150 --> 00:52:01.960
In fact, the way these experiments
are done is very clever.

00:52:01.960 --> 00:52:04.970
You offer people various
cups of different things

00:52:04.970 --> 00:52:06.030
or different food items.

00:52:06.030 --> 00:52:07.680
And then you just look
at what they eat more of

00:52:07.680 --> 00:52:09.130
or what they prefer to eat more of.

00:52:09.130 --> 00:52:11.710
So this experiment is so crucial

00:52:11.710 --> 00:52:14.480
because what it says
is that the preference

00:52:14.480 --> 00:52:17.990
for sugar containing foods is in part

00:52:17.990 --> 00:52:20.000
due to the sweetness of those foods,

00:52:20.000 --> 00:52:22.470
but in part due to something else.

00:52:22.470 --> 00:52:24.050
And this something else is what we call

00:52:24.050 --> 00:52:26.890
the post-ingestive effect,
and as I mentioned before,

00:52:26.890 --> 00:52:28.200
it took about 15 minutes.

00:52:28.200 --> 00:52:30.840
And you've actually experienced this,

00:52:30.840 --> 00:52:32.260
whether you realize it or not,

00:52:32.260 --> 00:52:35.450
this phenomenon of post-ingestive
rewarding properties

00:52:35.450 --> 00:52:37.030
of sweet foods,

00:52:37.030 --> 00:52:39.190
meaning what happens in your body

00:52:39.190 --> 00:52:40.740
when you ingest something that increases

00:52:40.740 --> 00:52:42.480
your blood glucose very much,

00:52:42.480 --> 00:52:46.610
has no doubt controlled
you from the inside,

00:52:46.610 --> 00:52:48.260
below your awareness,

00:52:48.260 --> 00:52:51.283
this was happening to you
and you didn't realize it.

00:52:52.220 --> 00:52:53.330
And here's how it works.

00:52:53.330 --> 00:52:55.900
We all have neurons within our gut.

00:52:55.900 --> 00:52:58.960
These neurons have a name,
they're called neuropod cells.

00:52:58.960 --> 00:53:01.220
Neuropod cells were famously discovered

00:53:01.220 --> 00:53:06.000
by Professor, Dr. Diego
Bohorquez at Duke University.

00:53:06.000 --> 00:53:09.910
And these cells respond
to, among other things,

00:53:09.910 --> 00:53:12.800
to the presence of sugar within the gut.

00:53:12.800 --> 00:53:16.170
So when we ingest a sugary food or drink,

00:53:16.170 --> 00:53:19.590
or we ingest a food or
drink that simply contains

00:53:19.590 --> 00:53:21.270
fructose, sucrose, glucose,

00:53:21.270 --> 00:53:26.270
or some other form of sugar
that later, through metabolism,

00:53:26.420 --> 00:53:28.183
will be converted into glucose,

00:53:29.180 --> 00:53:32.000
the neuropod cells are able to register

00:53:32.000 --> 00:53:37.000
the presence of those sweet
or glucose stimulating foods.

00:53:37.250 --> 00:53:41.250
And in response to that,
send electrical signals,

00:53:41.250 --> 00:53:43.570
because electrical signals are
the way neurons communicate,

00:53:43.570 --> 00:53:46.560
up to the brain, via the
so-called vagus nerve.

00:53:46.560 --> 00:53:48.720
The vagus nerve, of course,
being a nerve pathway,

00:53:48.720 --> 00:53:50.670
famous for its role in relaxation.

00:53:50.670 --> 00:53:53.080
That's kind of the assumption out there,

00:53:53.080 --> 00:53:54.440
that it's always involved in relaxation,

00:53:54.440 --> 00:53:55.350
that's not the case.

00:53:55.350 --> 00:53:57.830
It's involved in a lot of
things besides relaxation.

00:53:57.830 --> 00:53:58.740
But nonetheless,

00:53:58.740 --> 00:54:02.140
these neuropod cells
send electrical signals

00:54:02.140 --> 00:54:05.100
through a particular
highway within the vagus

00:54:05.100 --> 00:54:07.780
to the so-called nodose
ganglion, and this is a cluster,

00:54:07.780 --> 00:54:09.910
a ganglion is just a cluster of neurons.

00:54:09.910 --> 00:54:12.900
And then the nodose ganglion
sends on information

00:54:12.900 --> 00:54:16.090
to the nucleus of the solitary tract.

00:54:16.090 --> 00:54:18.470
The nucleus of the solitary
tract is an area of the brain

00:54:18.470 --> 00:54:20.660
that we're going to talk
about extensively today.

00:54:20.660 --> 00:54:23.963
It's very important for
understanding sugar preference.

00:54:25.610 --> 00:54:28.890
These neuropod cells
also trigger activation

00:54:28.890 --> 00:54:33.320
of dopamine pathways within
the mesolimbic reward pathway.

00:54:33.320 --> 00:54:36.910
In other words, there are
signals conveyed from the gut,

00:54:36.910 --> 00:54:39.800
meaning stomach and
intestines, to the brain

00:54:39.800 --> 00:54:42.730
anytime we ingest sweet foods,

00:54:42.730 --> 00:54:45.240
but it has nothing to
do with our perception

00:54:45.240 --> 00:54:47.670
of them being sweet, it has
everything to do with the fact

00:54:47.670 --> 00:54:50.730
that sweetness of food is
almost always correlated

00:54:50.730 --> 00:54:54.240
with an ability to increase blood glucose.

00:54:54.240 --> 00:54:58.160
And the net effect of
this is a parallel pathway

00:54:58.160 --> 00:55:01.240
by which dopamine is increased further.

00:55:01.240 --> 00:55:04.340
Now, the experiment
that I described before

00:55:04.340 --> 00:55:07.540
of animals or humans ingesting something

00:55:07.540 --> 00:55:08.710
that contains sugar,

00:55:08.710 --> 00:55:10.930
but not being able to
perceive its sweetness,

00:55:10.930 --> 00:55:14.100
and yet, after a period of time,

00:55:14.100 --> 00:55:16.600
still preferring that food or drink

00:55:16.600 --> 00:55:19.320
to non-sugar containing food or drinks,

00:55:19.320 --> 00:55:21.770
even though they can't
distinguish their taste,

00:55:21.770 --> 00:55:26.600
is dependent on these neuropod
cells and related pathways.

00:55:26.600 --> 00:55:28.980
What this may for you is that anytime

00:55:28.980 --> 00:55:30.643
you eat something sweet,

00:55:31.730 --> 00:55:35.080
that substance is
actually causing your gut,

00:55:35.080 --> 00:55:38.480
your stomach and your intestine,
or to be more precise,

00:55:38.480 --> 00:55:41.440
I should say, that
substance, food substance,

00:55:41.440 --> 00:55:45.220
is causing the neuropod cells
in your stomach and intestines

00:55:45.220 --> 00:55:48.890
to send a parallel set of
signals up to your brain saying,

00:55:48.890 --> 00:55:52.340
eat more of that, or simply
eat more, eat more, eat more,

00:55:52.340 --> 00:55:54.570
and preferably eat more sweet foods.

00:55:54.570 --> 00:55:56.860
So we've all heard of hidden sugars,

00:55:56.860 --> 00:55:59.710
meaning the sugars that
manufacturers have put into foods

00:55:59.710 --> 00:56:01.620
and disguised them with other flavors.

00:56:01.620 --> 00:56:03.700
I talked about this in
the episode on salt,

00:56:03.700 --> 00:56:06.060
using salt to mask the taste of sweetness

00:56:06.060 --> 00:56:08.280
so that people ingest more sugar.

00:56:08.280 --> 00:56:09.840
That is not an accident

00:56:09.840 --> 00:56:12.640
that hidden sugars are
often hidden with salt

00:56:12.640 --> 00:56:14.550
or with other flavors.

00:56:14.550 --> 00:56:17.970
It's done so that people
will, meaning you or me,

00:56:17.970 --> 00:56:21.930
will want to ingest more
of a particular food,

00:56:21.930 --> 00:56:24.800
independent of how sweet that food tastes.

00:56:24.800 --> 00:56:27.080
And in fact, some crackers, for instance,

00:56:27.080 --> 00:56:29.650
chips for instance, you might
think, oh, well, you know,

00:56:29.650 --> 00:56:32.760
chips, they're not sweet,
they're salty and savory.

00:56:32.760 --> 00:56:36.120
And again, I'll mention, I
love salty and savory foods,

00:56:36.120 --> 00:56:38.500
including certain foods, I love
kettle chips, for instance,

00:56:38.500 --> 00:56:41.330
I try not to walk by them
in the grocery store.

00:56:41.330 --> 00:56:43.230
I usually have to eat one
bag while I'm in the store,

00:56:43.230 --> 00:56:44.590
and then another later.

00:56:44.590 --> 00:56:49.590
The savory foods are often
laden with these hidden sugars

00:56:50.010 --> 00:56:51.950
that we can't register as sweetness,

00:56:51.950 --> 00:56:53.400
but trigger the neuropod cells,

00:56:53.400 --> 00:56:54.850
which then further trigger dopamine,

00:56:54.850 --> 00:56:56.690
which make us want more of them.

00:56:56.690 --> 00:56:59.350
Now we may be able to
resist eating more of them,

00:56:59.350 --> 00:57:03.610
but it makes us crave
more food in general.

00:57:03.610 --> 00:57:07.370
Now we will talk about ways
to regulate this pathway,

00:57:07.370 --> 00:57:10.750
to sort of intervene in
this subconscious pathway.

00:57:10.750 --> 00:57:14.520
But for now I'm hoping
that just the understanding

00:57:14.520 --> 00:57:15.830
that we all have this pathway,

00:57:15.830 --> 00:57:18.003
this is hardwired into our body,

00:57:19.630 --> 00:57:21.880
could potentially allow
people to better understand

00:57:21.880 --> 00:57:25.460
why is it that their
cravings are so intense,

00:57:25.460 --> 00:57:28.480
that it's not necessarily just
about the taste of that food.

00:57:28.480 --> 00:57:33.090
And when you consider this
in concert with the fact

00:57:33.090 --> 00:57:36.530
that we have this dopamine pain
pleasure balance, excuse me,

00:57:36.530 --> 00:57:38.220
that I referred to earlier,

00:57:38.220 --> 00:57:40.450
you start to realize that
there are multiple mechanisms

00:57:40.450 --> 00:57:44.590
hardwired into us, that make
it especially hard to not eat

00:57:44.590 --> 00:57:47.470
the sweet thing or to not eat
the food that we're craving.

00:57:47.470 --> 00:57:48.810
And indeed, that's the case.

00:57:48.810 --> 00:57:50.720
We have two major accelerators.

00:57:50.720 --> 00:57:52.990
It's like a car with two accelerators,

00:57:52.990 --> 00:57:54.830
and we will talk about the brakes,

00:57:54.830 --> 00:57:58.060
but two ways that really
get us into forward motion

00:57:58.060 --> 00:58:01.130
toward pursuing the
consumption of sweet foods.

00:58:01.130 --> 00:58:03.330
Now, if it doesn't already
seem diabolical enough,

00:58:03.330 --> 00:58:05.930
that sweet things that
we perceive as sweet,

00:58:05.930 --> 00:58:07.040
make us want to eat more of those

00:58:07.040 --> 00:58:08.910
because of dopamine and then send us down

00:58:08.910 --> 00:58:13.030
this pain pleasure pathway
that I mentioned earlier,

00:58:13.030 --> 00:58:16.000
and the fact that we have
this subconscious circuit

00:58:16.000 --> 00:58:18.140
coming from the neuropod cells in our gut

00:58:18.140 --> 00:58:20.710
that are registering the presence of sugar

00:58:20.710 --> 00:58:22.730
or glucose increasing foods in our gut

00:58:22.730 --> 00:58:24.917
and sending those signals to
the brain for yet more dopamine

00:58:24.917 --> 00:58:27.690
and pain pleasure challenges,

00:58:27.690 --> 00:58:30.110
there's a third layer to this whole thing.

00:58:30.110 --> 00:58:32.240
And that has to do with how sugar

00:58:32.240 --> 00:58:33.710
is metabolized in the brain.

00:58:33.710 --> 00:58:35.830
Or I should say how glucose is used.

00:58:35.830 --> 00:58:38.300
Without getting into too much detail,

00:58:38.300 --> 00:58:41.670
some of the more beautiful
studies of neuroimaging

00:58:41.670 --> 00:58:45.090
and evaluating which
brain areas are active

00:58:45.090 --> 00:58:46.340
when we eat certain foods

00:58:46.340 --> 00:58:49.830
were done by Dr. Dana Small's
lab at Yale University,

00:58:49.830 --> 00:58:53.140
and in some of her previous
work when she was elsewhere,

00:58:53.140 --> 00:58:55.170
and of course by other laboratories too.

00:58:55.170 --> 00:58:56.720
And they used an approach called

00:58:56.720 --> 00:58:58.670
positron emission tomography,

00:58:58.670 --> 00:59:02.960
and they and others have used
PET scanning as it's called,

00:59:02.960 --> 00:59:04.373
positron mission tomography,

00:59:05.540 --> 00:59:08.480
along with an tool
called 2-Deoxy-D-glucose.

00:59:08.480 --> 00:59:11.520
2-Deoxy-D-glucose is actually
involved in the procedure

00:59:11.520 --> 00:59:13.490
of seeing which brain areas are active

00:59:13.490 --> 00:59:16.520
when people eat foods or engage
in other types of behaviors.

00:59:16.520 --> 00:59:19.330
But the way that 2-Deoxy-D-glucose,

00:59:19.330 --> 00:59:21.330
sometimes shortened 2DG.

00:59:21.330 --> 00:59:25.550
The way that it works is
to block glucose uptake

00:59:25.550 --> 00:59:26.930
from neurons.

00:59:26.930 --> 00:59:29.700
And instead, bring along with it,

00:59:29.700 --> 00:59:32.430
a marker that one can see through imaging.

00:59:32.430 --> 00:59:33.780
So in other words,

00:59:33.780 --> 00:59:37.200
a tool for looking at what
parts of the brain are active

00:59:37.200 --> 00:59:39.120
when eating particular foods,

00:59:39.120 --> 00:59:42.600
actually prevents foods such as sugar,

00:59:42.600 --> 00:59:46.950
from allowing glucose to
get into particular neurons.

00:59:46.950 --> 00:59:49.010
Now that might seem like a bad situation,

00:59:49.010 --> 00:59:49.843
you'd say, well, wait,

00:59:49.843 --> 00:59:52.530
you're trying to understand
how sugar works in the brain,

00:59:52.530 --> 00:59:56.440
and then you block the
ability for sugar, glucose,

00:59:56.440 --> 00:59:59.750
to bind to, or be used by these neurons,

00:59:59.750 --> 01:00:01.540
because of the thing that
you're using for the experiment.

01:00:01.540 --> 01:00:03.410
Exactly, it's a huge problem,

01:00:03.410 --> 01:00:04.670
but it turns out to be a huge problem

01:00:04.670 --> 01:00:06.110
that led to a great insight.

01:00:06.110 --> 01:00:08.500
And the great insight is this,

01:00:08.500 --> 01:00:11.970
the preference for sweet
tasting foods and liquids

01:00:11.970 --> 01:00:16.970
is actually blocked by 2-Deoxy-D-glucose.

01:00:17.520 --> 01:00:19.280
What that means, experimentally,

01:00:19.280 --> 01:00:21.550
but also in terms of what
it means for you and me

01:00:21.550 --> 01:00:23.890
in the real world, is that there's yet

01:00:23.890 --> 01:00:26.140
a third parallel pathway

01:00:26.980 --> 01:00:30.990
that's related to the use of blood sugar,

01:00:30.990 --> 01:00:34.100
the use of glucose by neurons,

01:00:34.100 --> 01:00:38.810
that further reinforces our
desire to eat more sweet things.

01:00:38.810 --> 01:00:40.900
And the preference for sweet foods

01:00:40.900 --> 01:00:45.430
can actually be eliminated
through 2-Deoxy-D-glucose.

01:00:45.430 --> 01:00:48.420
Now I am definitely don't
want people going out

01:00:48.420 --> 01:00:49.840
and consuming 2-Deoxy-D-glucose.

01:00:49.840 --> 01:00:51.070
This is a laboratory tool,

01:00:51.070 --> 01:00:54.050
it is not something that
you should be ingesting.

01:00:54.050 --> 01:00:56.150
So don't go look it up
and try and get some,

01:00:56.150 --> 01:00:58.190
there might be other uses for
it, but that's not the point.

01:00:58.190 --> 01:01:03.190
The point is that it is the
sweet taste of sugary foods.

01:01:03.210 --> 01:01:06.870
It is the signals coming from your gut,

01:01:06.870 --> 01:01:09.470
from your digestive tract to your brain,

01:01:09.470 --> 01:01:13.890
and it's the use of the
metabolic consequences

01:01:13.890 --> 01:01:18.620
of sugary foods that are
acting as a three pronged push

01:01:18.620 --> 01:01:21.840
on your desire to consume
more sugary foods.

01:01:21.840 --> 01:01:24.780
So this car analogy that I used before,

01:01:24.780 --> 01:01:27.060
where it's some weird car
that has two accelerators,

01:01:27.060 --> 01:01:29.380
it actually has three accelerators.

01:01:29.380 --> 01:01:33.750
And so with three accelerators,
all pushing the system hard,

01:01:33.750 --> 01:01:34.820
we can say, wow,

01:01:34.820 --> 01:01:37.570
there must be something really
special about this pathway.

01:01:37.570 --> 01:01:38.700
And indeed there is,

01:01:38.700 --> 01:01:41.700
this pathway is the quickest
source of fuel for the brain

01:01:41.700 --> 01:01:42.750
and the rest of the nervous system,

01:01:42.750 --> 01:01:45.950
it's the source of fuel for
the brain and nervous system.

01:01:45.950 --> 01:01:48.630
And I realize, as I say
that all the ketonistas

01:01:48.630 --> 01:01:49.540
are probably going no,

01:01:49.540 --> 01:01:51.370
actually ketones are the preferred source.

01:01:51.370 --> 01:01:53.950
Okay, i acknowledge that
there are conditions

01:01:53.950 --> 01:01:56.130
under which you can bring
your blood glucose very low,

01:01:56.130 --> 01:01:57.320
and there are reasons to do that.

01:01:57.320 --> 01:01:59.410
Actually ketosis has been a

01:01:59.410 --> 01:02:01.280
terrifically successful treatment

01:02:01.280 --> 01:02:02.720
for a lot of forms of epilepsy,

01:02:02.720 --> 01:02:04.420
in particular, pediatric epilepsy.

01:02:06.190 --> 01:02:08.560
Many people do derive
benefit from ketogenic diets,

01:02:08.560 --> 01:02:10.690
so I'm not knocking ketogenic diets,

01:02:10.690 --> 01:02:14.540
but if you were to look at
what neurons normally prefer,

01:02:14.540 --> 01:02:19.540
meaning in a typical diet
regimen, it would be glucose.

01:02:19.950 --> 01:02:22.260
And the fact that fructose

01:02:22.260 --> 01:02:23.970
is eventually converted to glucose,

01:02:23.970 --> 01:02:25.740
the fact that when we ingest sucrose,

01:02:25.740 --> 01:02:27.880
it's eventually converted into
a fuel that neurons can use,

01:02:27.880 --> 01:02:30.070
that's very much in the glucose pathway,

01:02:30.070 --> 01:02:32.000
what you basically arrive at is the fact

01:02:32.000 --> 01:02:35.000
that your nervous system is
a glucose consuming machine.

01:02:35.000 --> 01:02:37.120
And you've got at least three pathways,

01:02:37.120 --> 01:02:38.570
of which I've described,

01:02:38.570 --> 01:02:41.150
that are pushing on your brain consciously

01:02:41.150 --> 01:02:44.060
and subconsciously to get
you to seek and consume

01:02:44.060 --> 01:02:45.190
more sugar.

01:02:45.190 --> 01:02:48.080
Now that all sounds like a
pretty depressing picture,

01:02:48.080 --> 01:02:49.560
at least for those of you
that are trying to reduce

01:02:49.560 --> 01:02:50.393
your sugar intake.

01:02:50.393 --> 01:02:53.110
And of course we can
all reduce sugar intake

01:02:53.110 --> 01:02:54.430
by way of sheer will,

01:02:54.430 --> 01:02:56.480
we can not have those foods at home,

01:02:56.480 --> 01:02:58.400
we can restrict ourselves from those.

01:02:58.400 --> 01:03:01.330
But there are some things that we all can

01:03:01.330 --> 01:03:05.020
and perhaps should do in order
to regulate these pathways

01:03:05.020 --> 01:03:08.410
such that we don't feel
so controlled by them,

01:03:08.410 --> 01:03:11.360
but rather that we control their output.

01:03:11.360 --> 01:03:12.960
And of course they are us and we are them,

01:03:12.960 --> 01:03:15.610
so this gets into all sorts
of issues of consciousness

01:03:15.610 --> 01:03:17.590
and freewill that I
certainly don't want to cover

01:03:17.590 --> 01:03:18.900
in this episode.

01:03:18.900 --> 01:03:22.770
But nonetheless, I think
once you understand

01:03:22.770 --> 01:03:24.610
that these circuits exist

01:03:24.610 --> 01:03:28.130
and you understand that there
are simple substitutions

01:03:28.130 --> 01:03:31.570
and modifications that one
can make to their food intake

01:03:31.570 --> 01:03:34.820
that can work within these pathways

01:03:34.820 --> 01:03:37.170
and even bypass some of these pathways,

01:03:37.170 --> 01:03:39.420
you start to realize that
you have a lot more control

01:03:39.420 --> 01:03:42.503
over sugar intake and sugar appetite

01:03:42.503 --> 01:03:44.160
than you previously thought.

01:03:44.160 --> 01:03:46.570
Now, many of you have heard of
the so-called glycemic index.

01:03:46.570 --> 01:03:48.440
The glycemic index is a measure of

01:03:48.440 --> 01:03:50.000
how quickly blood sugar rises

01:03:50.000 --> 01:03:51.970
after ingesting particular foods.

01:03:51.970 --> 01:03:53.980
And very broadly speaking,

01:03:53.980 --> 01:03:56.980
we can say that there are
low glycemic index foods,

01:03:56.980 --> 01:04:00.080
of less than 55, typically,
is the measurement,

01:04:00.080 --> 01:04:01.940
or medium glycemic index foods,

01:04:01.940 --> 01:04:04.020
which go from about 55 to 69,

01:04:04.020 --> 01:04:06.890
and then so called high glycemic
foods, which are above 70.

01:04:06.890 --> 01:04:08.920
And of course there's additional nuance

01:04:08.920 --> 01:04:10.930
related to glycemic load

01:04:10.930 --> 01:04:14.980
and many more features
of the glycemic index.

01:04:14.980 --> 01:04:16.220
A couple of things to understand

01:04:16.220 --> 01:04:18.770
about how the glycemic index is measured.

01:04:18.770 --> 01:04:21.050
And then I'd like to
just briefly talk about

01:04:21.050 --> 01:04:25.550
how the glycemic index can
be leveraged to short circuit

01:04:25.550 --> 01:04:28.730
some of the neural circuits
that would otherwise lead us

01:04:28.730 --> 01:04:33.120
to crave and perhaps
even ingest sugar foods.

01:04:33.120 --> 01:04:37.760
First of all, measurements
of glycemic indices of food

01:04:37.760 --> 01:04:40.510
are typically made by having
people ingest those foods

01:04:40.510 --> 01:04:41.800
in isolation.

01:04:41.800 --> 01:04:46.450
And in general, we can say
that anytime we ingest fiber

01:04:46.450 --> 01:04:49.870
and/or fat, lipids, along
with a particular food,

01:04:49.870 --> 01:04:53.870
it will reduce the glycemic
index of that particular food,

01:04:53.870 --> 01:04:57.390
either the absolute level of blood glucose

01:04:57.390 --> 01:05:00.160
that a particular food causes

01:05:00.160 --> 01:05:05.090
or the rate at which that
elevation in blood glucose occurs.

01:05:05.090 --> 01:05:09.280
And this is why there are some
seemingly paradoxical aspects

01:05:09.280 --> 01:05:11.450
to sweet stuff in terms
of the glycemic index.

01:05:11.450 --> 01:05:14.520
For instance, Ice cream
has a lower glycemic index,

01:05:14.520 --> 01:05:16.480
provided its ice cream that includes fat,

01:05:16.480 --> 01:05:17.313
which I hope it would,

01:05:17.313 --> 01:05:18.380
'cause that's the good tasting ice cream,

01:05:18.380 --> 01:05:19.430
in my opinion,

01:05:19.430 --> 01:05:23.150
compared to something like
mangoes or table sugar, right?

01:05:23.150 --> 01:05:27.210
So the glycemic index is
not something to hold holy,

01:05:27.210 --> 01:05:28.100
in most cases,

01:05:28.100 --> 01:05:31.040
because most people are not
ingesting foods in isolation.

01:05:31.040 --> 01:05:32.520
And there's actually a lot of argument

01:05:32.520 --> 01:05:33.960
as to whether or not the glycemic index

01:05:33.960 --> 01:05:38.210
is really as vital as some people claim.

01:05:38.210 --> 01:05:39.410
There's also the context

01:05:39.410 --> 01:05:41.220
in which you ingest particular foods.

01:05:41.220 --> 01:05:42.680
As I mentioned earlier,

01:05:42.680 --> 01:05:45.080
after I do hard training of any kind,

01:05:45.080 --> 01:05:46.900
meaning training that
ought to deplete glycogen,

01:05:46.900 --> 01:05:48.090
so hard resistance training,

01:05:48.090 --> 01:05:50.160
I actually make it a point to ingest

01:05:50.160 --> 01:05:53.300
some very sweet high
glycemic foods like a mango,

01:05:53.300 --> 01:05:54.730
I'll also ingest some starches

01:05:54.730 --> 01:05:56.120
'cause I'm trying to replenish glycogen.

01:05:56.120 --> 01:05:58.790
I'm also trying to spike
my blood sugar a little bit

01:05:58.790 --> 01:06:00.070
because that can be advantageous

01:06:00.070 --> 01:06:02.450
in terms of certain strength
and hypertrophy protocols,

01:06:02.450 --> 01:06:03.283
et cetera.

01:06:03.283 --> 01:06:05.247
But most of the time I'm avoiding
these high glycemic foods

01:06:05.247 --> 01:06:07.590
and high sugar foods, I
should point that out.

01:06:07.590 --> 01:06:09.710
Now, why am I telling you
about the glycemic index?

01:06:09.710 --> 01:06:13.310
Well, if we zoom out
and take our perspective

01:06:13.310 --> 01:06:15.680
on all of this discussion
about the glycemic index,

01:06:15.680 --> 01:06:18.090
through the lens of the nervous system,

01:06:18.090 --> 01:06:20.380
and we remind ourselves that neurons

01:06:20.380 --> 01:06:24.240
prefer glucose for energy
and that all sweet things,

01:06:24.240 --> 01:06:25.650
or things that we perceive as sweet,

01:06:25.650 --> 01:06:28.470
but also sweet things that
are ingested and registered

01:06:28.470 --> 01:06:30.540
by those neuropod cells in our gut

01:06:30.540 --> 01:06:32.450
trigger the release of dopamine

01:06:32.450 --> 01:06:34.390
and trigger these neural circuits

01:06:34.390 --> 01:06:37.010
to make us want to eat
more of these foods,

01:06:37.010 --> 01:06:40.040
what we start to realize
is that a sharp rise

01:06:40.040 --> 01:06:43.860
in blood glucose or a very
high degree of elevation

01:06:43.860 --> 01:06:44.980
in blood glucose,

01:06:44.980 --> 01:06:47.900
is going to be a much more potent signal

01:06:47.900 --> 01:06:51.630
than would a more moderate
rise in blood glucose

01:06:51.630 --> 01:06:54.890
or a slower rise in blood glucose.

01:06:54.890 --> 01:06:58.230
So if we think about the
analogy of three accelerators,

01:06:58.230 --> 01:07:00.130
meaning three parallel neural circuits,

01:07:00.130 --> 01:07:03.270
all essentially there
to get us to seek out

01:07:03.270 --> 01:07:07.260
and consume more sweet
tasting and sugary foods,

01:07:07.260 --> 01:07:11.010
well then the glycemic index
is sort of our measurement

01:07:11.010 --> 01:07:12.950
of how hard we are pushing down

01:07:12.950 --> 01:07:16.500
or how fast we are pushing down
on those three accelerators.

01:07:16.500 --> 01:07:18.200
And so for those of you that are trying to

01:07:18.200 --> 01:07:19.550
reduce sugar take,

01:07:19.550 --> 01:07:22.040
and you want to do that
through an understanding

01:07:22.040 --> 01:07:23.560
of how these neural circuits work,

01:07:23.560 --> 01:07:26.620
and you want to short circuit
some of the dopamine release

01:07:26.620 --> 01:07:30.130
that's caused by ingesting sugary foods.

01:07:30.130 --> 01:07:35.130
It can be advantageous
to ingest sweet foods,

01:07:35.350 --> 01:07:38.960
either alone or in combination with foods

01:07:38.960 --> 01:07:42.900
that reduce glycemic index
or reduce glycemic load.

01:07:42.900 --> 01:07:46.020
So that might mean making
different food choices.

01:07:46.020 --> 01:07:48.810
So paying attention to sweet tasting foods

01:07:48.810 --> 01:07:50.840
that can satisfy sugar cravings,

01:07:50.840 --> 01:07:53.510
but do not have as steep or I should say,

01:07:53.510 --> 01:07:56.040
do not cause as steep
a rise in blood sugar,

01:07:56.040 --> 01:07:59.260
or it could mean consuming other foods

01:07:59.260 --> 01:08:00.880
along with sweet foods

01:08:00.880 --> 01:08:03.240
in order to reduce the glycemic index

01:08:03.240 --> 01:08:07.360
and thereby, slow or blunt
the release of dopamine.

01:08:07.360 --> 01:08:09.310
You might think, well, why
would I want to do that?

01:08:09.310 --> 01:08:11.680
I want the maximum dopamine output

01:08:11.680 --> 01:08:13.310
in response to a given sweet food.

01:08:13.310 --> 01:08:15.470
I don't just want the you know, level 10.

01:08:15.470 --> 01:08:18.080
I want the level 100 output of dopamine.

01:08:18.080 --> 01:08:19.130
But you really don't,

01:08:19.130 --> 01:08:21.690
because of the pleasure pain
balance that dopamine causes.

01:08:21.690 --> 01:08:24.980
And in fact, if we consider
some of the non-food substances

01:08:24.980 --> 01:08:27.310
that really push hard on
these dopamine pathways,

01:08:27.310 --> 01:08:29.880
we can come up with a somewhat sinister,

01:08:29.880 --> 01:08:31.863
but nonetheless appropriate analogy.

01:08:33.250 --> 01:08:37.570
The drug, cocaine, causes
very robust, potent increases

01:08:37.570 --> 01:08:39.530
in dopamine within the brain,

01:08:39.530 --> 01:08:42.890
and typically causes people
to want to ingest more cocaine

01:08:42.890 --> 01:08:47.280
because of those sharp
increases in dopamine.

01:08:47.280 --> 01:08:50.140
But within the category
of the drug cocaine,

01:08:50.140 --> 01:08:52.120
there are various modes of ingestion.

01:08:52.120 --> 01:08:53.730
Some people inhale it,

01:08:53.730 --> 01:08:57.520
some people will inject it intravenously,

01:08:57.520 --> 01:08:58.910
some people will smoke it,

01:08:58.910 --> 01:09:02.130
and those different
forms of taking cocaine

01:09:02.130 --> 01:09:04.950
actually impact the dopamine
circuits differently.

01:09:04.950 --> 01:09:07.670
And it turns out that crack cocaine,

01:09:07.670 --> 01:09:11.270
the smokable form of cocaine rock,

01:09:11.270 --> 01:09:13.930
increases dopamine to a very high degree,

01:09:13.930 --> 01:09:16.260
but also very quickly.

01:09:16.260 --> 01:09:20.560
And it is the sharp rise
in dopamine over time,

01:09:20.560 --> 01:09:23.400
not so much the absolute
level of dopamine,

01:09:23.400 --> 01:09:27.450
that makes crack cocaine
so absolutely addictive.

01:09:27.450 --> 01:09:28.900
So sometimes you'll hear, you know,

01:09:28.900 --> 01:09:30.400
sugar is like crack,

01:09:30.400 --> 01:09:32.140
well, and that's getting a little extreme

01:09:32.140 --> 01:09:34.050
because even though I don't
think the measurements

01:09:34.050 --> 01:09:36.100
have been done in the same experiment,

01:09:36.100 --> 01:09:37.660
I think it's reasonable to think

01:09:37.660 --> 01:09:40.850
that the absolute level of dopamine caused

01:09:40.850 --> 01:09:42.850
by ingesting sugar, at
least for most people,

01:09:42.850 --> 01:09:46.090
is not going to be as high as
the absolute level of dopamine

01:09:46.090 --> 01:09:48.390
caused by smoking crack.

01:09:48.390 --> 01:09:49.500
Of course it goes without saying,

01:09:49.500 --> 01:09:52.163
please don't do cocaine
in any form, by the way.

01:09:53.700 --> 01:09:58.700
It is appropriate to say that
the rate of dopamine increase

01:09:58.870 --> 01:10:03.870
over time has a profound
effect on how people will,

01:10:03.950 --> 01:10:05.750
and if people will,

01:10:05.750 --> 01:10:08.460
go on to want to pursue
more of what caused

01:10:08.460 --> 01:10:10.160
that increase in dopamine.

01:10:10.160 --> 01:10:13.150
So what I'm basically saying
is if you're going to ingest

01:10:13.150 --> 01:10:17.083
sweet foods in order to
satisfy a sweet craving,

01:10:18.400 --> 01:10:22.330
ingesting sweet foods for which
the glycemic index is lower

01:10:22.330 --> 01:10:26.060
or in which you've adjusted
those glycemic index foods

01:10:26.060 --> 01:10:29.253
through the co-ingestion
of fiber, or maybe fat,

01:10:30.090 --> 01:10:31.610
might be beneficial.

01:10:31.610 --> 01:10:34.510
So is this justification
for putting peanut butter

01:10:34.510 --> 01:10:35.880
on that piece of chocolate

01:10:35.880 --> 01:10:39.800
or for having a of ice
cream along with the mango

01:10:39.800 --> 01:10:41.200
that you're craving?

01:10:41.200 --> 01:10:44.230
In some sense, yes, however,

01:10:44.230 --> 01:10:47.210
there's also the issue of
how sweet and how delicious

01:10:47.210 --> 01:10:48.320
something tastes.

01:10:48.320 --> 01:10:51.730
Highly palatable foods,
absolutely delicious foods,

01:10:51.730 --> 01:10:53.910
trigger that one neural circuit,

01:10:53.910 --> 01:10:56.410
that one accelerator that
we were talking about

01:10:56.410 --> 01:10:58.950
in terms of our analogy
of three accelerators,

01:10:58.950 --> 01:11:01.850
and the more delicious something
tastes within our mouth,

01:11:01.850 --> 01:11:04.660
the further increase in dopamine.

01:11:04.660 --> 01:11:07.900
So if you really wanted to
adjust your sugar cravings

01:11:07.900 --> 01:11:12.620
and you really still want
to ingest some sugary foods,

01:11:12.620 --> 01:11:15.550
you probably would better
off combining fiber

01:11:15.550 --> 01:11:17.990
with that sugary or sweet food.

01:11:17.990 --> 01:11:19.940
Now I do realize that
it's somewhat unusual

01:11:19.940 --> 01:11:22.280
and you probably get some strange stares,

01:11:22.280 --> 01:11:24.540
if you decided to consume broccoli,

01:11:24.540 --> 01:11:26.360
for instance, along with your chocolate,

01:11:26.360 --> 01:11:28.450
or with a another dessert
that would otherwise

01:11:28.450 --> 01:11:31.000
cause a steep increase in blood sugar

01:11:31.000 --> 01:11:32.690
and has a high glycemic index.

01:11:32.690 --> 01:11:35.920
But nonetheless, if your goal is to blunt

01:11:35.920 --> 01:11:37.330
your sugar cravings,

01:11:37.330 --> 01:11:40.350
what you really need to do is
blunt that dopamine increase.

01:11:40.350 --> 01:11:42.290
So what we're really talking about here

01:11:42.290 --> 01:11:45.140
is trying to reduce the dopamine signal

01:11:45.140 --> 01:11:47.750
that is the consequence
of ingesting sweet foods.

01:11:47.750 --> 01:11:49.080
And we're talking about doing that

01:11:49.080 --> 01:11:50.860
through these different parallel pathways,

01:11:50.860 --> 01:11:52.960
not just by preventing sweet taste,

01:11:52.960 --> 01:11:55.790
but also by preventing
the post-ingestive effects

01:11:55.790 --> 01:11:57.760
of sweet foods.

01:11:57.760 --> 01:12:00.660
And of course the backdrop to all of this

01:12:00.660 --> 01:12:03.300
is that most of us, again,
most of us, not all of us,

01:12:03.300 --> 01:12:06.793
should probably be ingesting
fewer refined sugars.

01:12:07.660 --> 01:12:09.650
Certainly there are exceptions to that.

01:12:09.650 --> 01:12:13.070
But I think the bulk of
data point to the fact

01:12:13.070 --> 01:12:15.580
that ingesting these highly palatable,

01:12:15.580 --> 01:12:17.890
certainly highly palatable,
highly processed foods,

01:12:17.890 --> 01:12:21.350
or foods that contain a lot
of high fructose corn syrup,

01:12:21.350 --> 01:12:23.050
can be really deleterious to our health,

01:12:23.050 --> 01:12:24.790
especially in kids.

01:12:24.790 --> 01:12:27.610
And I'm not going to cite
off a bunch of statistics,

01:12:27.610 --> 01:12:28.640
you've all heard them before,

01:12:28.640 --> 01:12:32.840
that you know, for hundreds of
years, we ingested, you know,

01:12:32.840 --> 01:12:36.320
the equivalent of a few cups
or pounds of sugar per year.

01:12:36.320 --> 01:12:37.980
And you know, now people are ingesting

01:12:37.980 --> 01:12:40.340
hundreds of pounds of sugar per year.

01:12:40.340 --> 01:12:43.720
The major culprit always
seems to be sugary drinks,

01:12:43.720 --> 01:12:46.520
meaning soft drinks, and I
think indeed that's the case.

01:12:47.640 --> 01:12:49.680
I do want to point out the incredible work

01:12:49.680 --> 01:12:50.910
of Dr. Robert Lustig,

01:12:50.910 --> 01:12:52.840
who's a pediatric endocrinologist

01:12:52.840 --> 01:12:54.900
at the University of
California San Francisco,

01:12:54.900 --> 01:12:57.895
who was really early in the
game of voicing the dangers

01:12:57.895 --> 01:13:00.550
of so-called hidden sugars
and highly processed foods,

01:13:00.550 --> 01:13:03.700
there are other people, of
course, now talking about this.

01:13:03.700 --> 01:13:07.210
His laboratory has done important
work showing for instance,

01:13:07.210 --> 01:13:10.140
that if high fructose corn syrup

01:13:10.140 --> 01:13:13.140
or even just fructose is
replaced with glucose,

01:13:13.140 --> 01:13:16.690
even if the same number
calories is ingested,

01:13:16.690 --> 01:13:20.910
that there are important
meaning significant reductions

01:13:20.910 --> 01:13:23.370
in type two diabetes,

01:13:23.370 --> 01:13:24.660
some of the metabolic syndromes

01:13:24.660 --> 01:13:26.347
associated with high fructose corn syrup

01:13:26.347 --> 01:13:28.020
and on and on and on.

01:13:28.020 --> 01:13:30.680
And of course there are other
culprits in type two diabetes,

01:13:30.680 --> 01:13:33.900
there are other factors that
are going to lead to obesity.

01:13:33.900 --> 01:13:36.440
But I think that work
from Lustig and others

01:13:36.440 --> 01:13:39.210
has really illustrated that
we should all be trying

01:13:39.210 --> 01:13:42.430
to reduce our intake of
highly refined sugars

01:13:42.430 --> 01:13:44.280
and high fructose corn syrup.

01:13:44.280 --> 01:13:46.660
And certainly trying to reduce our intake

01:13:46.660 --> 01:13:51.280
of very sugary drinks,
not just soft drinks,

01:13:51.280 --> 01:13:53.950
but also fruit juices that
contain a lot of sugar.

01:13:53.950 --> 01:13:56.090
Now, even for people that
are of healthy weight

01:13:56.090 --> 01:13:58.360
and who don't have metabolic syndromes,

01:13:58.360 --> 01:14:00.200
there may be an additional reason

01:14:00.200 --> 01:14:03.670
to not want to ingest very sweet foods

01:14:03.670 --> 01:14:05.200
and highly refined sugars.

01:14:05.200 --> 01:14:08.220
And this has to do with
a new and emerging area

01:14:08.220 --> 01:14:11.053
of nutrition neuroscience.

01:14:11.950 --> 01:14:14.700
And I want to point out that
these are new data, right,

01:14:14.700 --> 01:14:15.670
so it's not a lock,

01:14:15.670 --> 01:14:17.610
the double blind placebo
controlled studies

01:14:17.610 --> 01:14:20.320
in large populations
have not been finished.

01:14:20.320 --> 01:14:23.420
So I want to make sure that that's clear,

01:14:23.420 --> 01:14:25.370
but I also want to make clear

01:14:25.370 --> 01:14:27.380
what some of the really exciting data,

01:14:27.380 --> 01:14:29.750
coming from Dana Small's lab at Yale,

01:14:29.750 --> 01:14:32.030
and from other laboratories are showing.

01:14:32.030 --> 01:14:33.920
And this has to do with what's called

01:14:33.920 --> 01:14:36.070
conditioned taste preference.

01:14:36.070 --> 01:14:39.670
Using a kind of Pavlovian
paradigm, what they do,

01:14:39.670 --> 01:14:42.790
is they have people, and these
studies were done in people,

01:14:42.790 --> 01:14:45.960
ingest maltodextrin, which
increases blood glucose,

01:14:45.960 --> 01:14:47.000
doesn't have much flavor,

01:14:47.000 --> 01:14:49.800
but even if it does have a
little bit of subtle flavor,

01:14:49.800 --> 01:14:53.670
the maltodextrin is cloaked
by some other flavor.

01:14:53.670 --> 01:14:56.330
And by cloaking it with that other flavor

01:14:56.330 --> 01:14:57.950
or pairing it with that other flavor,

01:14:57.950 --> 01:14:59.880
what they find is that over time,

01:14:59.880 --> 01:15:02.360
because the maltodextrin
increases blood glucose,

01:15:02.360 --> 01:15:04.060
and they're ingesting a particular flavor

01:15:04.060 --> 01:15:06.050
along with that maltodextrin,

01:15:06.050 --> 01:15:08.193
they can then remove the maltodextrin,

01:15:09.300 --> 01:15:13.850
and the flavor will induce
an increase in insulin.

01:15:13.850 --> 01:15:15.350
The increase in insulin of course,

01:15:15.350 --> 01:15:17.590
is the consequence of
the fact that anytime

01:15:17.590 --> 01:15:18.780
there's a rise in blood glucose,

01:15:18.780 --> 01:15:20.420
provided the person isn't diabetic,

01:15:20.420 --> 01:15:25.260
there's a parallel increase in insulin.

01:15:25.260 --> 01:15:28.470
Now this is very interesting
because what it says is,

01:15:28.470 --> 01:15:31.620
well at a first pass, it says
that we are very Pavlovian

01:15:31.620 --> 01:15:34.900
in terms of our physiological
responses to foods

01:15:34.900 --> 01:15:37.660
and particular flavors
come to be associated

01:15:37.660 --> 01:15:41.270
with particular patterns
of blood glucose increase

01:15:41.270 --> 01:15:44.294
and hence patterns of insulin increase,

01:15:44.294 --> 01:15:45.127
because of course,

01:15:45.127 --> 01:15:47.290
insulin manages glucose in the bloodstream

01:15:47.290 --> 01:15:48.453
as I mentioned earlier.

01:15:49.520 --> 01:15:52.440
This also has implications
for understanding

01:15:52.440 --> 01:15:53.900
things like artificial sweeteners.

01:15:53.900 --> 01:15:55.830
And here I want to
highlight that this is still

01:15:55.830 --> 01:15:58.930
very controversial work, needs
more data, but nonetheless,

01:15:58.930 --> 01:16:01.330
I'd like to share it with
you for consideration.

01:16:03.080 --> 01:16:06.660
The small laboratory has
done studies in humans,

01:16:06.660 --> 01:16:08.660
both in adults and in children,

01:16:08.660 --> 01:16:13.070
showing that if the flavor
of artificial sweeteners

01:16:13.070 --> 01:16:14.850
is paired with maltodextrin,

01:16:14.850 --> 01:16:17.450
and then the maltodextrin is removed,

01:16:17.450 --> 01:16:20.920
that the artificial sweetener taste itself

01:16:20.920 --> 01:16:24.470
can subsequently increase
insulin in the bloodstream.

01:16:24.470 --> 01:16:29.030
In other words, taking something
that increases blood sugar,

01:16:29.030 --> 01:16:31.900
attaching a flavor experience to that,

01:16:31.900 --> 01:16:35.283
having children or
adults ingest that thing,

01:16:36.150 --> 01:16:39.720
allows the nervous system
to associate that flavor

01:16:39.720 --> 01:16:41.570
with that increase in blood glucose.

01:16:41.570 --> 01:16:45.200
But then you can remove the
glucose increasing substance,

01:16:45.200 --> 01:16:47.630
and the flavor alone will increase insulin

01:16:47.630 --> 01:16:50.210
because insulin typically
follows blood glucose.

01:16:50.210 --> 01:16:52.050
So this is a conditioning effect.

01:16:52.050 --> 01:16:54.010
Now, the reason these
data are controversial

01:16:54.010 --> 01:16:54.843
is several fold.

01:16:54.843 --> 01:16:59.150
First of all, the landscape
around the discussion

01:16:59.150 --> 01:17:03.160
around artificial sweeteners
is definitely what I would call

01:17:03.160 --> 01:17:04.690
a barbed wire topic.

01:17:04.690 --> 01:17:07.050
And I want to preface what I'm
about to say next by saying,

01:17:07.050 --> 01:17:09.260
I actually ingest artificial sweeteners.

01:17:09.260 --> 01:17:12.320
I will have the occasional
diet soda, not every day,

01:17:12.320 --> 01:17:14.000
maybe I don't know, once or twice a month.

01:17:14.000 --> 01:17:15.110
I don't particularly like the taste,

01:17:15.110 --> 01:17:16.540
but I'll do it just 'cause it's around,

01:17:16.540 --> 01:17:18.920
and I want some caffeine
and I like the carbonation

01:17:18.920 --> 01:17:20.600
if I'm on a plane or something,

01:17:20.600 --> 01:17:24.520
I do ingest plant-based
non-caloric sweeteners.

01:17:24.520 --> 01:17:27.500
To my knowledge, there have
not been high quality studies

01:17:27.500 --> 01:17:29.540
of plant-based non-caloric sweeteners

01:17:29.540 --> 01:17:31.640
in the context that I'm referring to here.

01:17:32.490 --> 01:17:35.500
Nonetheless, these studies
show that particular flavors

01:17:35.500 --> 01:17:38.840
can be conditioned to
cause an insulin increase.

01:17:38.840 --> 01:17:42.660
And the flavor associated with
certain artificial sweeteners

01:17:43.810 --> 01:17:46.200
is included in that category of flavors

01:17:46.200 --> 01:17:47.760
that can induce insulin,

01:17:47.760 --> 01:17:49.133
even in the absence of something

01:17:49.133 --> 01:17:50.690
that can increase blood glucose.

01:17:50.690 --> 01:17:54.298
Now the simple takeaway from these studies

01:17:54.298 --> 01:17:56.590
would be the following,

01:17:56.590 --> 01:17:59.810
and this is actually one
interpretation that Dana Small

01:17:59.810 --> 01:18:00.920
has offered to her data,

01:18:00.920 --> 01:18:02.960
but she offers other
interpretations as well.

01:18:02.960 --> 01:18:05.800
One of interpretation is that if people

01:18:05.800 --> 01:18:08.490
are going to ingest artificial sweeteners,

01:18:08.490 --> 01:18:10.720
and they do that along with foods

01:18:10.720 --> 01:18:14.130
that very sharply increase blood glucose,

01:18:14.130 --> 01:18:17.770
then there is the potential,
I highlight the potential,

01:18:17.770 --> 01:18:21.940
for those same artificial
sweeteners to increase insulin,

01:18:21.940 --> 01:18:24.760
even in the absence of food.

01:18:24.760 --> 01:18:26.630
In other words, let's
just draw the scenario out

01:18:26.630 --> 01:18:27.530
in the real world.

01:18:28.870 --> 01:18:31.810
You're having a diet soda

01:18:31.810 --> 01:18:35.260
along with a cheeseburger and fries.

01:18:35.260 --> 01:18:37.920
You do that every day for lunch, okay?

01:18:37.920 --> 01:18:39.970
Somewhat extreme example,
but natural world example,

01:18:39.970 --> 01:18:41.090
you do that every day for lunch.

01:18:41.090 --> 01:18:43.543
And then you just have a diet soda alone.

01:18:44.570 --> 01:18:47.700
The extreme interpretation of the data

01:18:47.700 --> 01:18:49.120
that they've collected says,

01:18:49.120 --> 01:18:51.460
well, that diet soda alone
will increase insulin,

01:18:51.460 --> 01:18:53.890
even though there's no
increase in blood glucose

01:18:53.890 --> 01:18:55.070
because you haven't ingested food with it

01:18:55.070 --> 01:18:58.620
because you conditioned that
taste of artificial sweetener

01:18:58.620 --> 01:19:02.240
to the experience of a rise
in glucose and hence insulin.

01:19:02.240 --> 01:19:04.820
Now the counter argument to this would be,

01:19:04.820 --> 01:19:06.840
well, that's a very unusual situation,

01:19:06.840 --> 01:19:10.090
maltodextrin causes big
increases in blood glucose,

01:19:10.090 --> 01:19:12.770
so that's not really a fair experiment

01:19:12.770 --> 01:19:14.340
or it's not a natural world experiment.

01:19:14.340 --> 01:19:16.320
And I think that's a decent assessment.

01:19:16.320 --> 01:19:19.320
Although I will point out
that one of the reasons

01:19:19.320 --> 01:19:21.810
why this study is so controversial

01:19:21.810 --> 01:19:23.450
or why these data are so controversial,

01:19:23.450 --> 01:19:26.240
is that the experiment
actually had to be stopped.

01:19:26.240 --> 01:19:27.840
And particularly the
experiment in children

01:19:27.840 --> 01:19:32.310
had to be stopped because
the changes in insulin

01:19:32.310 --> 01:19:36.530
that were observed early in
the study were so detrimental,

01:19:36.530 --> 01:19:39.770
that the institutional review
board, quite appropriately,

01:19:39.770 --> 01:19:41.350
said, we can't do this to these kids.

01:19:41.350 --> 01:19:44.640
They're experiencing these
odd shifts in insulin

01:19:44.640 --> 01:19:46.760
that are not healthy for them

01:19:46.760 --> 01:19:48.810
when they're just ingesting
artificial sweeteners

01:19:48.810 --> 01:19:51.860
in the absence of these
glucose increasing foods.

01:19:51.860 --> 01:19:55.080
So once again, I do ingest
artificial sweeteners,

01:19:55.080 --> 01:19:56.310
I'm not saying that they are dangerous,

01:19:56.310 --> 01:19:58.650
I'm not saying that
they are not dangerous,

01:19:58.650 --> 01:20:00.950
I'm saying that you have
to decide for yourself.

01:20:00.950 --> 01:20:01.860
In previous episodes,

01:20:01.860 --> 01:20:03.900
I've highlighted that
artificial sweeteners

01:20:03.900 --> 01:20:07.530
have been shown in studies of animals,

01:20:07.530 --> 01:20:11.930
that when given in very high
doses, sucralose in particular,

01:20:11.930 --> 01:20:16.460
there can be fairly robust
disruption to the gut microbiome,

01:20:16.460 --> 01:20:18.560
which is vital for immune
health and brain health,

01:20:18.560 --> 01:20:20.170
et cetera, et cetera.

01:20:20.170 --> 01:20:24.070
But thus far, our knowledge
of how artificial sweeteners

01:20:24.070 --> 01:20:26.810
negatively impacts or positively
impacts, I should say,

01:20:26.810 --> 01:20:31.810
the microbiome and other
deleterious effects on the body,

01:20:32.580 --> 01:20:36.130
has mainly been explored
in animal studies.

01:20:36.130 --> 01:20:39.410
Again, the work by Dana Small
has been done in humans.

01:20:39.410 --> 01:20:42.040
There's some parallel work
by others in animal models.

01:20:42.040 --> 01:20:45.563
I bring it up today to
illustrate the following point.

01:20:47.560 --> 01:20:49.270
Normally we have a pathway

01:20:49.270 --> 01:20:51.010
that we don't have to condition at all,

01:20:51.010 --> 01:20:52.640
it's there from birth,

01:20:52.640 --> 01:20:55.140
whereby ingestion of sweet foods

01:20:55.140 --> 01:20:56.633
causes increases in dopamine.

01:20:57.660 --> 01:21:02.020
And there are parallel pathways
by which neurons in our gut

01:21:02.020 --> 01:21:03.560
and elsewhere in our body,

01:21:03.560 --> 01:21:05.853
trigger further increases in dopamine.

01:21:07.560 --> 01:21:10.270
There's no need for a conditioned response

01:21:10.270 --> 01:21:12.640
or to become Pavlovian about this, right?

01:21:12.640 --> 01:21:15.590
You're hardwired to want
to eat sweet things,

01:21:15.590 --> 01:21:18.610
by at least two and probably
three parallel pathways.

01:21:18.610 --> 01:21:21.630
Now the work from Dana
Small's lab and others

01:21:21.630 --> 01:21:24.910
that have illustrated this
conditioned flavor preference,

01:21:24.910 --> 01:21:28.010
I think beautifully show that any flavor

01:21:28.010 --> 01:21:30.690
that's associated with a glucose spike

01:21:30.690 --> 01:21:34.370
or a long sustained increase in glucose

01:21:34.370 --> 01:21:35.920
can also be conditioned.

01:21:35.920 --> 01:21:38.070
In other words, the circuits for dopamine

01:21:38.070 --> 01:21:40.570
that reinforce the desire
to eat particular things

01:21:40.570 --> 01:21:42.430
is not unique to the sugar pathway.

01:21:42.430 --> 01:21:45.480
And this is one of the reasons I believe,

01:21:45.480 --> 01:21:47.870
why ingestion of sweet foods

01:21:47.870 --> 01:21:50.610
doesn't just us to want
to eat more sweet foods,

01:21:50.610 --> 01:21:52.230
I think that is absolutely clear

01:21:52.230 --> 01:21:54.333
based on animal data and on human data,

01:21:54.333 --> 01:21:55.550
I think that's robust,

01:21:55.550 --> 01:21:57.600
it's actually the stuff of textbooks now,

01:21:59.090 --> 01:22:01.090
but in addition, ingesting sweet foods

01:22:01.090 --> 01:22:03.980
and/or foods that raise blood glucose,

01:22:03.980 --> 01:22:05.810
but that we don't perceive as sweet.

01:22:05.810 --> 01:22:08.320
So for instance, foods with hidden sugars,

01:22:08.320 --> 01:22:11.270
sugars that have been masked
asked by salty or spicy taste,

01:22:12.170 --> 01:22:15.037
increases our desire for
glucose elevating foods

01:22:15.037 --> 01:22:16.950
and food, generally,

01:22:16.950 --> 01:22:19.610
I think that's the only
logical interpretation

01:22:19.610 --> 01:22:21.630
of the data that I can arrive at.

01:22:21.630 --> 01:22:23.840
So for people that struggle
with regulating their appetite

01:22:23.840 --> 01:22:26.250
or with regulating their sugar appetite,

01:22:26.250 --> 01:22:29.150
I think the understanding of
conditioned flavor preference,

01:22:29.150 --> 01:22:31.210
while a little bit complicated,

01:22:31.210 --> 01:22:34.060
ought to be useful in
trying to navigate reducing

01:22:34.060 --> 01:22:35.820
sugar cravings and sugar intake.

01:22:35.820 --> 01:22:38.410
As a segue into tools
to control sugar intake,

01:22:38.410 --> 01:22:42.570
as a means to both regulate
sugar intake itself,

01:22:42.570 --> 01:22:44.410
as well as food intake overall

01:22:44.410 --> 01:22:46.413
and steer us towards healthier choices.

01:22:47.260 --> 01:22:50.450
I'd like to talk about some
of the special populations

01:22:50.450 --> 01:22:52.900
out there that might want
to be especially wary

01:22:52.900 --> 01:22:57.730
of having a dysregulated
sugar appetite system.

01:22:57.730 --> 01:23:00.060
And the group I'm
referring to specifically

01:23:00.060 --> 01:23:03.230
are those with attention
deficit hyperactivity disorder

01:23:03.230 --> 01:23:05.540
or, I should say, and/or,

01:23:05.540 --> 01:23:08.990
people who have issues with
focus and attention, generally.

01:23:08.990 --> 01:23:10.300
And I think more and more nowadays,

01:23:10.300 --> 01:23:13.070
I'm hearing that people are
having a hard time focusing.

01:23:13.070 --> 01:23:16.010
This probably has something
to do with our interactions

01:23:16.010 --> 01:23:17.270
with electronic devices.

01:23:17.270 --> 01:23:19.640
As I always say, if a picture
is worth a thousand words,

01:23:19.640 --> 01:23:22.560
a movie is worth a million pictures,

01:23:22.560 --> 01:23:25.530
and the fact that we
can access so any movies

01:23:25.530 --> 01:23:27.160
just by scrolling with our thumb,

01:23:27.160 --> 01:23:28.260
is something that the nervous system

01:23:28.260 --> 01:23:30.820
has just never contended
with before in human history.

01:23:30.820 --> 01:23:32.350
I'm confident in that.

01:23:32.350 --> 01:23:34.290
And it's not that it can't deal with it,

01:23:34.290 --> 01:23:36.360
the question is what's the trade off?

01:23:36.360 --> 01:23:38.660
What are the consequences of that?

01:23:38.660 --> 01:23:42.660
So attention and ability
to focus is obviously key

01:23:42.660 --> 01:23:45.130
to success in school,
success in relationships,

01:23:45.130 --> 01:23:48.380
success in the workplace,
and success in life.

01:23:48.380 --> 01:23:51.350
We could probably even go so
far as to say one's ability

01:23:51.350 --> 01:23:54.940
to succeed in anything is
proportional to one's ability

01:23:54.940 --> 01:23:57.320
to focus and then deliberately defocus

01:23:57.320 --> 01:23:58.980
when the time comes to defocus, right?

01:23:58.980 --> 01:24:00.840
'Cause we all need rest
and we need to disengage

01:24:00.840 --> 01:24:01.763
and then reengage,

01:24:03.640 --> 01:24:07.920
If you look at the sum
total of the meta-analysis

01:24:07.920 --> 01:24:12.130
and the clinical data
on ADHD and nutrition,

01:24:12.130 --> 01:24:14.190
you arrive at a pretty clear answer,

01:24:14.190 --> 01:24:16.210
which is that sugar consumption,

01:24:16.210 --> 01:24:18.070
in particular, highly refined sugars,

01:24:18.070 --> 01:24:21.150
is just not good for people with ADHD

01:24:21.150 --> 01:24:22.820
or with attentional issues.

01:24:22.820 --> 01:24:25.460
Now, as I say that, I
also want you to recall

01:24:25.460 --> 01:24:27.170
the earlier study that I referred to,

01:24:27.170 --> 01:24:29.460
whereby the tuning of neurons in the brain

01:24:29.460 --> 01:24:31.020
is highly dependent on glucose.

01:24:31.020 --> 01:24:34.110
So this doesn't mean consuming no glucose

01:24:34.110 --> 01:24:35.150
is going to be a good idea.

01:24:35.150 --> 01:24:37.600
It doesn't mean that the ketogenic diet

01:24:37.600 --> 01:24:40.380
is necessarily the best diet for ADHD.

01:24:40.380 --> 01:24:42.550
Although there are some
people pursuing that

01:24:42.550 --> 01:24:45.810
and exploring that, and we'll
do yet another episode on ADHD

01:24:45.810 --> 01:24:47.920
at some point that go was a
little bit deeper into that,

01:24:47.920 --> 01:24:48.910
because there's some new data.

01:24:48.910 --> 01:24:53.910
I did do a very long and fairly
extensive episode on ADHD.

01:24:54.470 --> 01:24:57.880
You're welcome to look that
up if you like in our archive,

01:24:57.880 --> 01:25:01.397
excuse me, in our archive
at hubermanlab.com,

01:25:02.580 --> 01:25:03.540
it's all timestamped.

01:25:03.540 --> 01:25:05.750
So, you know, because the
number of people said,

01:25:05.750 --> 01:25:07.267
oh, you know, it's an episode on ADHD

01:25:07.267 --> 01:25:08.720
and you made it two and a half hours long.

01:25:08.720 --> 01:25:11.680
Yes we did, because we wanted
it to be as comprehensive

01:25:11.680 --> 01:25:13.070
as we could at the time.

01:25:13.070 --> 01:25:14.260
But it is timestamped,

01:25:14.260 --> 01:25:15.520
you can just jump to the
particular topics of interest

01:25:15.520 --> 01:25:18.530
and there's short little cassettes there.

01:25:18.530 --> 01:25:21.260
Now, if you'd like to
know upon what I'm basing

01:25:21.260 --> 01:25:23.930
this statement that sugar consumption

01:25:23.930 --> 01:25:25.830
and highly refined sugar consumption

01:25:25.830 --> 01:25:28.140
is potentially bad for ADHD,

01:25:28.140 --> 01:25:31.560
I'm basing this mainly on the conclusions

01:25:31.560 --> 01:25:33.040
of a really nice paper,

01:25:33.040 --> 01:25:34.477
the title of the paper is

01:25:34.477 --> 01:25:37.320
"Sugar Consumption, Sugar
Sweetened Beverages,

01:25:37.320 --> 01:25:39.360
and Attention Deficit
Hyperactivity Disorder:

01:25:39.360 --> 01:25:41.270
A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis."

01:25:41.270 --> 01:25:42.270
And this is a paper,

01:25:43.500 --> 01:25:45.640
we'll put a link to this in the caption.

01:25:45.640 --> 01:25:48.770
This was published in
Complementary Therapies in Medicine

01:25:48.770 --> 01:25:51.040
which is a bit of an atypical
journal in some sense.

01:25:51.040 --> 01:25:53.430
But I really like this meta-analysis.

01:25:53.430 --> 01:25:58.340
The sum total of this
meta-analysis is really that when,

01:25:59.320 --> 01:26:04.260
especially kids, get beyond
four sugary drinks per week,

01:26:04.260 --> 01:26:07.150
so four sodas of a typical 12 ounce soda,

01:26:07.150 --> 01:26:10.150
when they get past four
12 ounce sugary sodas,

01:26:10.150 --> 01:26:11.550
they didn't, as far as I know,

01:26:11.550 --> 01:26:14.200
look at artificially sweetened sodas.

01:26:14.200 --> 01:26:17.520
That's when you start
to see a shift towards

01:26:17.520 --> 01:26:21.980
more negative outcomes, more
symptoms of existing ADHD.

01:26:21.980 --> 01:26:24.080
Now where the controversy comes in

01:26:24.080 --> 01:26:26.220
is whether or not sugar consumption

01:26:26.220 --> 01:26:28.857
can actually trigger or cause ADHD.

01:26:28.857 --> 01:26:32.190
And I don't think we can conclude
that at all at this time.

01:26:32.190 --> 01:26:35.300
And this review, if you
decide to check it out,

01:26:35.300 --> 01:26:37.300
review/meta-analysis, I should say,

01:26:37.300 --> 01:26:38.460
if you decide to check it out,

01:26:38.460 --> 01:26:40.860
goes into some of the nuance around that.

01:26:40.860 --> 01:26:44.977
What's also interesting in
terms of ADHD and sugar intake,

01:26:44.977 --> 01:26:47.160
and it probably has
relevance to all of us,

01:26:47.160 --> 01:26:49.660
is that they also cover
some of the interesting data

01:26:49.660 --> 01:26:54.000
showing that supplementation
with omega-3 fatty acids

01:26:54.000 --> 01:26:57.130
can actually beneficial for people,

01:26:57.130 --> 01:26:59.360
in particular kids with ADHD,

01:26:59.360 --> 01:27:02.450
I've talked before about
the utility of omega-3s,

01:27:02.450 --> 01:27:03.850
that's been shown in various studies

01:27:03.850 --> 01:27:06.480
that have compared
omega-3 fatty acid intake

01:27:06.480 --> 01:27:09.890
to say prescription
antidepressants, like SSRIs,

01:27:09.890 --> 01:27:12.790
like Prozac, Zoloft,
and similar, Fluoxetine,

01:27:12.790 --> 01:27:15.160
and the results of those
studies are pretty clear,

01:27:15.160 --> 01:27:16.070
at least to me,

01:27:16.070 --> 01:27:20.630
that provided that one gets
at least one gram of EPA,

01:27:20.630 --> 01:27:21.670
essential fatty acids,

01:27:21.670 --> 01:27:23.410
so it's not just getting one gram per day,

01:27:23.410 --> 01:27:27.220
but one or more grams
per day of the EPA form

01:27:27.220 --> 01:27:28.560
of essential fatty acid,

01:27:28.560 --> 01:27:30.710
can rival some prescription
antidepressants

01:27:31.770 --> 01:27:35.230
and/or decrease the
amount of antidepressants

01:27:35.230 --> 01:27:36.210
that's required to take.

01:27:36.210 --> 01:27:38.960
Which for a lot of people
can be a very useful thing

01:27:38.960 --> 01:27:40.440
because of the side effect profile

01:27:40.440 --> 01:27:41.580
of many of those antidepressants.

01:27:41.580 --> 01:27:43.560
Of course, talk to your
psychiatrist, talk to your doctor,

01:27:43.560 --> 01:27:46.290
but the omega-3s are no doubt powerful.

01:27:46.290 --> 01:27:47.520
And then of course there's a whole story

01:27:47.520 --> 01:27:49.520
about omega-3s and heart health.

01:27:49.520 --> 01:27:51.140
And of course, like anything else,

01:27:51.140 --> 01:27:52.800
there's some controversy around omega-3s,

01:27:52.800 --> 01:27:54.620
but I think the data
are clear enough to me

01:27:54.620 --> 01:27:56.060
around mood and cardiovascular health,

01:27:56.060 --> 01:27:57.497
that certainly I personally ingest them.

01:27:57.497 --> 01:27:59.830
But as always, I always say,

01:27:59.830 --> 01:28:02.020
anytime you're going to
add or remove anything

01:28:02.020 --> 01:28:04.030
from your nutrition,
supplementation, exercise,

01:28:04.030 --> 01:28:06.330
or otherwise your lifestyle,

01:28:06.330 --> 01:28:08.290
definitely consult with a
board certified physician.

01:28:08.290 --> 01:28:09.560
I don't just say that to protect me,

01:28:09.560 --> 01:28:11.360
I say that to protect you,

01:28:11.360 --> 01:28:13.920
you are responsible for
your health ultimately.

01:28:13.920 --> 01:28:17.100
So omega-3 supplementation
has been shown to be

01:28:17.100 --> 01:28:18.390
beneficial for ADHD.

01:28:18.390 --> 01:28:20.040
Why am I talking about this in the context

01:28:20.040 --> 01:28:22.340
of an episode on sugar
and the nervous system?

01:28:22.340 --> 01:28:24.990
Well, if you remember
those neuropod cells,

01:28:24.990 --> 01:28:27.160
those cells in your gut
that respond to sugar

01:28:27.160 --> 01:28:28.640
and send signals up to the brain

01:28:28.640 --> 01:28:30.410
to cause the release of dopamine.

01:28:30.410 --> 01:28:34.580
Well, it turns out that
neuropod cells also respond to

01:28:34.580 --> 01:28:37.420
amino acids and to fatty acids,

01:28:37.420 --> 01:28:40.290
in particular, essential fatty acids.

01:28:40.290 --> 01:28:43.240
So these cells that we
call neuropod cells,

01:28:43.240 --> 01:28:45.300
have three jobs.

01:28:45.300 --> 01:28:47.980
One is to levels of sugar in the gut,

01:28:47.980 --> 01:28:51.090
the other is to detect
levels of amino acids,

01:28:51.090 --> 01:28:54.760
and to detect levels of
particular essential fatty acids,

01:28:54.760 --> 01:28:58.380
and communicate that
information to the brain.

01:28:58.380 --> 01:28:59.990
And I should point out
when I say communicate

01:28:59.990 --> 01:29:01.340
that information, they're
not actually saying,

01:29:01.340 --> 01:29:02.790
hey, there's amino acids here,

01:29:02.790 --> 01:29:04.540
or hey, there's sugar here,

01:29:04.540 --> 01:29:06.130
or, hey, there's essential fatty acids,

01:29:06.130 --> 01:29:09.190
because the language of these
cells is somewhat generic.

01:29:09.190 --> 01:29:11.740
It's just the firing of
electrical potentials.

01:29:11.740 --> 01:29:12.800
But that's the key point,

01:29:12.800 --> 01:29:16.600
it's generic and all three
or any of those three,

01:29:16.600 --> 01:29:19.510
sugar, essential fatty
acids, or amino acids,

01:29:19.510 --> 01:29:21.960
will trigger these neurons to signal

01:29:21.960 --> 01:29:23.260
to the brain to increase dopamine.

01:29:23.260 --> 01:29:24.970
And it is not coincidental

01:29:24.970 --> 01:29:28.530
that omega-3 fatty acid
supplementation can help ameliorate

01:29:28.530 --> 01:29:30.450
some of the symptoms of ADHD

01:29:31.760 --> 01:29:34.980
by way of presumably increasing dopamine

01:29:34.980 --> 01:29:37.190
in this neuropod to dopamine pathway

01:29:37.190 --> 01:29:39.210
that we talked about earlier.

01:29:39.210 --> 01:29:41.950
The whole thing has a very
nice logical structure to it.

01:29:41.950 --> 01:29:43.890
And points to yet again,

01:29:43.890 --> 01:29:47.630
the immense value of
bringing the proper amounts,

01:29:47.630 --> 01:29:49.570
maybe even supplementing
the proper amounts

01:29:49.570 --> 01:29:52.930
of omega-3 fatty acids and the
proper amounts of amino acids

01:29:52.930 --> 01:29:55.960
into the gut as a way to supplant

01:29:55.960 --> 01:29:58.040
some of the stimulation of these pathways

01:29:58.040 --> 01:30:00.043
that would otherwise be caused by sugar.

01:30:01.470 --> 01:30:04.150
There's actually a version
of this where one could say,

01:30:04.150 --> 01:30:06.820
if you want to reduce sugar cravings,

01:30:06.820 --> 01:30:10.610
you might consider
increasing certain forms

01:30:10.610 --> 01:30:13.830
of amino acid intake or certain
forms of fatty acid intake.

01:30:13.830 --> 01:30:17.230
So what are some ways that we
can reduce our sugar cravings,

01:30:17.230 --> 01:30:20.050
and ideally ways that we can do that,

01:30:20.050 --> 01:30:22.600
that also benefit us in other ways,

01:30:22.600 --> 01:30:25.420
both nutritionally and from
the neuroscience standpoint?

01:30:25.420 --> 01:30:29.280
Well, these neuropod cells that respond to

01:30:29.280 --> 01:30:33.290
and signal the brain when we
ingest sugar, as I mentioned,

01:30:33.290 --> 01:30:36.260
also respond to amino acids
and essential fatty acids.

01:30:36.260 --> 01:30:38.780
We already talked about
the essential fatty acids.

01:30:38.780 --> 01:30:40.800
I make it a particular
point to ingest anywhere

01:30:40.800 --> 01:30:43.990
from one to three grams, that's grams,

01:30:43.990 --> 01:30:47.220
of EPA essential fatty acid per day.

01:30:47.220 --> 01:30:48.790
I make it a point to do
that with the ingestion

01:30:48.790 --> 01:30:51.090
of high quality omega-3s.

01:30:51.090 --> 01:30:53.150
For me, the simplest way to do that,

01:30:53.150 --> 01:30:56.460
and I think for most people,
the lowest cost way to do that,

01:30:56.460 --> 01:30:58.370
is to use some liquid form of fish oil

01:30:58.370 --> 01:31:00.120
or some capsule form of fish oil.

01:31:00.120 --> 01:31:03.120
You really do have to see
how much essential fatty acid

01:31:03.120 --> 01:31:05.300
in the form of EPA is in those.

01:31:05.300 --> 01:31:08.020
If you try and do that
only through capsules,

01:31:08.020 --> 01:31:09.140
it can get kind of expensive,

01:31:09.140 --> 01:31:12.600
depending on which
particular brands you use,

01:31:12.600 --> 01:31:14.320
some have more EPA, some less.

01:31:14.320 --> 01:31:16.160
You can, of course also
do this through foods,

01:31:16.160 --> 01:31:17.760
you can do this with non fish sources,

01:31:17.760 --> 01:31:20.230
through things like algae,
and there's some other forms,

01:31:20.230 --> 01:31:21.860
you can look it up online now,

01:31:21.860 --> 01:31:24.180
plant-based sources of EPAs,

01:31:24.180 --> 01:31:26.830
you can do this with
high quality fish oil.

01:31:26.830 --> 01:31:31.210
In any case, that does
seem at least based on

01:31:31.210 --> 01:31:35.430
a modest amount, but
nonetheless solid literature,

01:31:35.430 --> 01:31:38.440
to reduce sugar cravings somewhat,

01:31:38.440 --> 01:31:42.060
but that could easily be by
mere replacement of calories.

01:31:42.060 --> 01:31:44.670
So we don't know yet
based on human studies,

01:31:44.670 --> 01:31:46.470
whether or not the ingestion of those EPAs

01:31:46.470 --> 01:31:48.860
is specifically activating
the neuropod cells,

01:31:48.860 --> 01:31:51.660
which specifically
activates dopamine release

01:31:51.660 --> 01:31:55.190
and bypasses the need for,
or the craving for sugar.

01:31:55.190 --> 01:31:56.210
We don't know that yet.

01:31:56.210 --> 01:32:00.620
But logically, it holds up to assume that.

01:32:00.620 --> 01:32:02.250
The fact that these neuropod cells

01:32:02.250 --> 01:32:04.690
and I should say other
neurons within the gut

01:32:04.690 --> 01:32:07.950
respond very robustly to the presence

01:32:07.950 --> 01:32:12.950
of particular amino acids
is also a potential lever

01:32:13.040 --> 01:32:14.780
by which one could reduce sugar cravings.

01:32:14.780 --> 01:32:16.770
And there's an interesting literature

01:32:16.770 --> 01:32:18.630
around the amino acid, glutamine,

01:32:18.630 --> 01:32:21.480
in particular supplementing
with the amino acid, glutamine,

01:32:21.480 --> 01:32:23.840
as it relates to sugar cravings,

01:32:23.840 --> 01:32:27.730
and certainly as it relates
to other aspects of the gut,

01:32:27.730 --> 01:32:29.600
in particular leaky gut.

01:32:29.600 --> 01:32:32.700
The use of supplemental glutamine
to try and treat leaky gut

01:32:32.700 --> 01:32:34.330
is not a new phenomenon.

01:32:34.330 --> 01:32:36.300
There are other approaches too, of course.

01:32:36.300 --> 01:32:39.350
But there are many people
who are experimenting

01:32:39.350 --> 01:32:43.220
with supplementing with
glutamine, several grams per day,

01:32:43.220 --> 01:32:46.560
often even, you know, five
grams distributed through

01:32:46.560 --> 01:32:49.410
three or four different
servings throughout the day

01:32:49.410 --> 01:32:51.610
as a way to blunt their sugar cravings.

01:32:51.610 --> 01:32:55.940
Now there has not yet been
a large scale clinical trial

01:32:55.940 --> 01:32:57.810
using glutamine to reduce sugar cravings,

01:32:57.810 --> 01:33:01.350
but the results of the few
studies that I looked at,

01:33:01.350 --> 01:33:03.660
as well as my understanding of the logic

01:33:03.660 --> 01:33:06.470
of these neuro circuits,
including the neuropod cells,

01:33:06.470 --> 01:33:10.100
brings us to a conclusion
that it makes sense

01:33:10.100 --> 01:33:13.280
why if there's a population
of neurons within our gut,

01:33:13.280 --> 01:33:14.870
that responds very robustly

01:33:14.870 --> 01:33:18.530
to the presence of sugar,
fatty acids, or amino acids,

01:33:18.530 --> 01:33:21.200
that the intake of particular amino acids

01:33:21.200 --> 01:33:23.730
would allow the dopamine pathways

01:33:23.730 --> 01:33:26.350
that might otherwise be triggered by sugar

01:33:26.350 --> 01:33:28.620
to be triggered by
something like glutamine,

01:33:28.620 --> 01:33:31.190
which has very few or no calories.

01:33:31.190 --> 01:33:34.210
And in fact, having talked
about this previously,

01:33:34.210 --> 01:33:37.690
a number of people that I
know went out and tried this,

01:33:37.690 --> 01:33:40.714
now this is of course is
what I call anec data, right?

01:33:40.714 --> 01:33:43.580
This is not a quality peer reviewed study,

01:33:43.580 --> 01:33:45.740
this is anec data.

01:33:45.740 --> 01:33:47.560
Many of them have reported back

01:33:47.560 --> 01:33:49.870
that they actually feel
as if their sugar cravings

01:33:49.870 --> 01:33:51.000
are reduced.

01:33:51.000 --> 01:33:52.847
I know some people who
actually take glutamine

01:33:52.847 --> 01:33:54.510
and mix it with full fat cream

01:33:54.510 --> 01:33:56.070
and take it kind of like
a shot of full fat cream,

01:33:56.070 --> 01:33:57.720
which sounds absolutely
delicious by the way,

01:33:57.720 --> 01:33:58.850
glutamine is a little bit chalky,

01:33:58.850 --> 01:34:02.010
so it's not that great
tasting to ingest with sugar.

01:34:02.010 --> 01:34:03.990
I should mention, if you do
try and take this approach

01:34:03.990 --> 01:34:06.820
of ingesting glutamine
to reduce sugar cravings,

01:34:06.820 --> 01:34:08.410
you want to increase
the amount of glutamine

01:34:08.410 --> 01:34:09.930
that you take somewhat gradually.

01:34:09.930 --> 01:34:12.060
It can create some gastric distress

01:34:12.060 --> 01:34:12.893
if you just, you know,

01:34:12.893 --> 01:34:14.510
I certainly wouldn't take
a big table spoon of it,

01:34:14.510 --> 01:34:16.550
throw it in water and chug
it down three times a day.

01:34:16.550 --> 01:34:17.960
Some of you with very hardy stomachs

01:34:17.960 --> 01:34:19.007
can probably tolerate that.

01:34:19.007 --> 01:34:22.700
You know, if you're like my bulldog,

01:34:22.700 --> 01:34:24.470
which unfortunately
passed away, but Costello,

01:34:24.470 --> 01:34:26.500
I always imagine that if ever
we did an autopsy on him,

01:34:26.500 --> 01:34:27.680
he'd have like a license plate

01:34:27.680 --> 01:34:29.490
and like a human being in his gut,

01:34:29.490 --> 01:34:31.070
'cause it seemed like
he could ingest anything

01:34:31.070 --> 01:34:31.903
with no issues,

01:34:31.903 --> 01:34:34.520
but of course, many people
have kind of sensitive guts.

01:34:34.520 --> 01:34:37.350
So if you're going to try taking glutamine

01:34:37.350 --> 01:34:39.020
as a means to reduce sugar cravings,

01:34:39.020 --> 01:34:41.300
just know that the
studies are still ongoing.

01:34:41.300 --> 01:34:43.490
Some people have achieved benefit.

01:34:43.490 --> 01:34:45.780
Please also realize that
there's an entire literature

01:34:45.780 --> 01:34:49.466
devoted to the potential
hazards of increasing glutamine

01:34:49.466 --> 01:34:52.450
if you have a preexisting cancer.

01:34:52.450 --> 01:34:54.640
So if you have cancer
or you're cancer prone,

01:34:54.640 --> 01:34:56.670
I would really discourage
you from this approach.

01:34:56.670 --> 01:34:59.840
And in any case, as always,
talk to your doctor.

01:34:59.840 --> 01:35:01.440
The logic nonetheless is there,

01:35:01.440 --> 01:35:05.820
why increasing amino acid
intake or fatty acid intake

01:35:05.820 --> 01:35:08.730
might decrease sugar craving.

01:35:08.730 --> 01:35:11.740
Now there are other ways
to reduce sugar craving

01:35:11.740 --> 01:35:14.980
and there are certainly
ways to reduce the sharp

01:35:14.980 --> 01:35:17.420
rise in blood glucose that can occur

01:35:17.420 --> 01:35:19.630
when we ingest sugary sweet foods

01:35:19.630 --> 01:35:22.500
or even just an abundance
of carbohydrate foods,

01:35:22.500 --> 01:35:24.590
and there are a huge
number of these things.

01:35:24.590 --> 01:35:27.080
I'm going to sort of
layer up through the ones

01:35:27.080 --> 01:35:29.040
that you might find in your
cupboard, at the grocery store,

01:35:29.040 --> 01:35:31.750
and then get into some
of the more extravagant

01:35:31.750 --> 01:35:34.120
or I should say, esoteric ones,

01:35:34.120 --> 01:35:36.600
many of which however can be quite potent.

01:35:36.600 --> 01:35:40.990
The first of which is
simple lemon juice, right,

01:35:40.990 --> 01:35:42.400
or lime juice.

01:35:42.400 --> 01:35:45.540
There was an old lore
and actually some papers,

01:35:45.540 --> 01:35:48.890
pointing to the idea that
the ingestion of vinegar,

01:35:48.890 --> 01:35:51.930
either white vinegar or wine vinegar,

01:35:51.930 --> 01:35:54.660
could somehow blunt blood glucose

01:35:54.660 --> 01:35:56.300
after the ingestion of sugary foods

01:35:56.300 --> 01:35:58.440
or the ingestion of a lot
of carbohydrate foods,

01:35:58.440 --> 01:36:00.290
or even just a big meal.

01:36:00.290 --> 01:36:03.010
Actually, Tim Ferris, I should
say the great Tim Ferris,

01:36:03.010 --> 01:36:04.570
'cause I do have great
respect for the fact

01:36:04.570 --> 01:36:08.070
that he seems to be about 10
years ahead of everything,

01:36:08.070 --> 01:36:10.170
both in terms of nutrition
and skill learning

01:36:10.170 --> 01:36:11.003
and things of that sort,

01:36:11.003 --> 01:36:13.457
many of the things that
he predicted in his books,

01:36:13.457 --> 01:36:15.750
"The Four Hour Body"
and "The Four Hour Chef"

01:36:15.750 --> 01:36:19.310
actually turned out to be
true based on scientific data,

01:36:19.310 --> 01:36:21.250
some of which only exists
in the animal models,

01:36:21.250 --> 01:36:26.250
but now also some predictions
that played out to be true

01:36:26.510 --> 01:36:28.580
in both the animal models
and and the human models.

01:36:28.580 --> 01:36:33.550
So I think that Tim
certainly deserves a hat tip

01:36:33.550 --> 01:36:36.036
for the fact that he
experimented with these methods

01:36:36.036 --> 01:36:39.320
and reported his experiences
with those methods

01:36:39.320 --> 01:36:41.730
and that now some of them, many of them,

01:36:41.730 --> 01:36:43.850
have been validated by
what I perceive to be

01:36:43.850 --> 01:36:45.250
quality science.

01:36:45.250 --> 01:36:47.060
He talked about the fact that,

01:36:47.060 --> 01:36:49.660
at least in his experiments on himself,

01:36:49.660 --> 01:36:53.470
the ingestion of vinegar
did not seem to blunt

01:36:53.470 --> 01:36:56.130
blood glucose, and he was using
continuous glucose monitors.

01:36:56.130 --> 01:36:58.530
These incidentally, another
example of where Ferris

01:36:58.530 --> 01:37:01.080
was early to the game and the rest of us

01:37:01.080 --> 01:37:02.700
are kind of just in his wake.

01:37:02.700 --> 01:37:05.780
He talked about the use
of blood glucose monitors,

01:37:05.780 --> 01:37:07.940
those, I think at the time
were implanted below the skin.

01:37:07.940 --> 01:37:11.090
Nowadays there are less
invasive blood glucose monitors,

01:37:11.090 --> 01:37:12.310
things like Levels and so forth,

01:37:12.310 --> 01:37:14.100
and these are actually
becoming pretty popular.

01:37:14.100 --> 01:37:15.250
I've worn one of these before.

01:37:15.250 --> 01:37:17.220
They're actually pretty informative.

01:37:17.220 --> 01:37:19.940
I learned for instance,
that when I go in the sauna

01:37:19.940 --> 01:37:23.370
that I experience a sharp
rise in blood glucose.

01:37:23.370 --> 01:37:27.230
And that makes sense because
of the dehydration associated

01:37:27.230 --> 01:37:28.660
with being in the sauna,
a lot of sweating,

01:37:28.660 --> 01:37:31.410
the concentration of sugar
in the bloodstream goes up.

01:37:31.410 --> 01:37:33.120
So there's some other
things that certain foods

01:37:33.120 --> 01:37:34.580
affect my blood glucose
one way or another.

01:37:34.580 --> 01:37:36.970
It's kind of an interesting
and fun experiment

01:37:36.970 --> 01:37:39.880
that some of you might be
interested in doing as well.

01:37:39.880 --> 01:37:43.690
Regardless, there are now
data pointing to the fact

01:37:43.690 --> 01:37:46.370
that lemon juice and lime juice,

01:37:46.370 --> 01:37:48.500
a couple tablespoons or so,

01:37:48.500 --> 01:37:51.390
if ingested before, or
even during, or even after,

01:37:51.390 --> 01:37:53.340
consumption of sugary foods,

01:37:53.340 --> 01:37:57.130
or I should say foods that
sharply increase blood glucose

01:37:57.130 --> 01:37:58.660
or large carbohydrate meals,

01:37:58.660 --> 01:38:00.890
can actually blunt the
blood glucose response.

01:38:00.890 --> 01:38:04.290
And I did see that when I did
my own experiments on myself

01:38:04.290 --> 01:38:06.590
with a continuous glucose monitor,

01:38:06.590 --> 01:38:09.660
it was kind of fun to
do those experiments.

01:38:09.660 --> 01:38:11.050
I preferred to do those experiments

01:38:11.050 --> 01:38:12.720
by eating somewhat larger meals

01:38:12.720 --> 01:38:14.800
of things that didn't
contain a lot of sugar.

01:38:14.800 --> 01:38:16.620
I saw some big increases in blood glucose

01:38:16.620 --> 01:38:17.500
in certain instances,

01:38:17.500 --> 01:38:20.260
and then I would ingest some
lemon juice or lime juice,

01:38:20.260 --> 01:38:21.730
typically mixed in with water.

01:38:21.730 --> 01:38:23.420
And sure enough, you could see a blunting

01:38:23.420 --> 01:38:24.670
of the blood glucose response.

01:38:24.670 --> 01:38:27.830
And of course this was
real time, continuous,

01:38:27.830 --> 01:38:30.970
hence continuous, blood
glucose monitoring.

01:38:30.970 --> 01:38:33.000
So for those of you that are interested

01:38:33.000 --> 01:38:37.270
in blunting your blood sugar
response, certain foods,

01:38:37.270 --> 01:38:39.330
that's a simple, low cost way to do that,

01:38:39.330 --> 01:38:40.180
if you want to explore it.

01:38:40.180 --> 01:38:43.020
I will say, if you are fasting

01:38:43.020 --> 01:38:45.180
and you already have low blood glucose,

01:38:45.180 --> 01:38:47.770
and you ingest lemon juice,

01:38:47.770 --> 01:38:49.530
or I should say some lemon juice and water

01:38:49.530 --> 01:38:51.300
or lime juice and water,

01:38:51.300 --> 01:38:54.720
be careful because you can
actually become hypoglycemic.

01:38:54.720 --> 01:38:57.820
For the very same reasons
that that lemon juice

01:38:57.820 --> 01:38:59.670
and lime juice can blunt blood glucose

01:38:59.670 --> 01:39:02.510
when your blood glucose
levels are moderate to high,

01:39:02.510 --> 01:39:05.010
you can also reduce blood
glucose levels even further

01:39:05.010 --> 01:39:06.723
when blood glucose levels are low.

01:39:07.770 --> 01:39:10.160
Now that's lemon juice
and lime juice there.

01:39:10.160 --> 01:39:13.150
But we can't say that has
to do with acidic things,

01:39:13.150 --> 01:39:14.210
generally.

01:39:14.210 --> 01:39:16.120
And just as a side point,

01:39:16.120 --> 01:39:17.960
many of you have probably
seen in the store,

01:39:17.960 --> 01:39:21.420
so called adjusted pH
waters or foods and drinks

01:39:21.420 --> 01:39:23.010
that are supposed to adjust your pH.

01:39:23.010 --> 01:39:23.920
Hate to break it to you,

01:39:23.920 --> 01:39:26.470
but your pH is very tightly regulated

01:39:26.470 --> 01:39:27.950
throughout your brain and body.

01:39:27.950 --> 01:39:29.700
You do not want this to change.

01:39:29.700 --> 01:39:33.800
It is entirely impossible,
at least in any safe way,

01:39:33.800 --> 01:39:35.990
that you would become
quote unquote more alkaline

01:39:35.990 --> 01:39:38.400
by ingesting an alkaline
water or something like that.

01:39:38.400 --> 01:39:42.170
It is true that the pH,
your alkalinity and acidity

01:39:42.170 --> 01:39:43.900
varies in different
compartments in your body,

01:39:43.900 --> 01:39:45.270
that's important.

01:39:45.270 --> 01:39:48.220
Unless you are hemorrhaging or vomiting,

01:39:48.220 --> 01:39:50.690
or there's something badly
wrong with you, health wise,

01:39:50.690 --> 01:39:52.780
and you're in a really dire circumstance,

01:39:52.780 --> 01:39:54.820
you don't want big shifts in your body pH

01:39:54.820 --> 01:39:58.370
and your body has all sorts
of ways of buffering against

01:39:58.370 --> 01:39:59.390
changes in pH.

01:39:59.390 --> 01:40:02.650
So I encourage you not to fall on the,

01:40:02.650 --> 01:40:05.110
or hop on the bandwagon of adjusting pH

01:40:05.110 --> 01:40:06.720
and becoming less acidic,
and that kind of thing.

01:40:06.720 --> 01:40:08.770
When you ingest lemon juice or lime juice,

01:40:08.770 --> 01:40:11.560
the mechanism by which
it blunts blood glucose

01:40:11.560 --> 01:40:13.270
is probably twofold.

01:40:13.270 --> 01:40:17.190
One is probably through
the post-ingestive effects

01:40:17.190 --> 01:40:18.560
of glucose in the gut,

01:40:18.560 --> 01:40:22.850
meaning the way in which
sugars are interacting

01:40:22.850 --> 01:40:26.340
with neurons and other
components of your gut circuitry

01:40:26.340 --> 01:40:30.480
to impact things like
gastric emptying time,

01:40:30.480 --> 01:40:33.907
to impact things like the
firing of those neuropod cells

01:40:33.907 --> 01:40:35.840
and their signaling to the brain.

01:40:35.840 --> 01:40:40.120
But almost certainly it
has something to do also

01:40:40.120 --> 01:40:43.290
with the perception of
sour taste on the tongue.

01:40:43.290 --> 01:40:47.350
We didn't go into this too
much today, but you, of course,

01:40:47.350 --> 01:40:49.860
don't just have sweet taste
receptors in your mouth,

01:40:49.860 --> 01:40:51.260
you also have bitter taste receptors,

01:40:51.260 --> 01:40:53.090
you have salty taste receptors,

01:40:53.090 --> 01:40:55.340
you have sour taste
receptors in your mouth

01:40:55.340 --> 01:40:58.500
and of course, that means
your tongue and palate.

01:40:58.500 --> 01:41:00.370
And those are interacting.

01:41:00.370 --> 01:41:05.103
If you ingest a substance that's
just sweet or mostly sweet,

01:41:05.980 --> 01:41:08.850
that causes a certain set of
effects on your blood glucose,

01:41:08.850 --> 01:41:10.510
but also your brain, dopamine,

01:41:10.510 --> 01:41:12.650
and the other neuro
circuits of your brain.

01:41:12.650 --> 01:41:15.770
If you also ingest something that's sour

01:41:15.770 --> 01:41:17.850
like lemon juice or lime juice,

01:41:17.850 --> 01:41:20.940
it adjusts the output
of those neural circuits

01:41:20.940 --> 01:41:21.860
in your brain.

01:41:21.860 --> 01:41:23.630
So again, where you have a situation

01:41:23.630 --> 01:41:25.290
where you have two parallel pathways,

01:41:25.290 --> 01:41:27.830
one that's post-ingestive
coming from phenomenon

01:41:27.830 --> 01:41:30.020
within our gut neurons,

01:41:30.020 --> 01:41:31.670
but also things like
gastric emptying time,

01:41:31.670 --> 01:41:35.550
the clearance and transfer of food,

01:41:35.550 --> 01:41:38.470
and the conversion of food
into particular nutrients

01:41:38.470 --> 01:41:41.390
and the circulation of
glucose in your bloodstream

01:41:41.390 --> 01:41:42.700
and how it gets into the brain.

01:41:42.700 --> 01:41:45.960
But also simply by
ingesting something sour,

01:41:45.960 --> 01:41:47.020
you are changing the way

01:41:47.020 --> 01:41:50.300
that sweet things impact your brain.

01:41:50.300 --> 01:41:52.490
And so I think it stands to reason

01:41:52.490 --> 01:41:55.190
that the lemon juice/lime juice effect

01:41:55.190 --> 01:41:57.100
is not going to be magic.

01:41:57.100 --> 01:41:58.800
It's going to have
everything to do with the way

01:41:58.800 --> 01:42:02.570
that ingesting sour foods
can adjust the taste.

01:42:02.570 --> 01:42:05.620
Excuse me, can adjust the neural response

01:42:05.620 --> 01:42:07.000
to taste of sweet foods.

01:42:07.000 --> 01:42:09.360
And in fact, we know based
on the beautiful work

01:42:09.360 --> 01:42:13.150
of Charles Zuker at
Columbia Medical School,

01:42:13.150 --> 01:42:14.880
that that's exactly what I happens.

01:42:14.880 --> 01:42:17.150
They've measured the activity of neurons

01:42:17.150 --> 01:42:18.820
at various locations in the so-called

01:42:18.820 --> 01:42:20.430
taste pathways of the brain.

01:42:20.430 --> 01:42:23.470
And they found that when particular tastes

01:42:23.470 --> 01:42:25.860
like just sweet or just
bitter or just sour

01:42:25.860 --> 01:42:28.700
evoke certain ensembles of neurons to fire

01:42:28.700 --> 01:42:30.360
in particular sequences,

01:42:30.360 --> 01:42:32.840
when sweet and sour are co-ingested,

01:42:32.840 --> 01:42:35.530
when bitter and sour are co-ingested,

01:42:35.530 --> 01:42:37.530
you get distinct ensembles,

01:42:37.530 --> 01:42:40.030
meaning distinct patterns of
activity of those neurons.

01:42:40.030 --> 01:42:43.250
And of course, distinct
patterns of downstream activity

01:42:43.250 --> 01:42:44.410
within the brain and body.

01:42:44.410 --> 01:42:47.480
So while it is still somewhat mysterious

01:42:47.480 --> 01:42:50.690
as to how exactly things like
lemon juice and lime juice

01:42:50.690 --> 01:42:53.730
can reduce our blood glucose spikes.

01:42:53.730 --> 01:42:56.510
When we ingest those with sweet foods

01:42:56.510 --> 01:42:59.740
or carbohydrate laden
foods, or with big meals,

01:42:59.740 --> 01:43:01.690
you can use this as a tool
with the understanding

01:43:01.690 --> 01:43:03.650
that there's a grounding in the biology

01:43:03.650 --> 01:43:05.140
of the way these circuits work.

01:43:05.140 --> 01:43:07.220
Now, some of you have
probably heard that cinnamon

01:43:07.220 --> 01:43:09.400
can be a useful tool for
controlling blood sugar.

01:43:09.400 --> 01:43:10.930
And indeed that's the case.

01:43:10.930 --> 01:43:13.690
It's very clear that cinnamon can adjust

01:43:13.690 --> 01:43:16.500
the rate of glucose entry into the stream,

01:43:16.500 --> 01:43:19.900
possibly by changing the
rate of gastric emptying,

01:43:19.900 --> 01:43:22.880
it might slow the rate of gastric emptying

01:43:22.880 --> 01:43:26.220
and thereby also reduce the glycemic index

01:43:26.220 --> 01:43:27.220
of particular foods.

01:43:27.220 --> 01:43:29.720
So I suppose if I were
going to eat a mango,

01:43:29.720 --> 01:43:31.620
and I hadn't just done a
bunch of hard training,

01:43:31.620 --> 01:43:33.580
I might sprinkle some cinnamon on it here.

01:43:33.580 --> 01:43:35.410
I always enjoy kind of
coming up with new ideas

01:43:35.410 --> 01:43:38.190
of ways that I can eat
foods during these podcasts.

01:43:38.190 --> 01:43:39.960
In any event, there's
some debate out there,

01:43:39.960 --> 01:43:42.080
if you look online as to
whether or not Saigon Cinnamon,

01:43:42.080 --> 01:43:46.090
Cassia cinnamon, excuse me,
or Ceylon cinnamon is best

01:43:46.090 --> 01:43:49.630
for purposes of blunting
blood glucose spikes.

01:43:49.630 --> 01:43:54.390
But I think the, at least
by my read of the data

01:43:54.390 --> 01:43:55.570
and from what I've found,

01:43:55.570 --> 01:43:57.820
it doesn't really matter
provided it's real cinnamon.

01:43:57.820 --> 01:43:59.670
And you have to actually
look and make sure

01:43:59.670 --> 01:44:00.930
that it's real cinnamon,

01:44:00.930 --> 01:44:03.380
because a lot of cinnamon
that you buy is not real.

01:44:03.380 --> 01:44:06.630
I do want to provide a cautionary
note about cinnamon however.

01:44:06.630 --> 01:44:09.270
Cinnamon contains
something called coumarin.

01:44:09.270 --> 01:44:12.270
which can be toxic at high levels.

01:44:12.270 --> 01:44:15.670
So you don't want to ingest
more than about a teaspoon,

01:44:15.670 --> 01:44:18.870
maybe a teaspoon and a
half of cinnamon per day,

01:44:18.870 --> 01:44:20.490
because you'll start
to exceed the threshold

01:44:20.490 --> 01:44:23.370
at which cinnamon could
start to be problematic.

01:44:23.370 --> 01:44:26.570
But certainly if you're
going to have a big meal

01:44:26.570 --> 01:44:29.140
or a meal that has a lot of sugar in it,

01:44:29.140 --> 01:44:31.610
or a lot of carbohydrate laden foods,

01:44:31.610 --> 01:44:34.100
and you don't want an
increase in blood glucose,

01:44:34.100 --> 01:44:36.960
you could put cinnamon in a beverage,

01:44:36.960 --> 01:44:38.250
you could put cinnamon on food

01:44:38.250 --> 01:44:41.580
in order to blunt that
blood glucose increase,

01:44:41.580 --> 01:44:42.810
reduce the glycemic index

01:44:42.810 --> 01:44:44.700
by way of reducing gastric emptying time.

01:44:44.700 --> 01:44:47.150
Again, just making sure
that you don't get out past

01:44:47.150 --> 01:44:48.897
that one and a half teaspoons per day,

01:44:48.897 --> 01:44:51.410
'cause you really don't
want to start dealing

01:44:51.410 --> 01:44:53.340
with any of the toxicity
related to coumarin.

01:44:53.340 --> 01:44:55.580
So we've talked about
lemon juice and lime juice

01:44:55.580 --> 01:44:57.200
and cinnamon, these
are kind of commonplace

01:44:57.200 --> 01:44:58.203
in many kitchens.

01:44:59.670 --> 01:45:03.070
Then of course we can venture
into the more esoteric

01:45:03.070 --> 01:45:04.870
or I would say the more advanced tools

01:45:04.870 --> 01:45:07.720
for adjusting sugar intake.

01:45:07.720 --> 01:45:11.970
And the one that comes to
mind is of course Berberine.

01:45:11.970 --> 01:45:14.600
Berberine is a derivative of tree bark

01:45:14.600 --> 01:45:17.170
and it is a very, very potent substance

01:45:17.170 --> 01:45:18.330
for reducing blood glucose.

01:45:18.330 --> 01:45:20.720
So much so that it is on par with

01:45:20.720 --> 01:45:22.490
Metformin or Glibenclamide,

01:45:22.490 --> 01:45:25.450
which are prescription
drugs specifically used

01:45:25.450 --> 01:45:27.430
to reduce blood glucose.

01:45:27.430 --> 01:45:30.620
So using Berberine is a serious step,

01:45:30.620 --> 01:45:33.000
you should absolutely talk
to your doctor about it.

01:45:33.000 --> 01:45:34.340
I know of a number of people that use it

01:45:34.340 --> 01:45:37.730
to lower blood glucose when
they eat really large meals.

01:45:37.730 --> 01:45:39.670
I know the number of
people that are using it

01:45:39.670 --> 01:45:43.590
to get to some of the
other effects of Metformin

01:45:43.590 --> 01:45:44.610
that people have discussed,

01:45:44.610 --> 01:45:48.314
things like activating or
tapping into the so-called

01:45:48.314 --> 01:45:50.330
AMPK pathway, reducing mTOR,

01:45:50.330 --> 01:45:54.050
these are people that are
aiming their activities

01:45:54.050 --> 01:45:55.990
at increasing longevity,

01:45:55.990 --> 01:45:58.060
a somewhat controversial approach, still,

01:45:58.060 --> 01:45:59.883
but I know many people are doing it.

01:46:00.720 --> 01:46:03.100
It is true that if you ingest Berberine,

01:46:03.100 --> 01:46:05.460
your blood glucose will plummet.

01:46:05.460 --> 01:46:08.100
And I point that out because
I've actually tried it before,

01:46:08.100 --> 01:46:11.700
it gave me brutal headaches
and I felt really dizzy,

01:46:11.700 --> 01:46:13.780
and I felt like I couldn't see straight.

01:46:13.780 --> 01:46:16.480
And actually I couldn't see
straight, why did it do that?

01:46:16.480 --> 01:46:18.580
Well, it made me hypoglycemic.

01:46:18.580 --> 01:46:21.850
It actually drove my blood
glucose down too far.

01:46:21.850 --> 01:46:23.180
And the reason it did that

01:46:23.180 --> 01:46:25.820
is that I took Berberine
on an empty stomach.

01:46:25.820 --> 01:46:27.570
I know some people can tolerate it.

01:46:28.430 --> 01:46:30.980
I would say, be very cautious
about ingesting Berberine

01:46:30.980 --> 01:46:31.813
on an empty stomach,

01:46:31.813 --> 01:46:34.200
or if you are in a low carbohydrate diet,

01:46:34.200 --> 01:46:35.670
unless you really know what you're doing

01:46:35.670 --> 01:46:36.990
and you have a medical professional

01:46:36.990 --> 01:46:38.690
to kind of guide you through that.

01:46:39.660 --> 01:46:43.640
If I took Berberine along
with a very large meal,

01:46:43.640 --> 01:46:45.790
that included a lot of
carbohydrates, you know,

01:46:45.790 --> 01:46:48.610
I can recall the days in
which Costello and I would eat

01:46:48.610 --> 01:46:51.110
a couple of pizzas and then
we might get ice cream,

01:46:51.110 --> 01:46:51.943
that kind of thing.

01:46:51.943 --> 01:46:55.780
Then I felt perfectly fine
on even up to 750 milligrams

01:46:55.780 --> 01:46:57.420
or a gram of Berberine.

01:46:57.420 --> 01:47:00.900
It has the kind of unique
property of making you feel

01:47:00.900 --> 01:47:04.550
not overwhelmed by the amount
of blood glucose increase

01:47:04.550 --> 01:47:06.400
that you're experiencing
from eating a big meal.

01:47:06.400 --> 01:47:08.550
I don't quite know how
else to describe it.

01:47:08.550 --> 01:47:10.950
It's almost as if you can keep
eating and eating and eating,

01:47:10.950 --> 01:47:15.620
and of course you have to
protect your gastric volume.

01:47:15.620 --> 01:47:17.450
I mean, you only have so
much space in your stomach

01:47:17.450 --> 01:47:19.120
to ingest food.

01:47:19.120 --> 01:47:21.730
I wasn't using it to gorge on
food, I just heard about it,

01:47:21.730 --> 01:47:23.130
I was interested in experimenting with it.

01:47:23.130 --> 01:47:25.760
I don't have any chronic
blood sugar issues.

01:47:25.760 --> 01:47:27.640
But again, when I took
it on an empty stomach,

01:47:27.640 --> 01:47:31.450
it made me hypoglycemic on
a low carbohydrate intake,

01:47:31.450 --> 01:47:34.520
hypoglycemic, not a good experience.

01:47:34.520 --> 01:47:37.840
And again, an experience I think to avoid.

01:47:37.840 --> 01:47:40.520
But provided there's a lot of
glucose in your bloodstream,

01:47:40.520 --> 01:47:44.050
and certainly if you are
of the experimental type

01:47:44.050 --> 01:47:45.850
or you're trying to
regulate blood glucose,

01:47:45.850 --> 01:47:47.540
Berberine might be a good option.

01:47:47.540 --> 01:47:49.560
But again, talk to your doctor.

01:47:49.560 --> 01:47:51.250
It does have some other
interesting effects

01:47:51.250 --> 01:47:53.610
in terms of lowering total cholesterol

01:47:53.610 --> 01:47:55.640
that are research supported

01:47:55.640 --> 01:47:58.480
of reducing insulin a little bit.

01:47:58.480 --> 01:48:00.470
Well, that's not surprising
if you reduce blood glucose,

01:48:00.470 --> 01:48:02.210
you're going to reduce
insulin because of course,

01:48:02.210 --> 01:48:05.500
insulin manages blood
glucose in the bloodstream.

01:48:05.500 --> 01:48:07.580
So I would place Berberine and of course,

01:48:07.580 --> 01:48:11.740
Metformin and Glibenclamide in
the kind of the heavy hitting

01:48:11.740 --> 01:48:14.950
potent tools for regulating blood glucose.

01:48:14.950 --> 01:48:17.830
Now, this is an episode,
not about sugar per se,

01:48:17.830 --> 01:48:20.680
but sugar viewed through the
lens of the nervous system.

01:48:20.680 --> 01:48:21.640
And what's interesting about

01:48:21.640 --> 01:48:25.360
Berberine, Metformin, Glibenclamide
and related substances,

01:48:25.360 --> 01:48:29.210
is that some of the
effects are of course on

01:48:29.210 --> 01:48:31.100
gastric emptying or
buffering blood glucose

01:48:31.100 --> 01:48:33.860
within the bloodstream, et cetera.

01:48:33.860 --> 01:48:37.330
But there appeared to also
be some neural effects

01:48:37.330 --> 01:48:40.880
of having chronically low glucose

01:48:40.880 --> 01:48:44.560
or blunting blood glucose
through things like Berberine.

01:48:44.560 --> 01:48:47.200
And some of those neural effects include

01:48:47.200 --> 01:48:51.030
longstanding changes in
the hormonal cascades

01:48:51.030 --> 01:48:53.390
that are the consequence
of having low blood sugar

01:48:53.390 --> 01:48:56.750
and thereby, changes
in the neural circuits

01:48:56.750 --> 01:48:58.890
that manage blood glucose overall.

01:48:58.890 --> 01:49:02.320
The simple way of saying
this is that by maintaining

01:49:02.320 --> 01:49:03.840
low to moderate blood glucose,

01:49:03.840 --> 01:49:07.570
either by not ingesting heavily
carbohydrate laden foods.

01:49:07.570 --> 01:49:10.780
So here I'm speaking to the
low carb or the ketogenic types

01:49:10.780 --> 01:49:13.590
or by blunting blood
glucose through things like

01:49:13.590 --> 01:49:15.640
Metformin or Berberine,

01:49:15.640 --> 01:49:18.470
even if ingesting carbohydrates,
maybe even some sugars,

01:49:18.470 --> 01:49:21.700
over time, it seems that
there's a adjustment,

01:49:21.700 --> 01:49:24.000
what we call a homeostatic regulation

01:49:24.000 --> 01:49:25.890
of the neural circuits
that control things like

01:49:25.890 --> 01:49:27.800
sugar craving, and indeed,

01:49:27.800 --> 01:49:31.730
some people report feeling
fewer sugar cravings over time.

01:49:31.730 --> 01:49:34.940
Now I didn't use Berberine for
a very long period of time,

01:49:34.940 --> 01:49:36.560
I've never used Metformin.

01:49:36.560 --> 01:49:40.160
I have experienced a somewhat
odd but welcome phenomenon

01:49:40.160 --> 01:49:42.930
of with each progressive year of my life,

01:49:42.930 --> 01:49:45.760
I have fewer and fewer sugar cravings.

01:49:45.760 --> 01:49:46.990
Why that is, I don't know.

01:49:46.990 --> 01:49:49.340
I suspect it might have
something to do with my sleep.

01:49:49.340 --> 01:49:51.280
And I'll talk about that in a few minutes.

01:49:51.280 --> 01:49:52.640
But if you're going to explore

01:49:52.640 --> 01:49:54.150
Berberine or Metformin or otherwise,

01:49:54.150 --> 01:49:56.080
in addition to working with a doctor,

01:49:56.080 --> 01:49:58.720
I think you should understand
why you're doing it, right.

01:49:58.720 --> 01:50:01.810
I think that many of the
effects can be quite potent.

01:50:01.810 --> 01:50:04.140
They can happen in both the immediate term

01:50:04.140 --> 01:50:05.860
in terms of regulating blood glucose,

01:50:05.860 --> 01:50:09.290
they can send you hypoglycemic
if you aren't careful,

01:50:09.290 --> 01:50:12.340
they can also cause longstanding changes

01:50:12.340 --> 01:50:13.950
to the neural circuitry that regulates

01:50:13.950 --> 01:50:15.030
blood sugar over time.

01:50:15.030 --> 01:50:16.950
Some of which might be
welcome changes, right?

01:50:16.950 --> 01:50:19.040
Reduce sugar cravings for instance.

01:50:19.040 --> 01:50:21.690
And if you are really, really serious

01:50:21.690 --> 01:50:23.550
about modulating blood glucose

01:50:23.550 --> 01:50:25.400
through things like Berberine,

01:50:25.400 --> 01:50:26.870
the typical dose range, again,

01:50:26.870 --> 01:50:30.590
is anywhere from half a
gram to 1.5 grams daily,

01:50:30.590 --> 01:50:33.220
that's the typical dosages
that have been explored.

01:50:33.220 --> 01:50:37.610
And there are some other
substances like sodium caprate

01:50:37.610 --> 01:50:40.160
which are known to augment
the effects of Berberine

01:50:40.160 --> 01:50:42.230
via the AMPK pathways.

01:50:42.230 --> 01:50:45.210
They basically can increase
the ability for Berberine

01:50:45.210 --> 01:50:47.370
to have its glucose lowering actions.

01:50:47.370 --> 01:50:49.840
But that of course is getting
into the really potent,

01:50:49.840 --> 01:50:51.630
what I would call sharp blade tools

01:50:51.630 --> 01:50:53.600
for controlling blood glucose.

01:50:53.600 --> 01:50:56.880
And listen, anytime you're
dealing with blood glucose,

01:50:56.880 --> 01:51:00.150
you are dealing with the brain's
preferred source of fuel.

01:51:00.150 --> 01:51:00.983
And anytime you're dealing

01:51:00.983 --> 01:51:03.300
with the brain's preferred source of fuel,

01:51:03.300 --> 01:51:04.810
you have to be especially cautious

01:51:04.810 --> 01:51:07.050
about depriving the
brain of what it needs.

01:51:07.050 --> 01:51:09.300
So whether or not you're
low carb, high carb,

01:51:10.160 --> 01:51:13.360
you know, keto, vegan,
carnivore, these substances,

01:51:13.360 --> 01:51:15.070
like Berberine, are very, very potent

01:51:15.070 --> 01:51:16.700
and you need to take them seriously.

01:51:16.700 --> 01:51:20.010
There is yet another tool for
controlling sugar cravings

01:51:20.010 --> 01:51:23.760
and the neural circuits
that regulate sugar craving

01:51:23.760 --> 01:51:26.330
and its downstream consequences.

01:51:26.330 --> 01:51:30.250
And this tool is what I would
call a high performance tool,

01:51:30.250 --> 01:51:33.890
but it's one that you
probably didn't suspect

01:51:33.890 --> 01:51:36.020
and that's sleep.

01:51:36.020 --> 01:51:38.700
I've done extensive episodes about sleep,

01:51:38.700 --> 01:51:42.020
and we actually have an episode
called master your sleep.

01:51:42.020 --> 01:51:44.460
You can find that episode
easily at hubermanlab.com.

01:51:44.460 --> 01:51:46.030
It's available in all the various formats,

01:51:46.030 --> 01:51:47.910
YouTube, Apple, Spotify, et cetera.

01:51:47.910 --> 01:51:49.690
and it provides a lot of tools.

01:51:49.690 --> 01:51:51.670
And on social media, I
provide a lot of tools,

01:51:51.670 --> 01:51:53.200
often we have a newsletter
that provides tools

01:51:53.200 --> 01:51:54.803
on how to maximize sleep.

01:51:56.200 --> 01:52:00.580
What is the role of sleep
in sugar metabolism,

01:52:00.580 --> 01:52:03.410
sugar, hunger, and the way that the brain

01:52:03.410 --> 01:52:04.660
regulates those things?

01:52:04.660 --> 01:52:08.250
Well, there's a really
exciting study that came out

01:52:08.250 --> 01:52:10.017
just last year.

01:52:10.017 --> 01:52:11.940
This study was published in
the journal, Cell Report,

01:52:11.940 --> 01:52:14.120
a Cell Press journal, excellent journal.

01:52:14.120 --> 01:52:16.100
And the reason I love this study so much,

01:52:16.100 --> 01:52:17.480
is it involved having people,

01:52:17.480 --> 01:52:20.560
so yes, this was done in
humans, sleep in the laboratory.

01:52:20.560 --> 01:52:22.770
That's not unusual, there's
a sleep lab at Stanford,

01:52:22.770 --> 01:52:24.420
there's sleep labs elsewhere.

01:52:24.420 --> 01:52:27.770
But what they did was
they actually measured

01:52:27.770 --> 01:52:30.270
from the breath of these people,

01:52:30.270 --> 01:52:32.303
and they extracted from their breath,

01:52:33.340 --> 01:52:35.390
the metabolites that would allow them

01:52:35.390 --> 01:52:38.280
to understand what sorts of metabolism

01:52:38.280 --> 01:52:40.050
was occurring in these people's bodies

01:52:40.050 --> 01:52:42.170
at different phases of sleep.

01:52:42.170 --> 01:52:44.200
And this is a really remarkable study.

01:52:44.200 --> 01:52:47.700
They actually did this every 10 seconds

01:52:47.700 --> 01:52:48.800
throughout the entire night.

01:52:48.800 --> 01:52:50.850
So in little tiny 10 second bits,

01:52:50.850 --> 01:52:52.480
meaning at very high resolution,

01:52:52.480 --> 01:52:54.260
they could evaluate what is the metabolism

01:52:54.260 --> 01:52:56.280
in the brain and body
that people experience

01:52:56.280 --> 01:52:58.590
as they go from REM sleep,
rapid eye movement sleep

01:52:58.590 --> 01:53:00.790
to slow wave, sleep, and so on.

01:53:00.790 --> 01:53:03.550
And I'll go deeper into the
study again in the future,

01:53:03.550 --> 01:53:05.800
because it's so interesting,
and I think so important.

01:53:05.800 --> 01:53:09.430
But what they discovered
was that each stage of sleep

01:53:09.430 --> 01:53:12.430
was associated with a very
particular signature pattern

01:53:12.430 --> 01:53:16.150
of metabolism and
particular phases of sleep

01:53:16.150 --> 01:53:18.620
are associated with sugar metabolism,

01:53:18.620 --> 01:53:20.500
or more with fat metabolism,

01:53:20.500 --> 01:53:23.210
or more with other aspects of metabolism.

01:53:23.210 --> 01:53:26.220
And the reason why I think
this study is important

01:53:26.220 --> 01:53:28.610
to discuss in the context
of today's discussion

01:53:28.610 --> 01:53:30.150
about sugar in the brain,

01:53:30.150 --> 01:53:33.840
is that many people have experienced

01:53:33.840 --> 01:53:36.960
the effects of disrupted
sleep on their appetite.

01:53:36.960 --> 01:53:39.910
And in particular, it's been
reported that when people

01:53:39.910 --> 01:53:41.790
are sleep deprived or the quality

01:53:41.790 --> 01:53:43.690
of their sleep is disrupted,

01:53:43.690 --> 01:53:46.780
that their appetite for
sugary food increases.

01:53:46.780 --> 01:53:51.780
Now that was always assumed to
be due to some metabolic need

01:53:52.300 --> 01:53:54.120
that was triggered by
the sleep deprivation

01:53:54.120 --> 01:53:55.590
or by the poor sleep.

01:53:55.590 --> 01:53:56.780
But in reading over this study,

01:53:56.780 --> 01:53:59.120
some of the more important
points made by the authors

01:53:59.120 --> 01:54:00.710
relate to the fact that well,

01:54:00.710 --> 01:54:03.560
sleep is known to have
incredibly important effects

01:54:03.560 --> 01:54:05.920
on brain and body, for
a variety of systems,

01:54:05.920 --> 01:54:08.880
immune system, neural
functioning, et cetera.

01:54:08.880 --> 01:54:12.570
This very organized sequence of particular

01:54:12.570 --> 01:54:14.890
forms of metabolism being active

01:54:14.890 --> 01:54:16.570
during particular phases of sleep,

01:54:16.570 --> 01:54:18.520
which are very, very well orchestrated,

01:54:18.520 --> 01:54:20.910
as we know, slow wave sleep, and REM sleep

01:54:20.910 --> 01:54:22.360
being orchestrated in 90 minutes,

01:54:22.360 --> 01:54:24.860
so-called ultradian cycles
and so on and so forth,

01:54:25.900 --> 01:54:29.210
is thought to of perhaps
set up the brain and body

01:54:29.210 --> 01:54:32.400
to be able to regulate
itself in the waking hours.

01:54:32.400 --> 01:54:35.120
And therefore, when
people are sleep deprived

01:54:35.120 --> 01:54:38.810
or deprived of certain forms
or states within sleep,

01:54:38.810 --> 01:54:42.060
such as rapid eye movement sleep,

01:54:42.060 --> 01:54:43.940
that it creates a disruption

01:54:43.940 --> 01:54:46.660
in a particular set of metabolic pathways.

01:54:46.660 --> 01:54:48.770
Now we don't a leap
too far from this study

01:54:48.770 --> 01:54:50.640
to sugar metabolism
and the neural circuits

01:54:50.640 --> 01:54:52.300
controlling sugar metabolism.

01:54:52.300 --> 01:54:56.030
But I will say this, if
you look at the sum total

01:54:56.030 --> 01:55:00.700
of the data on obesity
or on type two diabetes

01:55:00.700 --> 01:55:02.900
or on metabolic syndromes of any kind,

01:55:02.900 --> 01:55:06.530
you almost always see
disruptions in sleep.

01:55:06.530 --> 01:55:09.270
Now, some of those could
be you do to sleep apnea

01:55:09.270 --> 01:55:12.010
caused by even just the
size of somebody's neck

01:55:12.010 --> 01:55:13.433
or the weight of their body.

01:55:14.890 --> 01:55:17.270
In other words, we don't know
the direction of the effect,

01:55:17.270 --> 01:55:19.030
metabolic syndromes could disrupt sleep,

01:55:19.030 --> 01:55:20.770
which disrupt metabolic syndromes.

01:55:20.770 --> 01:55:23.840
And indeed the authors point
out quite appropriately,

01:55:23.840 --> 01:55:25.800
that they don't understand the direction

01:55:25.800 --> 01:55:28.160
of the effects that they observe either.

01:55:28.160 --> 01:55:32.350
But there is now a plethora
of data pointing to the fact

01:55:32.350 --> 01:55:34.520
that getting quality sleep each night

01:55:34.520 --> 01:55:37.310
helps regulate not only appetite,

01:55:37.310 --> 01:55:39.760
but also the specific forms of metabolism

01:55:39.760 --> 01:55:42.180
that drive specific appetites.

01:55:42.180 --> 01:55:44.330
So the takeaway is,

01:55:44.330 --> 01:55:49.330
while there are extravagant
and potent and interesting ways

01:55:49.890 --> 01:55:50.970
to regulate blood glucose,

01:55:50.970 --> 01:55:53.180
everything from cinnamon to
lemon juice, to Berberine,

01:55:53.180 --> 01:55:56.170
to sodium caprate, to behavioral tools,

01:55:56.170 --> 01:55:57.690
to the mere understanding of how

01:55:57.690 --> 01:56:00.000
the direct and indirect pathways

01:56:00.000 --> 01:56:02.033
go from the gut to dopamine, et cetera,

01:56:03.300 --> 01:56:06.380
if you're not establishing
the firm foundation

01:56:06.380 --> 01:56:07.783
of proper metabolism,

01:56:09.120 --> 01:56:12.500
all of those things are going
to be sort of rearranging

01:56:12.500 --> 01:56:15.920
deck chairs on the Titanic
as it's sometimes described.

01:56:15.920 --> 01:56:17.270
So we can't overstate the importance

01:56:17.270 --> 01:56:21.290
of getting regular sufficient
amount of high quality sleep,

01:56:21.290 --> 01:56:22.867
at least 80% of the time,

01:56:22.867 --> 01:56:25.860
not for sake of immune system
function, for clear thinking,

01:56:25.860 --> 01:56:30.200
et cetera, but also for properly
regulating our metabolism,

01:56:30.200 --> 01:56:32.100
including our sugar metabolism.

01:56:32.100 --> 01:56:33.580
Thank you for joining
me for this discussion

01:56:33.580 --> 01:56:34.950
about sugar and the nervous system

01:56:34.950 --> 01:56:36.740
and how they are regulating each other

01:56:36.740 --> 01:56:38.940
in both the brain and body.

